window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["amity-mumbai-2021"] = {"id": "amity-mumbai-2021", "title": "Amity Mumbai 2021 M.Phil", "fullTitle": "Amity University (Mumbai) 2021, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance questions (from a coaching video, answers worked out)", "sub": "60 questions · no key was published, answers worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The three-component needed for any experimental studies are", "options": {"a": "Manipulation, Control and Observation", "b": "Manipulation, Control and Randomization", "c": "Control, Randomization and Observation", "d": "Randomization, Manipulation and Reference Collection"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the three components of a true experiment are manipulation, control and randomisation. An experiment is the only research design that can show cause and effect, and it needs three things to do so. Manipulation means the researcher deliberately changes the independent variable (IV), for example giving one group memory training and another group none. Control means holding every other variable constant, or using a control group, so that only the IV differs between conditions; time of day, instructions and noise are kept the same. Randomisation (random assignment) means each participant has an equal chance of being placed in any condition, so pre-existing differences in ability, mood or motivation are spread evenly across the groups. Ronald Fisher (1935) made randomisation a founding principle of experimental design, and Denise Polit and Cheryl Tatano Beck (2017) list manipulation, control and randomisation as the three defining properties of a true experiment. Suppose Arjun wants to test whether music improves recall. He randomly assigns 40 students to music or silence (randomisation), plays music to only one group (manipulation) and keeps the word list, timing and room identical (control). If the music group recalls more, he can infer cause. Remove randomisation and he has only a quasi-experiment. Option b is right because these are the three features that separate a true experiment from every other design. Exam trap: observation (measuring the dependent variable) happens in every study, including surveys and correlational research, so it does not define an experiment; and reference collection is a literature review step, not a design feature. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Some education research texts, such as Donald Ary and colleagues (2010), list control, manipulation and observation as the characteristics of experimental research, which makes option a defensible; but randomisation is what gives an experiment its causal power, and it is the triad used in psychology and nursing research texts.", "topic": "Components of a true experiment"}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. Rogerian therapy makes greatest use of body languages. II. Behaviour therapy is based on the principles of classical and operant conditioning. Select the correct answer using the codes given below", "options": {"a": "Only 1", "b": "Only 2", "c": "Both 1 and 2", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: only statement II is correct. Statement I is false, because the therapy that makes the greatest use of body language is Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls (1951) working with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman. Gestalt therapists work in the here and now and treat posture, gestures, breathing and tone of voice as messages the client is not yet aware of. A therapist might ask Meera, who taps her foot while saying she is calm, to \"be your foot and speak for it\", so that a disowned feeling comes into awareness. Carl Rogers (1951), by contrast, built client-centred therapy on verbal reflection of feelings and three core conditions: unconditional positive regard, empathic understanding and congruence. The Rogerian therapist follows the client's words and is deliberately non-directive; interpreting body language would make the therapist an expert interpreter, which Rogers avoided. Statement II is true. Behaviour therapy rests on learning principles. Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) underlies systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958), flooding and aversion therapy, while operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938) underlies token economies, shaping, time-out and contingency management. Hans Eysenck (1959) defined behaviour therapy as the application of modern learning theory to abnormal behaviour. Option b is right because statement I gives Rogers the hallmark of Gestalt therapy, while statement II is the standard textbook definition of behaviour therapy. Exam trap: examiners swap the humanistic therapies. Client-centred therapy uses reflection and empathy; Gestalt therapy uses awareness of the body, the empty chair and confrontation of avoidance.", "topic": "Gestalt versus Rogerian, behaviour therapy"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) meaning of speech is understood by morphemes. Reason (R) Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning in speech perception.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true and R explains A. Language is built in layers. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that distinguish one word from another (/b/ and /p/ in \"bat\" and \"pat\"), but on their own they carry no meaning. Morphemes are the smallest units that do carry meaning. A morpheme can be a free word (\"book\") or a bound piece that cannot stand alone, such as the plural \"-s\", the past tense \"-ed\" or the prefix \"un-\". So \"unhappiness\" has three morphemes: un + happy + ness. Above morphemes come syntax (rules for combining words), semantics (meaning of words and sentences) and pragmatics (use in a social context). Leonard Bloomfield (1933) defined the morpheme as the minimal meaningful form of a language, and every introductory psychology text repeats this. When Kabir hears \"the dogs barked\", he extracts meaning from the morphemes \"dog\", \"-s\", \"bark\" and \"-ed\": plural number and past time come from the bound morphemes, not from any single sound. A is true: grasping what speech means depends on identifying its morphemes. R is true: morphemes are the smallest meaningful units. R explains A: because morphemes are where meaning first enters the stream of sounds, the listener must decode them to understand speech. Option a is right because the reason gives the very property that makes the assertion work. Exam trap: phoneme versus morpheme. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound; morphemes are the smallest units of meaning, and a reversed definition is the usual distractor. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A strict reader could call A overstated, since full meaning also needs syntax, semantics and context, and pick d; but the setter pairs the two statements as claim and reason, so a is the better pick.", "topic": "Morphemes and meaning"}, {"q": "When we consider the height of adult males, height is the variable. But the height of any individual is a:", "options": {"a": "Variate", "b": "Data", "c": "Score", "d": "Scale"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the height of any one individual is a variate. In statistics a variable is a characteristic that can take different values across people, objects or occasions: height, weight, IQ, reaction time. A variate is one particular value that the variable takes for one case. The variable is the general dimension; the variate is a single observation on it. If a researcher records the heights of adult men, \"height\" is the variable. When Rohan is measured at 172 cm, that single figure, 172 cm, is a variate, and when Dev is measured at 165 cm, that is another variate of the same variable. A set of variates together forms the data, and organising them into a frequency distribution is the first step of descriptive statistics. The same root gives the words univariate (analysing one variable at a time, as with a mean or a histogram), bivariate (two variables, as in a correlation between height and weight) and multivariate (many variables at once, as in MANOVA or factor analysis). So the vocabulary runs from the general to the particular: variable (height), variate (Rohan's 172 cm), data (all the recorded heights) and scale (the system of units used, here a ratio scale in centimetres). Option a is right because the stem contrasts the general characteristic, height, with its value in one individual, which is the definition of a variate. Exam trap: \"score\" sounds right, but a score is a value earned on a test or rating scale, such as 45 on an anxiety inventory, while height is a physical measurement; and \"data\" is the whole collection of observations, not one value.", "topic": "Variable versus variate"}, {"q": "Which is NOT the characteristic of DSM-5?", "options": {"a": "It utilizes a categorical approach", "b": "It includes a polythetic criteria set for most disorders", "c": "It provides a multi-axial assessment system", "d": "It provides guidelines for cultural formulation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: DSM-5 does not provide a multi-axial assessment system. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer (1980), introduced a five-axis system: Axis I clinical disorders, Axis II personality disorders and mental retardation, Axis III general medical conditions, Axis IV psychosocial and environmental problems, and Axis V the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF). DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) kept it. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes in favour of non-axial documentation: mental disorders, personality disorders and relevant medical conditions are listed together, psychosocial problems are recorded with Z codes, and the GAF was replaced by tools such as the WHO Disability Assessment Schedule 2.0. DSM-5-TR (2022) kept this format. The other three statements are true. DSM-5 remains mainly categorical: a person either meets criteria for a disorder or does not, though it adds dimensional severity ratings. Most disorders use polythetic criteria sets, meaning a person needs only some symptoms from a list (for example 5 of 9 for major depressive disorder), so two people with the same diagnosis can share few symptoms. DSM-5 also gives an Outline for Cultural Formulation, a 16-question Cultural Formulation Interview and a section on cultural concepts of distress. If Ananya is diagnosed today, her record reads \"major depressive disorder, moderate; hypothyroidism\", not \"Axis I, Axis III\". Option c is right because the multi-axial system belonged to DSM-III to DSM-IV-TR and was abolished in 2013. Exam trap: students remember the five axes vividly from older books and assume they still exist; and \"categorical\" is still true of DSM-5 even though it added dimensional ratings.", "topic": "DSM-5 non-axial system"}, {"q": "Match the following:- Sampling: 1. Quota, 2. Purposive, 3. Systematic, 4. Stratified, 5. Cluster. A. A process of sampling in which a number of groups are identified in a population, then some groups are randomly selected for participation in a research project. B. A process in which subgroups, usually convenience samples, are identified and a specified number of individuals from each group are included in the research. C. A non-random sampling technique in which participants are selected for a study because of some desirable characteristics, like expertise in some area. D. A process of sampling in which an apparently unbiased but non-random sample is created, such as by creating a list of every element in the population and selecting every nth member from the population. E. A process of sampling in which groups of interest are identified, then participants are selected at random from these groups.", "options": {"a": "A C D E B", "b": "B D E C A", "c": "C B D A E", "d": "B C D E A"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: Quota B, Purposive C, Systematic D, Stratified E, Cluster A. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known chance of selection; in non-probability sampling it does not. Quota sampling (B) is non-probability: the researcher identifies subgroups, such as men and women or age bands, and recruits conveniently available people until each subgroup's set number (quota) is filled. Purposive or judgemental sampling (C) is also non-probability: people are chosen because they have a desired characteristic, such as being expert counsellors. Systematic sampling (D) takes every nth name from a list after a starting point; it looks unbiased, but strictly it is not simple random sampling, which is why the definition calls it apparently unbiased but non-random. Stratified random sampling (E) divides the population into strata of interest and then selects at random within each stratum, so every subgroup is properly represented. Cluster sampling (A) selects whole groups, such as schools or villages, at random and studies those clusters; it is efficient when no list of individuals exists. Leslie Kish (1965), in Survey Sampling, set out these designs. Suppose Ishaan studies exam stress in Pune. He could stratify by school board and pick students at random within each board (stratified), or randomly pick 10 schools and test every Class 12 student in them (cluster). Option d is right because it is the only sequence that pairs each method with its definition in the order 1 to 5. Exam trap: quota and stratified sampling both start by dividing the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: quota fills each subgroup by convenience, stratified fills it at random. In cluster sampling, by contrast, the groups themselves are what get sampled.", "topic": "Sampling methods matching"}, {"q": "The bridge that connects the two lobes in the thalamus is called as", "options": {"a": "Projection fibres", "b": "Diencephalon", "c": "Massa intermedia", "d": "Medial geneculate nucleus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the massa intermedia joins the two lobes of the thalamus. The thalamus is a pair of egg-shaped masses of grey matter, one on each side of the third ventricle, in the diencephalon at the top of the brainstem. It is the main relay station of the forebrain: almost every sensory pathway except smell synapses in a thalamic nucleus before reaching the cortex. In most human brains the two thalamic lobes touch across the third ventricle at a small bridge of grey matter called the massa intermedia, also known as the interthalamic adhesion. It is missing in a minority of people, more often in men, and its function is still uncertain. Neil Carlson (2013), in Physiology of Behavior, describes the thalamus as two lobes connected by the massa intermedia, which crosses the third ventricle. When Tara feels a pinprick on her hand, the signal reaches the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus and is relayed to the somatosensory cortex; sounds she hears are relayed through the medial geniculate nucleus to the auditory cortex, and what she sees passes through the lateral geniculate nucleus to the visual cortex. Each of these nuclei sits inside one thalamic lobe, while the massa intermedia is the only bridge between the two lobes. Option c is right because the massa intermedia is the named structure that links the left and right thalamus. Exam trap: diencephalon is tempting because the thalamus belongs to it, but it is the whole region (thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus and subthalamus), not a bridge. Commissures such as the corpus callosum join the cortical hemispheres, whereas projection fibres connect the cortex with lower centres.", "topic": "Massa intermedia of thalamus"}, {"q": "When treating a client with a specific phobia, a therapist has forced the client to imagined the feared stimulus which the client is unpleasant of, is a technique called?", "options": {"a": "Implosion", "b": "Systematic desensitization", "c": "Flooding", "d": "Conditioned aversion"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: making a phobic client vividly imagine the feared stimulus at full strength is implosion, or implosive therapy. Exposure therapies reduce fear through extinction: if the feared stimulus keeps appearing and nothing bad happens, the conditioned fear weakens. They differ in pace and in whether the stimulus is real or imagined. Systematic desensitisation, by Joseph Wolpe (1958), is gradual: the client learns relaxation and climbs a hierarchy of scenes from least to most frightening. Flooding plunges the client into the most feared situation at once, usually in real life (in vivo), with escape blocked until anxiety subsides. Implosive therapy, developed by Thomas Stampfl (1967) with Donald Levis, is flooding in imagination: the therapist describes exaggerated, highly vivid scenes of the feared object, sometimes laced with psychodynamic themes, and keeps the client imagining them until the anxiety \"implodes\" and burns out. Neha, for example, fears dogs. Her therapist asks her to close her eyes and picture snarling dogs surrounding and biting her, keeping the scene going for an hour without relaxation, until her fear drops. Option a is right because the stem stresses that the client is forced to imagine the unpleasant stimulus, which is the defining feature of implosion. Exam trap: flooding and implosion both use maximum intensity and no relaxation; the key word is imagined (implosion) versus real-life exposure (flooding). Pairing an unwanted behaviour with something unpleasant is aversion therapy, a different technique altogether. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Some texts allow \"imaginal flooding\", so c is defensible, but where both terms are offered, imagined exposure at full intensity is keyed as implosion.", "topic": "Implosion versus flooding"}, {"q": "Which of the following are the four stages of Adlerian therapy, through which the patients in therapy are encouraged to acquire a more positive and productive way of life by developing new insights, skills and behaviours.", "options": {"a": "Assessment, Clarification, Interpretation, Reassessment", "b": "Engagement, Assessment, Insight, Reorientation", "c": "Clarification, Confrontation, Insight, Orientation", "d": "Assessment, Clarification, Interpretation, Orientation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Adlerian therapy moves through engagement, assessment, insight and reorientation. Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology, which sees each person as a goal-directed, socially embedded whole whose problems come from a mistaken style of life and discouraged social interest. Rudolf Dreikurs (1967) turned Adler's ideas into a structured therapy, and Gerald Corey (2017) presents it in four phases. Engagement: therapist and client form a collaborative, respectful relationship and agree on goals. Assessment: the therapist explores the client's lifestyle through family constellation, birth order, early recollections and basic mistakes. Insight: the therapist offers tentative interpretations (\"Could it be that...?\") so the client understands the purposes behind the symptoms. Reorientation: the client turns insight into action, trying new behaviours and strengthening social interest, helped by encouragement, \"acting as if\" and \"catching oneself\". Consider Siddharth, a second-born who feels he must always outdo his brother. After building rapport, his therapist gathers his early memories, then helps him see that his goal of superiority keeps him anxious, and finally coaches him to act as if he were already good enough at work. Option b is right because these are the four phases named in standard counselling texts, ending in reorientation, where the change in behaviour happens. Exam trap: the other options mix in steps from other schools. Clarification, confrontation and interpretation come from psychodynamic technique, while \"reassessment\" and \"orientation\" are invented near misses for reorientation. Remember that insight alone is not the goal for Adlerians; it must be followed by action.", "topic": "Four phases of Adlerian therapy"}, {"q": "A general name for therapy is that attempt to change the clients behaviour by altering the consequences of the behaviour is a", "options": {"a": "Time out", "b": "In vivo desensitization", "c": "Contingency management", "d": "In vitro desensitization"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: contingency management is the general name for therapies that change behaviour by altering its consequences. A contingency is an if-then link between a behaviour and what follows it. Operant conditioning, from Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) work, shows that behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent, and behaviour followed by no reward or by punishment becomes less frequent. Contingency management applies this deliberately: the therapist first records the target behaviour and what currently reinforces it, then rearranges the consequences. It is an umbrella covering positive reinforcement, shaping, token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968), response cost, extinction and time-out. It is also one of the best-supported treatments for stimulant and other substance use, where clients earn vouchers or prizes for drug-free urine samples. For example, Aarav's parents give him a star each evening he finishes homework without being reminded, and five stars buy a weekend cricket match, while tantrums no longer win attention. His behaviour changes not through talking about it but through changing what it earns. Option c is right because the stem asks for the general name for consequence-based therapies, and contingency management is exactly that category. Exam trap: time-out is one specific contingency (removing access to reinforcement after misbehaviour), so it is an example of contingency management, not the general name. In vivo desensitisation works on the stimulus that comes before the response, through classical conditioning, not on consequences.", "topic": "Contingency management"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Neurotic needs include such motives as a desire to dominate, to inflict pain or to subject oneself to the will of another person. Reason (R) Neurotic needs are non-productive and do not foster heath.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. The statements come from Abraham Maslow's (1970) holistic-dynamic theory, in the account given by Jess Feist and Gregory Feist (2009). Maslow separated several kinds of needs. Conative needs form the five-level hierarchy: physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem and self-actualisation; aesthetic and cognitive needs sit alongside them. Neurotic needs are different. They are usually reactive, compensations for basic needs that were never met, and they include motives such as a desire to dominate, to inflict pain, or to subject oneself to the will of another person. So A is true. Such needs are nonproductive: they keep an unhealthy style of life going, have no value in the striving for self-actualisation and lead to pathology whether or not they are satisfied. So R is true. But R is a further property of neurotic needs, not the reason why they include domination, cruelty and submission. A lists what neurotic needs contain; R describes their effect. The content of these needs is explained by their origin in thwarted basic needs. For example, Vikram was never made to feel safe or loved as a child and now needs to control every decision his wife makes; the control never makes him feel secure, however often he gets it, which is exactly why Maslow called such needs nonproductive. Option b is right because A and R are separate true facts. Exam trap: the list of domination, pain and submission sounds like Karen Horney's (1942) neurotic needs or Erich Fromm's (1941) sadism and masochism, but this wording is Maslow's. Key note: no key was published for this paper. One could read R as the criterion that makes these motives neurotic and so choose a; but R states what neurotic needs do, not why they take this form, so b is the safer pick.", "topic": "Maslow's neurotic needs"}, {"q": "According to Lawrence Frank, Thematic Apperception Test is classified under which category of projective technique?", "options": {"a": "Construction technique", "b": "Refractive projective technique", "c": "Interpretative technique", "d": "Constitutive technique"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in Lawrence Frank's classification the TAT is an interpretative technique. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term \"projective methods\" in a paper arguing that a person given ambiguous material will impose his or her own private world on it. He sorted the methods by what the person is asked to do with the material. Constitutive methods give formless material that the person must structure, such as Rorschach inkblots, finger painting or clay. Constructive methods give definite materials to arrange into a larger product, such as building a scene in the World Test or a design in the Mosaic Test. Interpretative methods ask the person to say what an experience or picture means to them, which is what the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) does: the person tells a story about what led up to a scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. Cathartic methods, such as play, release emotion, and refractive methods read personality through distortions of a conventional medium, such as handwriting. When Pooja tells a story about TAT card 1 (a boy looking at a violin), she interprets the picture in terms of her own needs and pressures. Option c is right because the stem asks for Frank's category, and Frank placed story-telling about pictures under interpretation. Exam trap: in Gardner Lindzey's (1959) later and more popular five-way scheme the TAT is a construction technique, so construction is the right answer only when the question names Lindzey. Frank's constructive group meant arranging materials into a product, not telling stories.", "topic": "Frank's projective classification"}, {"q": "The ______ contains the nucleus and much of the machinery that provides the life process of the cell.", "options": {"a": "Soma", "b": "Dendrite", "c": "Axon", "d": "Terminal Buttons"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the soma, or cell body, contains the nucleus and much of the machinery for the life processes of the cell. A neuron has four main parts. Dendrites are branching extensions that receive messages from other neurons. The soma is the cell body. It holds the nucleus with the cell's DNA, and its cytoplasm contains ribosomes and rough endoplasmic reticulum that make proteins, Golgi apparatus that package them and mitochondria that supply energy. The soma integrates incoming signals and keeps the whole cell alive. The axon is a single long fibre that carries the action potential away from the soma, often wrapped in myelin. Terminal buttons (axon terminals) at the ends of the axon's branches release neurotransmitter into the synapse. The stem is almost word for word Neil Carlson's (2013) description in Physiology of Behavior. The importance of the soma was shown long ago. Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1888) argued from stained tissue that each neuron is a separate cell, and Augustus Waller (1850) had already found that a nerve fibre cut off from its cell body degenerates (Wallerian degeneration), because proteins are made in the soma and shipped down the axon. Think of Rhea's neuron as a factory: the dendrites are the mailroom, the soma is the head office and production floor, the axon is the delivery road and the terminal buttons are the loading docks. Option a is right because only the soma houses the nucleus and the protein-making machinery. Exam trap: students sometimes pick the axon because it is the most famous part, but the axon only conducts; dendrites receive and terminal buttons release.", "topic": "Parts of the neuron"}, {"q": "One of the dangers of individual testing is that the examinees may get bored if the questions are too easy or become frustrated if the items are too difficult. To get around this problem, some intelligence tests are arranged so that the more difficult questions are presented only to the examinees who have gotten the easier questions correct. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "Construct validity", "b": "Adaptive testing", "c": "Norm referenced interpretation", "d": "Cross-validation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: giving harder items only to examinees who pass the easier ones is adaptive testing. In a fixed test everyone takes the same items, so a bright child wastes time on easy items and a struggling child faces a string of failures. Adaptive, or tailored, testing fits the items to the person. Individual intelligence tests have long done this by hand. Lewis Terman's (1916) Stanford-Binet started at a level suited to the child, established a basal age where all items were passed and stopped at a ceiling where all were failed; the modern Stanford-Binet and Wechsler scales keep such start points and discontinue rules. Computerised adaptive testing (CAT), built on item response theory by Frederic Lord (1980), chooses each next item from a calibrated item bank to match the current estimate of ability: a correct answer brings a harder item, a wrong answer an easier one. Adaptive tests reach the same reliability with far fewer items and keep examinees neither bored nor frustrated, which is why the GRE and many licensing exams use them. For example, when Diya, aged 9, passes all the vocabulary items at the age 8 level, the examiner moves her to age 9 and 10 items and stops when she fails several in a row, so she never sits through 40 trivial words. Option b is right because the stem describes items being chosen according to the examinee's previous answers, which is the definition of adaptive testing. Exam trap: because the stem mentions intelligence tests, students drift towards a validity term, but construct validity and cross-validation concern whether a test measures what it should, not how items are given.", "topic": "Adaptive testing"}, {"q": "A variable which can theoretically assume all values within a certain interval and as such are divisible into smaller and smaller fractional units is known as:", "options": {"a": "Discrete variable", "b": "Continuous variable", "c": "Qualitative variable", "d": "Score"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a variable that can in theory take any value within an interval, and can be divided into ever smaller fractional units, is a continuous variable. Variables are classified by the kind of values they can take. A discrete, or discontinuous, variable takes only separate, countable values with gaps between them: the number of children in a family, the number of errors in a maze, the number of hospital admissions. A family can have 2 or 3 children but never 2.6. A continuous variable has no gaps: between any two values another value is always possible. Height, weight, time and temperature are continuous; a reaction time could be 0.43 seconds, 0.432 seconds or 0.4321 seconds, limited only by the precision of the instrument. Henry Garrett (1926), in Statistics in Psychology and Education, therefore treats a score on a continuous variable as an interval rather than a point: a score of 43 really stands for anything from 42.5 to 43.5, which is why grouped frequency tables use exact or real limits. Many psychological traits, such as intelligence or anxiety, are treated as continuous even though test scores come in whole numbers. For example, the time Aditi takes to solve a puzzle (63.7 seconds) is continuous, but the number of puzzles she solves (4) is discrete. Option b is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of continuity: all values within an interval and divisibility into finer fractions. Exam trap: discrete is the exact reverse and is the usual distractor. Qualitative (categorical) variables, such as gender or diagnosis, differ in kind rather than amount, so they cannot be divided at all.", "topic": "Continuous versus discrete variables"}, {"q": "Assume that a researcher wants to do one time data collection and uses non-experimental research approach, suggest which methods are suitable", "options": {"a": "Longitudinal, Survey, Quasi experimental design", "b": "Survey, Cross sectional, and Cross-over designs", "c": "Quasi experimental, Causal comparative and Cross over designs", "d": "Predictive, Survey and Cross-Sectional designs"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: predictive, survey and cross-sectional designs all collect data once without manipulating any variable, so they fit a one-time, non-experimental study. Research designs differ on two questions. First, does the researcher manipulate an independent variable? If yes, the design is a true experiment (with random assignment) or a quasi-experiment (manipulation without random assignment), the distinction drawn by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963). If no, it is non-experimental: variables are measured as they already exist. Second, how many times are data collected? A cross-sectional design measures people once, at one point in time; a longitudinal design follows the same people over repeated occasions. Non-experimental approaches include descriptive surveys, correlational designs, predictive correlational designs (a variable measured now is used to forecast an outcome) and ex post facto or causal-comparative designs. Example: Meera, a nursing researcher, gives a stress questionnaire and a sleep-quality scale to 300 hostel students in one week. She manipulates nothing, measures once, and uses stress scores to predict sleep quality. Her study is at once a survey, cross-sectional and predictive. Option d is right because every design it lists is non-experimental and can be completed in a single round of data collection. Exam trap: the word 'design' tempts students to accept any familiar design name. Cross-over designs (each participant receives two or more treatments in sequence) and quasi-experiments involve an intervention by the researcher, and longitudinal designs need repeated measurement, so none of them fits. Causal-comparative research is non-experimental, but option c pairs it with two intervention designs.", "topic": "Non-experimental one-time designs"}, {"q": "The premorbid intelligence is the intellectual functioning prior to known or suspected onset of brain disease or dysfunction. There are no validated methods to estimate premorbid ability using current ability alone. However obtained ability or achievement scores are sometimes used to provide a rough estimate of a child's suspected premorbid ability. Which of the following test is used to assess this function?", "options": {"a": "Colored progressive matrices", "b": "Wechsler's intelligence scale for children", "c": "Test of nonverbal intelligence", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three listed instruments are ability tests whose obtained scores can give a rough estimate of a child's premorbid ability. Premorbid intelligence is the intellectual level a person had before a brain injury or illness began. Neuropsychologists need it because a score means 'impaired' only when compared with where the person started. In adults, estimates come from demographic formulas, 'hold' subtests that resist damage (the Deterioration Quotient of David Wechsler, 1958), or reading tests such as the National Adult Reading Test of Hazel Nelson (1982). In children these methods work poorly, because ability is still developing and reading is not yet a stable 'hold' skill. So clinicians fall back on obtained ability or achievement scores: results of any earlier ability test, school achievement records, or the child's best current performances. Any standardised ability test can supply such a score: the Coloured Progressive Matrices of John C. Raven (1947), the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (now WISC-V), or the Test of Nonverbal Intelligence of Linda Brown, Rita Sherbenou and Susan Johnsen (1982). Example: Kabir, aged 10, had a WISC at age 8 for a school referral. After a head injury, that earlier Full Scale IQ, or a pre-injury CPM or TONI result, is the best rough guide to his premorbid level. Option d is right because the stem asks which test supplies an obtained ability score, and all three are ability tests used with children. Exam trap: students pick the WISC alone because it is the best-known children's IQ test, but the stem's point is that any obtained ability score is used. Key note: no key was published for this paper. If the setter meant the single most standard measure, b (WISC) is defensible, since WISC-IV subtests underpin child premorbid estimation formulas; but nothing excludes the other two tests, so d is the safer reading.", "topic": "Premorbid ability in children"}, {"q": "The probability that the desired response will be performed is increased by taking away or preventing something undesirable whenever the desired response is made. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "Positive punishment", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Positive reinforcement", "d": "Negative punishment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: increasing a response by removing or preventing something unpleasant whenever the response occurs is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner (1938), consequences change how often a behaviour is repeated. Two words decide every case. 'Reinforcement' means the behaviour becomes more likely; 'punishment' means it becomes less likely. 'Positive' means something is added; 'negative' means something is taken away. That gives four cells. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise after homework). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up). Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding). Negative punishment removes something pleasant (losing phone time). Negative reinforcement covers escape learning, ending an aversive stimulus already present, and avoidance learning, preventing it from starting, which is why the stem says 'taking away or preventing'. Example: Arjun has a headache, takes a painkiller, and the pain goes away. Next time he has a headache he reaches for the tablet sooner. The removal of pain has strengthened tablet-taking. Option b is right because the probability of the response goes up (reinforcement) and it does so by taking away or preventing something undesirable (negative). Exam trap: many students treat 'negative reinforcement' as a polite name for punishment. It is not: all reinforcement, positive or negative, strengthens behaviour, while punishment weakens it. Avoidance in anxiety disorders is maintained by exactly this process, the second stage of the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947).", "topic": "Negative reinforcement"}, {"q": "Sam has the tendency to make everything seem so much worse than it usually is. Rick would say that Sam has a tendency to", "options": {"a": "Overgeneralise", "b": "Minimise", "c": "Do all-or-nothing thinking", "d": "Magnify"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: making things seem much worse than they really are is magnification, one of the cognitive distortions described by Aaron Beck (1967). Beck found that depressed and anxious patients did not simply feel sad or worried; they processed information in systematically biased ways. He named these errors cognitive distortions: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. David Burns (1980) later popularised a longer list in Feeling Good. Magnification means exaggerating the size, importance or badness of a negative event, a mistake or a flaw. Its twin, minimisation, shrinks the importance of positive events or of one's own strengths. Beck paired them because the same distorted lens is at work: the bad is blown up and the good is shrunk. Catastrophising, expecting the worst possible outcome, is a close relative. Example: Riya gets one critical comment on a 20-page project and tells her friend, 'This is a disaster, my teacher thinks I'm useless.' The size of the problem has been magnified. If she then dismisses three compliments as 'nothing', that is minimisation. In cognitive therapy the therapist helps her weigh the evidence and rescale the event. Option d is right because Sam's habit is to make everything seem much worse than it usually is, which is the definition of magnification. Exam trap: overgeneralisation is the near-miss. It draws a broad rule from one event ('I failed once, I always fail'), spreading the problem across time and situations rather than exaggerating its size. Minimise is the paired distortion working in the opposite direction.", "topic": "Beck's magnification distortion"}, {"q": "Mentalization based treatment was developed as a treatment for ________ by ________.", "options": {"a": "Major depression - Fenogy & Batman", "b": "Reactive attachment - Harry Stack Sullivan", "c": "Bipolar Disorder - Klerma & Weissman", "d": "Personality disorders - Fonagy & Bateman"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: mentalisation-based treatment (MBT) was developed for personality disorders, specifically borderline personality disorder, by Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy (1999). Mentalisation is the capacity to understand one's own and other people's behaviour in terms of mental states: feelings, wishes, beliefs and intentions. Fonagy built the idea on the attachment theory of John Bowlby (1969): a child whose mind is reliably 'read' by a caregiver learns to read minds too. Bateman and Fonagy argued that people with borderline personality disorder lose this capacity under attachment stress, for example in a quarrel with a partner, and then fall into impulsive acts, self-harm and black-and-white views of others. MBT, delivered as individual plus group therapy, trains the patient to pause, wonder what is going on in their own and the other person's mind, and stay curious rather than certain. Their 1999 randomised trial of a day-hospital programme in London showed fewer suicide attempts and admissions than standard care, with gains still present at eight-year follow-up. Example: Meera, who has borderline personality disorder, sees that her boyfriend has not replied to a message, concludes 'he hates me' and cuts herself. In MBT the therapist slows the story down and asks what else he might have been thinking or feeling. Option d is right because it gives both the correct disorder group and the correct developers. Exam trap: interpersonal psychotherapy for depression belongs to Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), and transference-focused psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder to Otto Kernberg (1989); examiners swap these names. Key note: option a prints misspelt versions of the same two names ('Fenogy & Batman') but pairs them with major depression, so it is wrong on the disorder even if the names are read charitably.", "topic": "Mentalisation-based treatment"}, {"q": "A friend asks you to bring sandwiches to a picnic and you agree. Later she asks you to get cold drink, burger and salad. The person has used which compliance strategy?", "options": {"a": "The low-ball technique", "b": "The over-commitment strategy", "c": "The whole-part technique", "d": "The passive obedience method"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: getting someone to agree to a modest commitment and then raising its cost is the low-ball technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1978) named low-balling after a car-showroom trick. The salesperson offers an attractive deal, the buyer agrees, and then the deal changes: the 'manager' cancels the discount or the extras cost more. Many buyers still go ahead, because having said yes they feel committed to the decision and want to stay consistent. In Cialdini's classic study, students were asked to join an experiment at 7 a.m. When told the time first, only about 31 percent agreed; when first asked to agree to the study and only then told the time, about 56 percent agreed, and almost all of them turned up. The power lies in the earlier commitment, not in the size of the request. Example: Riya asks Kabir to bring sandwiches to a picnic and he agrees. Later she adds cold drinks, burgers and salad. Kabir grumbles but brings them, because backing out now would feel like breaking his word. Option a is right because the first agreement is made at a low cost and the cost of keeping it is then raised, which is the defining feature of the low-ball. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door also starts small, but there the second request is a separate, larger favour; in the low-ball, the original commitment itself becomes more costly. Door-in-the-face reverses the order: a large refused request first, then a smaller one. 'Over-commitment', 'whole-part' and 'passive obedience' are not standard compliance tactics.", "topic": "Low-ball compliance technique"}, {"q": "The Variable Response Inconsistency Scale and The True Response Inconsistency Scales are the validity scales in", "options": {"a": "MCMI - II", "b": "MMPI", "c": "MCMI", "d": "MMPI - II"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Variable Response Inconsistency (VRIN) and True Response Inconsistency (TRIN) scales are validity scales of the MMPI-2, printed here as 'MMPI - II'. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). Its original validity scales were ? (Cannot Say), L (Lie), F (Infrequency) and K (Correction, added in 1946 with Paul Meehl). When James Butcher and colleagues (1989) restandardised the test as the 567-item MMPI-2, they added new validity scales, including VRIN, TRIN and F(B) (Back F), with F(p) and S following later. VRIN uses pairs of items with similar or opposite content; answering them inconsistently suggests random or careless responding. TRIN uses pairs of items with opposite content; answering 'true' to both, or 'false' to both, shows acquiescence or nay-saying regardless of content. These scales are checked before any clinical scale is read, because a high F score is uninterpretable if VRIN shows the person answered at random. Example: Arjun completes the MMPI-2 in 20 minutes without reading the items. His VRIN T-score is very high, so the clinician concludes that the elevated F and clinical scales reflect random responding, not psychosis. Option d is right because VRIN and TRIN were introduced with the MMPI-2 and belong to it (versions also appear in the MMPI-A and, as VRIN-r and TRIN-r, in the MMPI-2-RF). Exam trap: students pick 'MMPI' because the scales belong to the MMPI family, but the 1943 original had only ?, L, F and K. The Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977) and its revisions use their own modifying indices (Disclosure, Desirability, Debasement) and a Validity index instead.", "topic": "MMPI-2 validity scales"}, {"q": "As per the Mac Lean's triune Brain, the ________ controls the survival activities like breathing, heart rate and balance.", "options": {"a": "Reptilian brain", "b": "Mammalian brain", "c": "Primate brain", "d": "Human brain"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the triune brain model of Paul MacLean (1990), the reptilian brain controls basic survival functions such as breathing, heart rate and balance. MacLean, who had named the limbic system in 1952, proposed from the 1960s that the human brain is really three brains laid down in evolutionary order. The reptilian brain or R-complex (the brainstem and basal ganglia, with the cerebellum usually added in popular accounts) is the oldest layer. It runs automatic, life-sustaining activity: breathing, heart rate, blood pressure, balance, and routine, territorial and ritual behaviours. The paleomammalian brain, the limbic system, adds emotion, memory and caregiving. The neomammalian brain, the neocortex, adds language, reasoning and planning. In this model the layers can pull against each other, for example when a strong emotion overrides reasoned judgement. Example: when Meera is in deep sleep or under anaesthesia, her neocortex is barely engaged, yet she keeps breathing and her heart keeps beating, because the medulla and pons, part of MacLean's 'reptilian' core, run these functions without thought. Option a is right because breathing, heart rate and balance are hindbrain and brainstem functions, which MacLean assigned to the reptilian layer. Exam trap: the mammalian brain is the tempting near-miss because it is 'older than the cortex', but it is MacLean's emotional (limbic) layer, not the life-support layer. 'Primate brain' and 'human brain' are not names of his layers. Modern neuroscience rejects the strict evolutionary story, since reptiles and birds have forebrain structures too, but the model remains a common exam item.", "topic": "MacLean's triune brain"}, {"q": "Consider the following statements:- I. In arbitrary interference, distortion of thinking, in which a person cannot draw a conclusion based on any evidence. II. In selective thinking, person focus on only one aspect of a situation while ignoring all other relevant position. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "options": {"a": "Only 1", "b": "Only 2", "c": "Both 1 and 2", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: both statements describe Aaron Beck's cognitive distortions correctly once the printed slips are read as intended (arbitrary 'inference' and selective 'abstraction'). Aaron Beck (1967) noticed that depressed patients made systematic errors in processing information, and two of his first-named errors are tested here. Arbitrary inference means reaching a conclusion without supporting evidence, or even against contrary evidence. Statement I, despite awkward wording ('cannot draw a conclusion based on any evidence'), is trying to say exactly that: the conclusion rests on no evidence. Mind reading and fortune telling, from David Burns (1980), are narrower later names for such jumps. Selective abstraction, also called the mental filter, means focusing on one detail taken out of context while ignoring other, more salient features, then judging the whole experience by that detail. Statement II calls it 'selective thinking' and says the person focuses on one aspect and ignores the other relevant parts, which is the standard definition. Example: Arjun's friend walks past without greeting him and Arjun decides, 'He is angry with me' (arbitrary inference; the friend may not have seen him). At his appraisal Arjun hears nine compliments and one suggestion, and leaves thinking only about the suggestion (selective abstraction). Option c is right because statement I defines arbitrary inference and statement II defines selective abstraction. Exam trap: selective abstraction is easily confused with overgeneralisation; the first narrows to one detail within a situation, the second stretches one event into a rule for all situations. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Read literally, statement I says a person 'cannot' draw a conclusion, which would make it false and the answer b; but the misprints ('interference', 'selective thinking', 'position') show a sloppy transcription of two standard definitions, so c is the likely intent.", "topic": "Arbitrary inference, selective abstraction"}, {"q": "What is the meaning of ethical standards in research?", "options": {"a": "Choice of rules for researcher", "b": "Enforceable rules of conduct", "c": "Procedural guide for research", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: ethical standards are enforceable rules of conduct, as distinct from aspirational principles. The clearest model is the Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association (2002, amended 2010 and 2016). It has two layers. The five General Principles (beneficence and nonmaleficence, fidelity and responsibility, integrity, justice, and respect for people's rights and dignity) are aspirational: they describe the ideals psychologists aim for and are not themselves grounds for sanctions. The Ethical Standards, in ten sections, are enforceable rules for conduct; breaking one can lead to a complaint, sanctions, or loss of membership or licence. Standard 8 covers research and publication: institutional approval, informed consent, deception and debriefing, humane care of animals, honest reporting of results, plagiarism and authorship credit. In India, the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) play a similar role for biomedical and health research, enforced through institutional ethics committees. Example: Kabir, a doctoral student, collects data without the informed consent his ethics committee required. He has not merely fallen short of an ideal; he has broken an enforceable standard, and the committee can act against him. Option b is right because the code itself defines its standards as enforceable rules for conduct. Exam trap: 'all of the above' is tempting because standards do shape procedure and constrain researchers. But a 'choice of rules' is the opposite of enforceable, and a procedural guide describes how to run a study, not the obligations that bind the researcher. Remember: principles aspire, standards enforce.", "topic": "Ethical standards in research"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Classical conditioning requires the learning of new behaviour through association where two stimuli are linked together to produce a new learned response. Reason (R) There are four basic components of a classical conditioning process (unconditioned stimuli, conditioned stimuli, unconditioned response and conditioned response).", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason only lists the parts of classical conditioning; it does not explain why the Assertion is true. Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in his studies of digestion in dogs. A neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS, food) that naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR, salivation). After several pairings the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces a conditioned response (CR, salivation to the bell). Is A true? Yes: classical conditioning is learning by association, in which two stimuli become linked so that a new learned response occurs to the formerly neutral one. Is R true? Yes: the four basic components are the UCS, UCR, CS and CR. Does R explain A? No. A says what kind of learning classical conditioning is (association through pairing). R is a list of labels. The reason association is needed lies in the contiguity and, as Robert Rescorla (1968) showed, the contingency between CS and UCS: the CS must predict the UCS. Example: Riya's phone buzzes every time her boss sends an urgent message. After weeks the buzz alone makes her stomach tighten. Labelling 'buzz = CS, tension = CR' names the parts; it does not explain the learning. Option b is right because both statements are correct but R merely describes components. Exam trap: in Assertion-Reason items students pick a whenever both statements are true and on the same topic; the real test is whether R answers 'why is A true?' Key note: no key was published for this paper. A setter could treat the four components as explaining how the association forms, making a the key; b is preferred because R describes parts rather than giving a reason.", "topic": "Classical conditioning components (A-R)"}, {"q": "Personality is-", "options": {"a": "Everchanging dimension", "b": "Rigid and fixed", "c": "Evolve over time mostly by late adolescence", "d": "It develops till adulthood"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: personality evolves over time, and its basic shape is mostly in place by late adolescence, though it keeps maturing afterwards. Personality is the relatively enduring pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving that marks a person out. Gordon Allport (1937) called it a 'dynamic organisation': stable enough to be recognisable, yet able to change. Infant temperament, described by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1977), is the raw material; through childhood and adolescence, temperament, experience and identity work combine into adult traits. By late adolescence traits are fairly stable, which is why DSM-5-TR (2022) traces the onset of personality disorders to adolescence or early adulthood and urges caution before diagnosing them in children. Longitudinal studies reviewed by Brent Roberts and Wendy DelVecchio (2000) show that stability keeps rising with age, while average levels still shift: conscientiousness and agreeableness rise and neuroticism falls into middle age (the maturity principle). Example: Kabir was a shy, cautious child; at 18 he is a reserved, careful student; at 35 he is still reserved but calmer and more organised. His core pattern was set by late adolescence, yet it kept maturing. Option c is right because it captures both continuity and change, with most of the formation done by late adolescence. Exam trap: 'rigid and fixed' and 'everchanging' are the two extremes examiners use to catch students; neither fits the evidence. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Option d ('develops till adulthood') is close, and many texts say personality consolidates in early adulthood; c is preferred because it adds continued evolution while placing most development by late adolescence.", "topic": "Stability and change of personality"}, {"q": "Which of the following 'symbol' is not a category of the shading determinant response in the comprehensive scoring system (CSS) of Rorschach Inkblot Method?", "options": {"a": "FT", "b": "FD", "c": "YF", "d": "FV"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: FD (form dimension) is not a shading determinant; it codes depth seen from form and perspective alone, while FT, YF and FV all involve shading. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) for the Rorschach Inkblot Method, determinants code which feature of the blot made the person see what they saw: form (F), movement (M, FM, m), chromatic colour (C, CF, FC), achromatic colour (C', C'F, FC'), shading, form dimension (FD), and pairs and reflections. Shading has three subcategories. Texture (T, TF, FT) is shading felt as a tactile surface, like fur or silk. Vista (V, VF, FV) is shading seen as depth or distance. Diffuse shading (Y, YF, FY) is shading used in a vague, general way, like smoke or fog. The letter order shows whether form dominates: FT means form is primary and texture secondary, TF the reverse, and pure T has no form. Form dimension also codes depth, but the depth comes from size or perspective cues in the outline, with no use of shading. Example: on Card VI, Meera says, 'an animal skin, it looks furry here' (FT, texture). On Card IV, Kabir says, 'a giant seen from below; the feet are huge and the head is tiny because it is far away' (FD). Option b is right because FD is a separate determinant category coded without shading. Exam trap: vista and form dimension both code depth, so students mix them; vista needs shading, FD does not. Texture is linked to needs for closeness, vista to painful self-inspection, and diffuse shading to situational stress.", "topic": "Rorschach shading determinants"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the level of cognition is not suggested by Albert Ellis?", "options": {"a": "Evaluations", "b": "Inferences", "c": "Schemas", "d": "Demands"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: schemas are not one of the levels of cognition in Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy; schema is a term of Aaron Beck and Jeffrey Young. Albert Ellis (1962) built REBT on the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it, and emotional and behavioural Consequences. REBT separates kinds of cognition at point B. Inferences are 'cold' interpretations or hunches about what is happening, which may be true or false ('my friend ignored me on purpose'). Evaluations are 'hot' appraisals of what an event means for the person; these are the rational or irrational beliefs that produce emotion. At the root of irrational evaluations are demands: rigid musts, shoulds and oughts, which Ellis called musturbation. From demands flow awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global self-rating. REBT therapists spend little time disputing inferences and move quickly to the demands. Schemas, by contrast, come from the cognitive model of Aaron Beck (1967), in which early experience forms deep schemas or core beliefs that generate dysfunctional assumptions and automatic thoughts; Jeffrey Young (1990) extended this into schema therapy. Example: Arjun's manager criticises his report. He infers, 'She thinks I'm incompetent' (inference), then evaluates, 'I must never be criticised, and it is awful that I was' (demand and evaluation). An REBT therapist targets the must; a Beckian therapist might trace a defectiveness schema. Option c is right because evaluations, inferences and demands are Ellis's terms, while schemas belong to Beck and Young. Exam trap: Ellis and Beck are both cognitive pioneers, so examiners mix their vocabularies. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A student could argue d, since demands are a kind of irrational evaluation rather than a separate level; but demands are central to Ellis's theory, whereas schema is not his term, so c is the clearer odd one out.", "topic": "Ellis's levels of cognition"}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly explains the process of learned helplessness?", "options": {"a": "Generalised helpless behaviour; Uncontrollable bad events; Perceived lack of control", "b": "Uncontrollable bad events; Generalised helpless behaviour; Perceived lack of control", "c": "Uncontrollable bad events; Perceived lack of control; Generalised helpless behaviour", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: learned helplessness runs from uncontrollable bad events, to a perceived lack of control, to generalised helpless behaviour. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs electric shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group the same shocks with no way to stop them, and a third group no shocks. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock, most yoked dogs lay down and whimpered instead of escaping, while the other dogs learned quickly. Both shocked groups had received the same amount of shock, so it was the lack of control, not the shock, that produced the passivity. David Myers (2010) summarises the chain in a flow diagram: uncontrollable bad events lead to perceived lack of control, which leads to generalised helpless behaviour. The middle step is cognitive: the animal or person learns that responses and outcomes are independent. Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later added attributions: helplessness spreads most when the cause is seen as internal, stable and global, and this became a model of depression. Example: Meera fails three maths tests despite studying hard (uncontrollable bad events), decides that nothing she does affects her marks (perceived lack of control), and stops trying in every subject, even ones she likes (generalised helpless behaviour). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that puts the cause first, the perception in the middle and the behaviour last. Exam trap: options a and b reorder the same three phrases; the behaviour is the outcome and cannot come first. 'All of the above' is impossible when the options contradict each other.", "topic": "Learned helplessness sequence"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a parametric correlation?", "options": {"a": "Product moment", "b": "Rank difference", "c": "Point biserial", "d": "Partial"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the product moment correlation (Pearson's r) is the parametric correlation. A parametric statistic estimates a population parameter and rests on assumptions about the population: both variables measured on an interval or ratio scale, a roughly normal distribution, a linear relationship and homoscedasticity (equal spread of scores along the line). Karl Pearson (1896) built the product moment coefficient on these assumptions; it uses the actual scores, multiplying each person's deviations from the two means (the 'products of moments'). A non-parametric (distribution-free) coefficient makes no such assumptions and usually works on ranks. Charles Spearman's (1904) rank difference method, rho, turns scores into ranks and uses the differences between ranks, so it suits ordinal data or badly skewed scores. Example: Riya correlates the exact marks of 50 students in statistics and in research methods; the marks are interval level and normally distributed, so she uses Pearson's r. If her teacher had only ranked the students from 1 to 50, she would use Spearman's rho. Option a is right because the product moment r is the classic parametric coefficient, the one every text names when it contrasts parametric with non-parametric correlation. Exam trap: examiners pair 'product moment' with 'rank difference' and hope students mix them up; rank difference is Spearman's non-parametric method. Point biserial and partial correlation are offshoots of Pearson's r built for special cases (a dichotomous variable, or a third variable held constant), not the basic parametric correlation. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Point biserial r is algebraically a Pearson r with one variable scored 0 or 1, and partial r is computed from Pearson coefficients, so both share parametric assumptions; the setter's intended contrast is product moment versus rank difference, and product moment is the parent coefficient, so a is the best pick.", "topic": "Parametric correlation"}, {"q": "________ is the primary neurotransmitter transferred secreted by efferent axons of the central nervous system.", "options": {"a": "Botulinum toxin", "b": "Acetylcholine", "c": "Dopaminergic", "d": "Curare"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: acetylcholine is the main neurotransmitter released by the efferent (outgoing) axons of the central nervous system. Efferent neurons carry commands out of the brain and spinal cord. Every somatic motor neuron in the spinal cord and brainstem releases acetylcholine (ACh) onto skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction, where it binds nicotinic receptors and makes the muscle contract. The preganglionic neurons of both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic systems also release ACh, so nearly all output leaving the central nervous system begins at an ACh synapse. Otto Loewi (1921) showed that nerves signal chemically: stimulating a frog heart's vagus nerve released a substance ('Vagusstoff') that slowed a second heart bathed in the same fluid. Henry Dale (1914) had already described the actions of acetylcholine, and the two shared the 1936 Nobel Prize. In the synapse ACh is broken down by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase. Example: when Arjun decides to kick a football, motor neurons in his spinal cord fire and release ACh onto his leg muscles, which contract. Option b is right because acetylcholine is the transmitter itself, released at motor and preganglionic autonomic synapses. Exam trap: the other options are substances that act on the acetylcholine synapse (botulinum toxin blocks its release; curare blocks its receptor) or an adjective describing dopamine-using neurons, so they sound related but none is a transmitter secreted by efferent axons. Key note: 'transferred secreted' in the stem is a printing slip for 'secreted'; the meaning is unchanged.", "topic": "Acetylcholine in efferent output"}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. Specific phobia is fear of objects II. Claustrophobia is fear of being in small place. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "options": {"a": "Only 1", "b": "Only 2", "c": "Both 1 and 2", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: both statements are correct. A specific phobia is a marked, persistent and excessive fear of a particular object or situation, out of proportion to the real danger, that leads to avoidance or intense distress and, in DSM-5-TR (2022), lasts 6 months or more. Statement I is true: the fear is tied to specific objects (dogs, spiders, needles), though the full definition also covers situations. DSM-5-TR lists five types: animal, natural environment (heights, water, storms), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, planes, enclosed places) and other. Statement II is also true: claustrophobia (from Latin claustrum, a closed place) is fear of small or enclosed spaces such as lifts, tunnels or MRI scanners, and it belongs to the situational type. The person usually fears being trapped or unable to breathe. Example: Meera climbs ten floors to her office every day because the thought of being shut inside the lift makes her heart race; this is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia. Phobias are often explained by conditioning. John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear of a white rat in Little Albert, and Orval Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory adds that avoidance is negatively reinforced because it lowers anxiety, which keeps the phobia alive. Martin Seligman (1971) added preparedness: we learn to fear snakes and heights more readily than cars. Option c is right because statement I gives the core of specific phobia and statement II gives the correct meaning of claustrophobia. Exam trap: students confuse claustrophobia with agoraphobia, the fear of open or public places where escape may be hard (a separate DSM-5-TR disorder), or reject statement I because it leaves out situations; an incomplete definition is not a false one.", "topic": "Specific phobia and claustrophobia"}, {"q": "Some psychologist stated that the relationship between intelligence and creativity is not the same throughout the range of intelligence (IQ). Which of the following is appropriate explanation of relationship between these at different levels of IQ?", "options": {"a": "Below the critical level of IQ, it is zero and above that is positive", "b": "Below the critical level of IQ, the relationship is negative and above that is positive", "c": "Below the critical level of IQ, the relationship is positive and above that is negative", "d": "Below the critical level of IQ, the relationship is positive and above that level zero relationship"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: below a critical IQ the relationship between intelligence and creativity is positive, and above it the relationship is about zero. This is the threshold theory (threshold hypothesis). Creativity, in the terms of Joy Paul Guilford (1950), rests on divergent thinking: producing many, varied and original ideas (fluency, flexibility, originality, elaboration). Intelligence tests mostly reward convergent thinking towards one correct answer. Ellis Paul Torrance (1962) and Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found that creativity and IQ correlate positively up to an IQ of about 120. Above that level more IQ adds little or nothing, and creativity depends on motivation, openness to experience and other personality factors. So a minimum level of intelligence is necessary but not sufficient for creativity. Emanuel Jauk and colleagues (2013) later supported a threshold but showed that its height depends on how creativity is scored. Example: Kabir (IQ 95) and Ishaan (IQ 115) differ in creativity much as they differ in IQ, but between Ananya (IQ 130) and Rohan (IQ 150) IQ no longer predicts who writes the more original story. Option d is right because it states both halves of the threshold theory: a positive correlation below the critical level and a zero correlation above it. Exam trap: option c also begins with 'positive', so students who remember only the first half pick it; the threshold theory says the link fades to zero above about 120, it does not turn negative. Option a simply reverses the two halves.", "topic": "Threshold theory of creativity"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Many eccentric characters are charming and emotionally stable. Reason (R) Strictly following social norms are guarantee of mental health.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the assertion is true, but the reason is false. This item tests how abnormality is defined. One definition calls behaviour abnormal when it breaks social norms; another uses statistical rarity. Both fail as complete definitions, because being unusual is not the same as being disordered, and conformity is no proof of health. The assertion (A) is true. David Weeks (1995) studied about 1,000 eccentric people in Britain and the United States and found them, on average, happier and healthier than most people, with fewer visits to the doctor; few had any mental disorder. Clinicians therefore look for distress, dysfunction and danger, not mere difference. The reason (R) is false. Strict conformity does not guarantee mental health. A person can follow every rule and still have obsessive-compulsive disorder or depression, and norms themselves change across cultures and eras (homosexuality was listed in the DSM until 1973). Marie Jahoda (1958) defined positive mental health by self-acceptance, growth, autonomy, accurate perception of reality and environmental mastery, not by conformity. Example: Devika, a retired teacher, wears bright mismatched clothes, keeps twenty parrots and chats with them for hours, yet she has close friends, sleeps well and enjoys life; she is eccentric, not disordered. Option c is right because A matches the evidence on eccentrics, while R makes an absolute claim ('guarantee') that every modern definition of abnormality rejects. Because R is false, it cannot explain A; in fact R would contradict A. Exam trap: students accept R because the social norms definition sounds familiar, missing that the word 'guarantee' turns one partial criterion into a false claim.", "topic": "Eccentricity and abnormality"}, {"q": "Which of the following are true about adherence? 1. The patient must understand the treatment regimen 2. The practitioner must be intelligent 3. The patient must be satisfied with the relationship and treatment regimen 4. The patient must decide to adhere", "options": {"a": "1 & 2", "b": "3 & 4", "c": "1, 3 & 4", "d": "Only 4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 1, 3 and 4 are true; adherence needs a patient who understands the regimen, is satisfied and decides to adhere, while the practitioner's intelligence is not a condition. Adherence (the older term is compliance) is the extent to which a patient's behaviour, such as taking medicine, following a diet or attending check-ups, matches the agreed treatment advice. Roughly half of patients with chronic illness do not adhere fully, which makes it a core topic in health psychology. Philip Ley's (1988) cognitive hypothesis model holds that adherence is predicted by the patient's understanding of the information, memory for it and satisfaction with the consultation. M. Robin DiMatteo (2004) and colleagues put the same ideas as information, motivation and strategy: the patient must know what to do, want to do it and be able to do it. Satisfaction grows from a warm relationship, clear communication and a regimen that fits daily life. Finally the patient must intend, or decide, to follow it; much nonadherence is intentional, such as stopping a drug because of side effects. Example: Sameer, newly diagnosed with diabetes, follows his insulin plan because his doctor explained it in simple Hindi (understanding), listened to his worries (satisfaction) and agreed the plan with him, so he chose to stick to it (decision). Option c is right because statements 1, 3 and 4 are the patient-side requirements named in adherence models. Exam trap: statement 2 sounds sensible, but what predicts adherence is the practitioner's communication, warmth and clarity, not intelligence; a brilliant doctor who speaks in jargon still gets poor adherence.", "topic": "Treatment adherence"}, {"q": "The scale, where absolute zero point is known, is popularly known as:", "options": {"a": "Ordinal scale", "b": "Ratio scale", "c": "Interval scale", "d": "Nominal scale"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ratio scale is the scale with a known, true (absolute) zero point. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names or classifies (blood group, roll numbers). An ordinal scale adds order (class ranks, socioeconomic class), but the gaps between ranks need not be equal. An interval scale adds equal units, so differences are meaningful, but its zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius does not mean no heat, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds an absolute zero, meaning a complete absence of the property, so ratios become meaningful: 0 kg means no weight, and 80 kg is twice 40 kg. Reaction time, height, weight, age and number of errors are ratio scales. Every statistic is allowed on ratio data, including the geometric mean and the coefficient of variation. Example: Neha measures reaction time in milliseconds; 600 ms really is twice 300 ms, because 0 ms is a real zero, so she has ratio data. Option b is right because a known absolute zero is the defining feature that separates the ratio scale from all the other levels. Exam trap: the interval scale is the near miss, since it also has equal units; students forget that its zero is arbitrary. Psychological test scores such as IQ are treated as interval, because an IQ of 0 does not mean zero intelligence, so an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70.", "topic": "Ratio scale"}, {"q": "Which of these following occur in trace and delayed conditioning?", "options": {"a": "CS appears first, it disappears then US appears; CS appears first, but also present when the US appears", "b": "When US is first and after the disappearance of US, CS is presented; CS appears first, it disappears then US appears", "c": "When CS and US occurs together; CS appears first, but also present when the US appears", "d": "CS appears first, it disappears then US appears; when US is presented first and after the disappearance of US, CS is presented"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in trace conditioning the CS comes on and goes off before the US appears, and in delayed conditioning the CS comes on first and is still present when the US arrives. These are timing arrangements in classical conditioning, first studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927). A conditioned stimulus (CS, such as a tone) is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (US, such as food) that naturally produces a response (salivation). Four arrangements are taught. In delayed (delay) conditioning the CS starts first and overlaps the US; it is the most effective arrangement, because the CS acts as a clear warning signal. In trace conditioning the CS ends before the US begins, leaving a gap (the trace interval), so the learner must hold a memory 'trace' of the CS; learning is weaker and gets worse as the gap grows. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and US start together, giving no warning, so learning is weak. In backward conditioning the US comes first and the CS follows; it is the least effective and may even teach that the CS signals safety. Example: in an eyeblink study, a tone keeps sounding while a puff of air hits Kabir's eye (delay); for Riya the tone stops half a second before the puff (trace). Kabir learns to blink to the tone faster. Option a is right because its first half describes trace conditioning and its second half describes delayed conditioning, in the order the stem asks. Exam trap: 'delay' does not mean the CS comes late or the response is slow; it means the US is delayed while the CS stays on. Examiners also slip in the backward (US first) and simultaneous (together) descriptions.", "topic": "Trace and delay conditioning"}, {"q": "The pragmatic paradigm proposes that", "options": {"a": "The researcher should adopt an objective stance", "b": "The researcher should adopt an subjective stance", "c": "The researcher should adopt an approach based on research demands", "d": "None of above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the pragmatic paradigm says the researcher should choose an approach based on what the research question demands. A paradigm is a set of beliefs about reality (ontology), knowledge (epistemology) and method that guides research, a term made famous by Thomas Kuhn (1962). Positivism holds that one objective reality exists and that the researcher studies it from a detached, value-free position, usually with experiments and statistics. Constructivism (interpretivism) holds that reality is built by people, so the researcher works subjectively and closely with participants, usually through interviews and qualitative analysis. Pragmatism comes from the philosophy of Charles Sanders Peirce (1878), William James (1907) and John Dewey (1938), which judges ideas by their practical consequences: what works. In research methods, Abbas Tashakkori and Charles Teddlie (1998) and John Creswell (2003) made it the philosophical basis of mixed methods. The pragmatist sets aside the objective versus subjective debate and lets the problem decide: a survey where numbers are needed, interviews where meanings are needed, or both. Example: Priya wants to know how many college students in Pune use counselling services and why others avoid them; she runs a questionnaire for the first part and focus groups for the second, because each question needs a different tool. Option c is right because 'an approach based on research demands' is the defining feature of pragmatism: the question comes first and the method follows. Exam trap: options a and b are the stances of positivism (objective) and constructivism (subjective); pragmatism is defined by refusing to be locked into either, so it can use both.", "topic": "Pragmatic paradigm"}, {"q": "The hypothesis that a match between one's needs and what one receives from others in one's social network is known as the?", "options": {"a": "Matching Social Context Hypothesis", "b": "Matching Hypothesis", "c": "Person-Environment Fit Hypothesis", "d": "Need Hypothesis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the matching hypothesis states that social support helps most when what a person receives from the social network matches what that person needs. Social support is information from others that one is loved, cared for, valued and part of a network of mutual obligation. It comes in several forms: emotional (warmth, listening), tangible or instrumental (money, transport, meals), informational (advice) and esteem or appraisal support. Sheldon Cohen and Garth McKay (1984) proposed that support buffers stress only when it fits the demands of the stressor, and Carolyn Cutrona and Daniel Russell (1990) developed this into optimal matching theory: uncontrollable stressors such as a death call mainly for emotional support, while controllable ones such as losing a job call for practical help and advice. Shelley Taylor (2018), in her health psychology text, calls this the matching hypothesis. Mismatched support can fail or even backfire: advice offered to someone who needs comfort can feel like criticism. Example: when Ananya's father is admitted to hospital, she needs someone to drive her there and look after her younger brother. Friends who only send motivational messages leave her stressed, while a neighbour who gives her a lift and cooks dinner matches her need and truly lowers her distress. Option b is right because the 'match' between needs and the support received is the exact definition of the matching hypothesis. Exam trap: the same name is used in social psychology for the matching hypothesis of attraction of Elaine Walster (1966), which says we choose partners of similar attractiveness; the stem's mention of needs and the social network shows the health psychology meaning is intended.", "topic": "Matching hypothesis of social support"}, {"q": "John feels as though he is not living up to expectations of his parents. How would he be described by Adler:", "options": {"a": "As having low self-realization", "b": "As having an Adlerian complex", "c": "As having an inferiority complex", "d": "As having low actualization"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Alfred Adler would describe John as having an inferiority complex. Adler (1907) began with 'organ inferiority', the idea that people compensate for weak body parts, and widened it into a general theory. Every child starts small, weak and dependent, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. They are the engine of growth, because they drive a striving for superiority (later he said perfection or completion) through compensation. When these feelings become exaggerated and overwhelming, so that the person feels helpless and unable to meet life's tasks, Adler called it an inferiority complex. He traced it to three childhood conditions: organ inferiority, pampering and neglect. Some people mask it with a superiority complex, an inflated show of boasting and arrogance. In Understanding Human Nature (1927) he also stressed that each person's fictional final goal and style of life shape how these feelings are handled. Example: Riya's parents expect top marks and a medical seat; she feels she can never measure up, compares herself unfavourably with her cousins and stops trying. In Adlerian terms, her normal feelings of inferiority have grown into an inferiority complex, exactly like John in the stem. Option c is right because a persistent sense of failing to meet others' standards is the textbook picture of the inferiority complex, Adler's best-known concept. Exam trap: the distractors swap in humanistic growth terms. Self-actualisation belongs to Abraham Maslow (1943) and Carl Rogers (1961), and self-realisation to Carl Jung's (1921) individuation and Karen Horney's (1950) real self, not to Adler.", "topic": "Adler's inferiority complex"}, {"q": "Lesions to the language dominant thalamus can result in ________.", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's", "b": "Anterograde Amnesia", "c": "Anosognosia", "d": "Transient Asphasia Syndrome"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: a lesion of the language-dominant (usually left) thalamus can produce a transient aphasia syndrome, known as thalamic aphasia. Aphasia is a loss or disturbance of language caused by brain damage. Most aphasias follow damage to the cortex of the dominant hemisphere, such as Broca's area, described by Paul Broca (1861), or Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874). But subcortical structures also take part in language. The thalamus is a relay and integration centre in the diencephalon, and its pulvinar, ventral anterior and mediodorsal nuclei are wired to the language cortex. Bruce Crosson (1984) summarised the classic picture after left thalamic stroke or haemorrhage: fluent but reduced speech, word-finding difficulty, frequent semantic paraphasias (wrong words) and relatively spared repetition and comprehension. It is usually mild and often clears within days to weeks, which is why it is called transient; the lesion is thought to disrupt the connected cortex only temporarily. Example: after a small left thalamic stroke, Ramesh, aged 62, keeps calling a pen a 'pencil' and struggles to name objects, yet repeats sentences well; within three weeks his speech is almost normal. Option d is right because 'language-dominant' points to a language disorder, and aphasia from dominant thalamic lesions is typically transient. Exam trap: anosognosia, unawareness of one's own deficit, follows damage to the non-dominant (right) hemisphere, especially the right parietal lobe, the reverse side. Key note: no key was published for this paper, and 'Asphasia' is a misprint for aphasia. Thalamic lesions can also cause anterograde amnesia, as in Korsakoff syndrome, but that needs mediodorsal or anterior damage, often on both sides; the stem's stress on language dominance favours aphasia.", "topic": "Thalamic aphasia"}, {"q": "This is amongst the 16 source traits concluded by Cattell in his personality theory:", "options": {"a": "Privateness", "b": "Love", "c": "Hope", "d": "Intelligence"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: privateness is one of Raymond Cattell's 16 source traits (Factor N). Cattell (1946) built a trait theory by factor analysis. Surface traits are clusters of behaviour that appear together on the surface; source traits are the deeper underlying factors that produce them. Starting from the list of about 4,500 trait words drawn up by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), Cattell factor-analysed L-data (life records), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests) and arrived at 16 primary source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). In the fifth edition (1993) the factors are warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, liveliness, rule-consciousness, social boldness, sensitivity, vigilance, abstractedness, privateness, apprehension, openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism and tension. Privateness runs from forthright, open and genuine at the low end to discreet, guarded and non-disclosing at the high end. Example: Arjun scores high on privateness; he is polite with colleagues but rarely shares personal details and chooses his words carefully, while his friend Vikram, a low scorer, tells everyone everything. Option a is right because privateness is the exact name of Factor N in the current 16PF list. Exam trap: love and hope sound like personality qualities, but they are character strengths in the classification of Christopher Peterson and Martin Seligman (2004), not 16PF factors. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Factor B was called 'intelligence' in older 16PF versions and in several textbooks, and Cattell counted intelligence as an ability source trait, so d can be defended; but the fifth edition, the one that uses the label 'privateness', names Factor B 'reasoning', so a setter who knew 'privateness' most likely keyed a.", "topic": "Cattell's 16 source traits"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) The thoughts and action of the individual reflect his/her wants and goals. Reason (R) Behaviour is solely determined by motivational dynamics.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the assertion is true, but the reason is false. Motivation is the set of processes that start, direct and sustain goal-directed behaviour. The assertion (A) states a core idea of motivational psychology: what a person thinks about and does largely reflects their needs, wants and goals. Henry Murray (1938) listed psychological needs such as achievement and affiliation that show up in fantasy and action (hence the Thematic Apperception Test), Abraham Maslow (1943) ranked needs from physiological to self-actualisation, and Edwin Locke (1968) showed in goal-setting theory that specific goals direct attention and effort. So A is true. The reason (R) is false because of the word 'solely'. Behaviour is multiply determined: motivation interacts with ability, habit and learning, emotion, biology, personality and the situation. Kurt Lewin (1936) summed this up as B = f(P, E): behaviour is a function of the person and the environment. A highly motivated student without ability or opportunity will not succeed, and much behaviour runs on habit or reflex with little motive at all. Example: Tanvi badly wants to become a clinical psychologist, so she reads about therapy and joins a counselling club (A in action); yet her entrance score also depends on her preparation, test anxiety and the paper's difficulty, not on motivation alone. Option c is right because A is a sound statement about motivation while R overstates it; since R is false, it cannot explain A. Exam trap: absolute words such as 'solely', 'only', 'always' and 'guarantee' in a reason are a strong sign that it is false, even when the idea behind it is familiar.", "topic": "Motivation and behaviour"}, {"q": "Raj wakes up in an unknown hotel room. He does not know where he is or how he got there and not sure which day it is. This is most likely due to dissociative", "options": {"a": "Personality", "b": "Amnesia", "c": "Fugue", "d": "Trance"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: waking in a strange place with no idea how one got there points to a dissociative fugue. Dissociation is a break in the normal integration of memory, identity, consciousness and perception. In dissociative amnesia the person cannot recall important personal information, usually about a traumatic or stressful event, far beyond ordinary forgetting. A fugue (Latin fuga, flight) adds sudden, unexpected travel or bewildered wandering away from home or work, with amnesia for the past and sometimes confusion about identity or a new identity. Fugues last from hours to months and often follow severe stress such as war, disaster or a personal crisis. During the fugue the person may seem normal to others; when it ends, they 'come to' in an unfamiliar place, confused and frightened, with no memory of the journey. DSM-IV-TR (2000) listed dissociative fugue as a separate disorder; DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) made it a specifier: dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue. Example: after a heavy business loss, Sunil walks out of his office in Mumbai, and two days later he finds himself in a hotel room in Goa, unsure of the date and with no memory of the trip; that is the end of a fugue. Option c is right because the key clue is the unexplained journey: Raj is in an unknown hotel and does not know how he got there, so there was travel he cannot recall. Exam trap: the vignette never says 'travelled', so students pick plain amnesia; the strange location is the travel clue that separates fugue from simple amnesia. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Under DSM-5-TR this would be coded as dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, so b is defensible; but among these options, fugue names the specific picture the stem describes.", "topic": "Dissociative fugue"}, {"q": "Health promoting behaviours are also known as?", "options": {"a": "Behavioural pathogens", "b": "Antigens", "c": "Behavioural immunogens", "d": "Behaviour modification"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: health-promoting behaviours are also called behavioural immunogens. The term comes from Joseph D. Matarazzo (1984), the psychologist who had earlier defined health psychology. He borrowed his words from immunology. A biological pathogen (a virus or bacterium) causes disease, while an immunogen is something that triggers protective immunity. Matarazzo argued that behaviour works the same way. Behavioural pathogens are habits that raise the risk of illness: smoking, heavy drinking, a high-fat diet, unprotected sex and inactivity. Behavioural immunogens are habits that protect health: regular exercise, enough sleep, wearing a seatbelt, eating fruit and vegetables, brushing teeth and going for screening tests. The Alameda County study by Nedra Belloc and Lester Breslow (1972) showed why this matters. Adults who kept more of seven simple habits (seven to eight hours of sleep, breakfast, no snacking between meals, a healthy weight, regular exercise, little or no alcohol, no smoking) were healthier, and later follow-ups found they also lived longer. For example, Arjun stops smoking and starts a daily brisk walk. He has dropped a behavioural pathogen and added a behavioural immunogen. Health psychologists such as Shelley Taylor (1986) also call these health behaviours or health habits, and they try to build them early, because habits formed in adolescence tend to last. Option c is right because \"behavioural immunogen\" is the exact technical label Matarazzo gave to health-promoting behaviours, in contrast to health-damaging behavioural pathogens. Exam trap: examiners place \"behavioural pathogens\" first because it sounds similar and comes from the same paper, but it means the opposite (health-damaging habits). A student who remembers only that Matarazzo joined behaviour with an immunology word can easily pick the wrong half of the pair.", "topic": "Behavioural immunogens and pathogens"}, {"q": "The three important components of any paradigm are", "options": {"a": "Methodology, Ontology, Data Collection", "b": "Epistemology, Ontology, Methodology", "c": "Ontology, Data Collection Method and Theory", "d": "Epistemology, Methodology and Theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the three core components of any research paradigm are epistemology, ontology and methodology. A paradigm is a set of basic beliefs, a worldview, that guides how research is done. Thomas Kuhn (1962) made the word popular in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, but the three-part definition used in research methods comes from Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994). They said every paradigm answers three linked questions. Ontology asks what the nature of reality is: is there one objective reality (realism) or many realities constructed by people (relativism)? Epistemology asks what the relationship is between the knower and what can be known: can the researcher stand apart and be objective, or is knowledge created between researcher and participant? Methodology asks how the inquirer should go about finding things out: by controlled experiments and measurement, or by dialogue and interpretation. The answers flow in order. Your ontology limits your epistemology, and both shape your methodology. Positivism, for example, assumes one real world, a detached and objective researcher, and experimental, hypothesis-testing methods. Constructivism assumes multiple realities, knowledge built jointly with participants, and interpretive, qualitative methods. Suppose Meera studies how mothers in Pune experience a child's autism diagnosis. Her choice of in-depth interviews follows from a constructivist ontology and epistemology. Option b is right because only it lists the three philosophical components that Guba and Lincoln named. Exam trap: data collection methods and theory are distractors. Methods (interviews, questionnaires) are tools chosen within a methodology, not a component of the paradigm, and a theory is a specific explanation tested within a paradigm. Later writers add axiology (the role of values) as a fourth element, but the classic triad remains ontology, epistemology and methodology.", "topic": "Components of a research paradigm"}, {"q": "Which of the following definitions of abnormality is currently through best?", "options": {"a": "Statistical infrequency", "b": "Norm violation", "c": "Harmful dysfunction", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: harmful dysfunction is the definition of abnormality most widely thought best today. Each older definition captures something but fails on its own. Statistical infrequency calls behaviour abnormal if it is rare, far from the average. But rarity is not disorder: a very high IQ is rare yet healthy, while depression is common yet clearly a disorder. Norm violation calls behaviour abnormal if it breaks social rules. But norms change across cultures and eras (homosexuality was listed as a disorder until 1973), and many rule breakers, such as protesters, are not ill. Jerome Wakefield (1992) proposed a hybrid. A condition is a disorder only when two things are true. First, there is a dysfunction: some internal mechanism (perception, memory, emotion regulation) fails to do the job natural selection designed it for. That part is meant to be a scientific fact. Second, the dysfunction is harmful, judged by the person's cultural values: it causes distress or disability. Because it joins a factual element to a value element, it sorts both rare-but-harmless and common-but-harmful conditions correctly. DSM-5 builds both ideas into its definition of mental disorder: a dysfunction in psychological, biological or developmental processes, usually with significant distress or disability. For example, Kabir's panic attacks come from a fear system firing without real danger (dysfunction) and stop him travelling to college (harm), so he meets both tests. Option c is right because harmful dysfunction repairs the main weaknesses of the purely statistical and purely social definitions. Exam trap: \"none of these\" tempts students who remember that no definition is perfect. Critics such as Scott Lilienfeld and Lori Marino (1995) argue that evolutionary function is hard to establish, yet the stem asks which is currently thought best, and that is harmful dysfunction. Key note: the printed \"currently through best\" is a typo for \"currently thought best\".", "topic": "Harmful dysfunction definition"}, {"q": "You are asked by a close friend to give you notes for exam. You refuse to help. Later your friend asks if you would give notes for two subjects. Feeling guilty, you now agree to help. Your behaviour is predicted by the:", "options": {"a": "Foot-in-the-face technique", "b": "High-ball effect", "c": "Low-ball technique", "d": "Door-in-the-face effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: agreeing to a smaller request after refusing a bigger one is the door-in-the-face effect. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) first showed it. They asked university students to counsel juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years. Almost everyone refused. The same students were then asked to take a group of delinquents to the zoo for one afternoon, and about 50 percent agreed, against roughly 17 percent of students who heard only the zoo request. The technique has two steps: a large request that is almost sure to be refused, then the request the asker really wanted. Two explanations are offered. Reciprocal concessions: the asker seems to have backed down, and the norm of reciprocity makes us feel we should back down too. Guilt and self-presentation: refusing once makes us feel bad or look unhelpful, and saying yes repairs that feeling. In the stem, the friend first asks for exam notes, you refuse, then the friend asks for notes in just two subjects. Feeling guilty, you agree. For instance, Riya refuses to lend Kabir her notes for all five papers, but says yes when he asks for only two. Option d is right because the sequence (large request refused, smaller request accepted, driven by guilt or reciprocity) is exactly door-in-the-face. Exam trap: students mix up door-in-the-face with foot-in-the-door, which runs in the opposite order (a small request accepted first, then a larger one). \"Foot-in-the-face\" is a made-up blend of the two names. Key note: the stem is loosely worded (\"give you notes\" should read \"give them notes\"), and it leaves the size of the first request implied; read sensibly, notes for only two subjects is the smaller second request, which is what makes this door-in-the-face.", "topic": "Door-in-the-face technique"}, {"q": "A fraction of a population drawn by using a suitable method so that it can be regarded representative of the entire population is known as:", "options": {"a": "Variable", "b": "Estimate", "c": "Group", "d": "Sample"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a representative part of a population, drawn by a proper method, is called a sample. A population (or universe) is the whole group the researcher wants to draw conclusions about: all Class 12 students in Maharashtra, or all adults with schizophrenia in India. Studying everyone (a census) is usually too costly or impossible, so the researcher studies a sample: a subset chosen so that it mirrors the population. Henry E. Garrett (1926), in Statistics in Psychology and Education, and Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, both stress that a sample is useful only when it is representative, because the whole point is to generalise from the part to the whole. How representative it is depends on the sampling method. Probability methods give every member a known, non-zero chance of selection: simple random, systematic, stratified and cluster sampling. Non-probability methods (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball) are cheaper but risk bias. The famous failure is the 1936 Literary Digest poll, which predicted that Alf Landon would beat Franklin Roosevelt because its huge sample came from telephone directories and car registrations, lists that over-represented wealthier voters. Size alone does not rescue a biased sample. For example, Meera wants to know how stressed undergraduates in Mumbai feel. She cannot test all of them, so she randomly selects 400 from college rolls, stratified by stream. Those 400 students are her sample. Option d is right because \"a fraction of a population drawn by a suitable method so that it represents the whole\" is the textbook definition of a sample. Exam trap: \"estimate\" is close in spirit, because sample values are used to estimate population parameters, but an estimate is a number calculated from the sample (such as a sample mean), not the group of people itself.", "topic": "Definition of a sample"}, {"q": "A researcher was interested in stress levels of lecturers during lectures. She took the same group of 8 lecturers and measured their anxiety (out of 15) during a normal lecture and again in a lecture in which she had paid students to be disruptive and misbehave. The data were not normally distributed. Which test should she use to compare her experimental conditions?", "options": {"a": "Paired samples t-test", "b": "Mann-Whitney test", "c": "Wilcoxon rank-sum test", "d": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: two conditions, the same participants and non-normal data call for the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Choosing a test needs three questions. How many conditions? Two here (a normal lecture and a disrupted lecture). Same or different people? The same 8 lecturers gave both scores, so this is a repeated-measures (related, within-subjects) design. Are parametric assumptions met? No: the data are not normally distributed, and with only 8 people and scores out of 15 normality cannot be trusted anyway. The parametric test for two related conditions is the paired (dependent) samples t-test. Its non-parametric equivalent is the signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). It works on the difference between each person's two scores. It drops zero differences, ranks the remaining differences by size while ignoring their sign, then gives each rank back its plus or minus sign. If the disruptive lecture truly raises anxiety, most of the large differences will be positive, so the sum of the negative ranks (T) will be small, and a small T means a significant result. Because it uses ranks instead of raw scores, it does not assume normality and is less affected by outliers. For example, Arjun scores 6 in the normal lecture and 13 in the disrupted one, so his difference of 7 gets a high positive rank. Option d is right because the signed-rank test is the non-parametric test for comparing two related (paired) conditions. Exam trap: Wilcoxon gave his name to two tests. The rank-sum test, equivalent to the U test of Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), compares two independent groups; the signed-rank test compares two related conditions. Students who see \"Wilcoxon\" and \"non-normal\" often pick the wrong one.", "topic": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test"}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Psychopathology is characterized by subjective discomfort. Reason (R) Psychopathology refers to mental disorder themselves.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R is true and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R is true, but R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true, but R is false", "d": "A is false, but R is true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false as a general claim, while the Reason is true. Start with R. The word psychopathology has two meanings. It names the scientific study of mental, emotional and behavioural disorders, and it also refers to the mental disorders themselves, the abnormal or maladaptive patterns of thinking and behaving. Dennis Coon and John Mitterer (2010) define it in exactly these two ways, so R is true. Now A. Subjective discomfort means private feelings of pain, unhappiness or emotional distress. It is one of several criteria for judging abnormality, alongside statistical abnormality, social nonconformity and maladaptive behaviour. Many disorders do bring distress: depression, panic disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder. But psychopathology does not always cause personal discomfort. A person in a manic episode may feel wonderful. A person with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt after hurting others. Someone with a fixed delusion may be calm and certain. Sometimes the absence of discomfort is itself the sign: if Kabir shows no grief at all after his closest friend dies, a clinician might suspect a problem. So subjective discomfort is common but neither necessary nor sufficient, which is why David H. Barlow and V. Mark Durand (1995) say no single criterion defines disorder. Saying psychopathology is characterised by subjective discomfort turns one partial criterion into the defining feature, so A is false. Because A is false, R cannot explain it. Option d is right because R is a correct definition of the term while A overstates one criterion as a universal rule. Exam trap: distress appears in every abnormality chapter, so students treat it as defining and pick a or b. Key note: no key was published for this paper. If the setter meant A loosely (distress is a common feature of disorders), both statements would be true and b would be the pick; read as a general claim, A is false and d fits best.", "topic": "Meaning of psychopathology"}, {"q": "The ________ plays important role in formation, consolidation and retention of memories, spatial navigation and regulation of stress.", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Thalamus", "c": "Hypothalamus", "d": "Hippocampus"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the hippocampus forms and consolidates memories, supports spatial navigation and helps regulate the stress response. It is a curved, seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. The evidence for each role is classic. Memory first. In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed most of both hippocampi from Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) to stop his seizures. Brenda Milner (Scoville and Milner, 1957) showed that he could no longer form new long-term declarative memories (anterograde amnesia), although his short-term memory, older memories and motor learning were largely intact. So the hippocampus consolidates new facts and events into long-term storage, which is later held in the cortex. Spatial navigation next. John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular spot, together building a cognitive map; he shared the 2014 Nobel Prize for this. Eleanor Maguire (2000) found that London taxi drivers, who memorise thousands of streets, had larger posterior hippocampi. Stress last. The hippocampus is rich in glucocorticoid receptors. When cortisol rises, it signals the hypothalamus to switch off the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, a negative feedback brake. Chronic stress and high cortisol can shrink the hippocampus, which links stress to memory problems in depression and PTSD. For example, Meera, revising under heavy stress for weeks, may find new facts harder to retain partly because of this cortisol effect. Option d is right because only the hippocampus combines all three roles named in the stem. Exam trap: the amygdala sits right next to the hippocampus and is also limbic and stress-related, but it handles fear and the emotional colouring of memories, not general consolidation or spatial maps. The hypothalamus starts the stress response but does not form memories.", "topic": "Functions of the hippocampus"}, {"q": "Cause and effect relationship can be established using ________.", "options": {"a": "Predictive designs", "b": "Descriptive designs", "c": "Experimental designs", "d": "Survey designs"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: cause and effect can be established with experimental designs. In an experiment the researcher actively manipulates an independent variable (IV), measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV), and controls other variables, ideally by randomly assigning participants to conditions. These features meet the conditions for causation set out by the philosopher John Stuart Mill (1843): the cause must come before the effect, cause and effect must vary together, and other explanations must be ruled out. Random assignment is the key step. It spreads all other differences between people evenly across the groups, so the only systematic difference left is the IV. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) called this internal validity and showed that true experimental designs, such as the pretest-posttest control group design, rule out threats like history, maturation and selection. For example, Kabir wants to know whether a mindfulness programme reduces exam anxiety. He randomly assigns 60 students to the programme or to a waiting list, then compares their anxiety scores. If the programme group ends up calmer, he can say the programme caused the drop. Non-experimental designs cannot do this. Descriptive designs (case studies, observation) say what is happening. Survey designs measure attitudes and behaviours in large groups, usually at one time. Predictive or correlational designs show that variables move together and allow forecasts, but a third variable may explain the link, and the direction of influence may be unclear. Option c is right because only experiments combine manipulation, control and random assignment, which together allow a causal conclusion. Exam trap: \"predictive\" sounds causal, because if X predicts Y it seems X must cause Y. But correlation is not causation: ice cream sales predict drowning deaths only because both rise in hot weather.", "topic": "Experiments and causation"}, {"q": "Philip hates watching movies but watches because his wife wants to. After three years Philip comes to genuinely enjoy watching movies. This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "How acceptance can lead to compliance", "b": "How compliance can lead to acceptance", "c": "The \"boomerang effect\"", "d": "How psychological reactance can lead to acceptance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Philip's story shows how compliance can lead to acceptance. David Myers (1983), in his Social Psychology textbook, separates kinds of going along with others. Compliance is outwardly acting in line with a request or social pressure while privately disagreeing: Philip sits through movies he dislikes because his wife wants him to. Obedience is acting in response to a direct order. Acceptance means both acting and believing in line with the pressure: after three years Philip genuinely enjoys movies. The interesting point is the direction of change. We usually assume attitudes drive behaviour, but repeated behaviour can also change attitudes. Leon Festinger (1957) explained this with cognitive dissonance: acting against an attitude creates discomfort, and over time we reduce it by coming to like what we are doing. Daryl Bem (1972) offered self-perception theory: Philip watches himself going to movies again and again and infers that he must enjoy them. Familiarity helps too, because Robert Zajonc (1968) showed that mere exposure makes things more liked. In this way public compliance turns into private acceptance. A similar example: Arjun joins his father's early morning yoga only to please him, but after a year he looks forward to it himself. Option b is right because Philip first complied (acted without liking) and the repeated behaviour later produced genuine liking, which is acceptance. Exam trap: option a reverses the order; acceptance is already inner agreement, so it cannot be the starting point that leads to mere compliance. Reactance is resistance to pressure, and it would make Philip dislike movies more, not less.", "topic": "Compliance leading to acceptance"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of the technique is used as a logical disputation by a cognitive behaviour therapist?", "options": {"a": "Calculating the evidence", "b": "Operationalizing a negative construct", "c": "Weighing up the evidence", "d": "Guided discovery"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: operationalising a negative construct is an example of logical disputation in cognitive behaviour therapy. Albert Ellis (1962) made disputing of beliefs the active step of rational emotive behaviour therapy, and Raymond DiGiuseppe (1991) sorted disputes into types that CBT writers such as Windy Dryden and Michael Neenan (2004) still use. An empirical dispute asks \"Where is the evidence?\" and tests a belief against facts. A pragmatic (functional) dispute asks \"Does this belief help you?\". A logical dispute asks \"Does this conclusion follow?\" and examines the meaning and internal reasoning of the belief. Operationalising a negative construct is a logical method. The client uses a global label such as \"I am a failure\" or, in a voice hearer, \"I am evil\". The therapist asks for a precise definition: what exactly would a failure be, what would an evil person do with their time? Once the label is defined, the client can see that the conclusion does not follow: failing one exam does not make a whole person a failure. The error exposed is a reasoning error, such as overgeneralisation or labelling. For example, Arjun says \"I'm useless\" after a poor interview. His therapist asks him to list what a truly useless person would do every day; Arjun sees that he does almost none of it, and the label collapses on logical grounds. Option b is right because defining the negative construct tests whether the conclusion follows from its own meaning, which is the essence of logical disputation. Exam trap: weighing up the evidence for and against a thought is the classic empirical technique of Aaron Beck (1979), not a logical one. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A student could argue for c if logic and evidence are not kept apart, but standard CBT texts file evidence-weighing under empirical disputing, leaving b as the logical technique.", "topic": "Logical disputation in CBT"}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. In partial correlation relationship between two variables are calculated. II. In multiple correlation relationship between two or more than two independent variables are calculated. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "options": {"a": "Only 1", "b": "Only 2", "c": "Both 1 and 2", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: as the setter clearly intended, both statements describe the two techniques correctly in outline. Begin with simple correlation, which measures the relationship between two variables, such as hours studied and marks, using the coefficient r of Karl Pearson (1896). Partial correlation also looks at the relationship between just two variables, but it removes (partials out) the influence of one or more other variables on both. Written r12.3, a notation from George Udny Yule (1907), it is the correlation between variables 1 and 2 with variable 3 held constant. For example, shoe size and reading ability correlate in children only because both rise with age; partial out age and the correlation falls to near zero. So statement I is true: partial correlation calculates the relationship between two variables, after controlling for others. Multiple correlation (R) measures the relationship between one dependent (criterion) variable and two or more independent (predictor) variables taken together. Written R1.23, it is the correlation between actual criterion scores and the best weighted combination of the predictors, and R squared gives the share of criterion variance the predictors explain together, as in multiple regression. For example, Riya predicts first-year college marks (criterion) from Class 12 marks and an aptitude score (two predictors); the R between marks and that combination is a multiple correlation. Statement II is a compressed version of this: multiple correlation is built on two or more independent variables. Option c is right because statements I and II each capture the defining feature of their technique. Exam trap: students often swap the two terms. Partial correlation always ends with one pair of variables; multiple correlation always ends with one criterion against a combined set. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Read strictly, statement II omits the dependent variable, which would make a the answer; c is preferred because statement I is just as compressed and the wording of this paper is loose throughout.", "topic": "Partial and multiple correlation"}, {"q": "Shadow in Carl Jung personality theory is-", "options": {"a": "Dark side of personality", "b": "Unconscious archetypes", "c": "Protective personality mechanism", "d": "Repressed childhood memories"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in analytical psychology the shadow is the dark side of personality. Carl Gustav Jung (1917) divided the psyche into the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious (inherited images shared by all humanity). The contents of the collective unconscious are archetypes, universal patterns that appear in myths, dreams and art across cultures. The shadow is one of the main archetypes. It holds the qualities we do not want to admit we have: aggression, selfishness, envy, sexual and animal impulses, and anything our upbringing taught us is unacceptable. Jung called it the archetype of darkness and repression. In stories it appears as the devil, the villain or the evil twin. We often project our shadow onto others, so a person who loudly condemns greed in others may be denying his own. The shadow is not purely bad; it also holds energy, spontaneity and creativity. Healthy growth, which Jung called individuation, requires recognising and integrating the shadow rather than denying it. In Aion (1951) he wrote that becoming aware of the dark aspects of the personality as present and real is the essential condition for any self-knowledge. For example, Kabir, who prides himself on being gentle, keeps dreaming of violent quarrels; a Jungian therapist would see his shadow pressing for recognition. Option a is right because Jung's own description of the shadow is the dark, rejected side of the personality. Exam trap: the persona is the protective mask we show the world, so \"protective personality mechanism\" describes the persona, not the shadow. Repressed childhood memories belong to the personal unconscious and to the ideas of Sigmund Freud (1915), not to this inherited archetype.", "topic": "Jung's shadow archetype"}, {"q": "Match the following terms- 1. Debriefing, 2. Dehoaxing, 3. Desensitization, 4. Active Deception. A. Mislead the participants by providing them within formation that is not true B. Disclosure of any deception and providing an opportunity for participants to ask questions about the research C. Telling research participants of any deception or uses used in a study D. Process of eliminating any negative after effects that a participant might experience", "options": {"a": "A C D B", "b": "B C D A", "c": "B A D C", "d": "D C D A"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the correct matching is 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A. Deception in research means misleading participants about the purpose or procedures of a study so that they behave naturally. The obedience study of Stanley Milgram (1963), where participants believed they were shocking a learner, is the famous case. Because deception overrides fully informed consent, codes such as the American Psychological Association's (2002) Ethics Code allow it only when the study is valuable, no non-deceptive method exists, and participants learn the truth afterwards. The four terms fit together. Active deception (4-A) is deliberately giving participants false information, such as a fake cover story or a confederate posing as another participant; passive deception simply withholds information. Debriefing (1-B) is the full disclosure after participation: explaining the true purpose, revealing any deception and giving participants the chance to ask questions. David S. Holmes (1976) divided debriefing into two parts. Dehoaxing (2-C) is telling participants about any deception or ruses used and convincing them the false information was false. Desensitising (3-D) is detecting and removing any negative after-effects, such as anxiety or a damaged self-image; a Milgram participant who now believes he is cruel needs to hear that most people obeyed. For example, Riya tells students they failed an intelligence test when they did not (active deception). Afterwards she explains the real aim and answers questions (debriefing), shows that the scores were fake (dehoaxing) and makes sure no one leaves feeling stupid (desensitising). Option b is right because B, C, D, A is the only sequence that pairs each term with its own definition. Exam trap: dehoaxing and debriefing are easily swapped. Debriefing is the whole post-study conversation, while dehoaxing is the specific step of revealing the deception. Desensitising here has nothing to do with the systematic desensitisation therapy of Joseph Wolpe (1958).", "topic": "Deception and debriefing terms"}, {"q": "________ is divided into four sections rostrum, genu, body and splenium.", "options": {"a": "Efferent", "b": "Corpus Callosum", "c": "Basal Ganglia", "d": "Cingulum"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the corpus callosum is divided into the rostrum, genu, body and splenium. The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain, a thick arched band of about 200 million myelinated axons that links the left and right cerebral hemispheres so they can share information. In a midline (sagittal) slice of the brain it looks like a curved band lying above the lateral ventricles. Anatomists name four parts from front to back. The rostrum is the thin beak that curves down and back under the front end. The genu (Latin for knee) is the sharp bend at the front, carrying fibres between the two prefrontal cortices. The body, or trunk, is the long middle arch, linking motor, sensory and parietal areas. The splenium is the thick, rounded rear end, carrying fibres between the occipital and posterior temporal lobes, so it is vital for sharing visual information. Some texts add a narrow isthmus between the body and the splenium. Clinical cases show why the parts matter. Roger Sperry (1968) and Michael Gazzaniga (1967) studied split-brain patients whose corpus callosum had been cut to control epilepsy; words flashed only to the right hemisphere could not be named aloud. Joseph Jules Dejerine (1892) described pure alexia, loss of reading with writing preserved, after damage to the left visual cortex plus the splenium, because words seen by the right hemisphere could no longer cross to the language areas. Option b is right because rostrum, genu, body and splenium are the standard named subdivisions of the corpus callosum and of no other structure in the list. Exam trap: the cingulum is a white-matter bundle running just above the corpus callosum inside the cingulate gyrus, so it is a tempting near-miss, but it is not divided into these four parts.", "topic": "Parts of the corpus callosum"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"amity-mumbai-2021": [{"q": "The three-component needed for any experimental studies are", "concept": "The answer is b: the three components of a true experiment are manipulation, control and randomisation. An experiment is the only research design that can show cause and effect, and it needs three things to do so. Manipulation means the researcher deliberately changes the independent variable (IV), for example giving one group memory training and another group none. Control means holding every other variable constant, or using a control group, so that only the IV differs between conditions; time of day, instructions and noise are kept the same. Randomisation (random assignment) means each participant has an equal chance of being placed in any condition, so pre-existing differences in ability, mood or motivation are spread evenly across the groups. Ronald Fisher (1935) made randomisation a founding principle of experimental design, and Denise Polit and Cheryl Tatano Beck (2017) list manipulation, control and randomisation as the three defining properties of a true experiment. Suppose Arjun wants to test whether music improves recall. He randomly assigns 40 students to music or silence (randomisation), plays music to only one group (manipulation) and keeps the word list, timing and room identical (control). If the music group recalls more, he can infer cause. Remove randomisation and he has only a quasi-experiment. Option b is right because these are the three features that separate a true experiment from every other design. Exam trap: observation (measuring the dependent variable) happens in every study, including surveys and correlational research, so it does not define an experiment; and reference collection is a literature review step, not a design feature. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Some education research texts, such as Donald Ary and colleagues (2010), list control, manipulation and observation as the characteristics of experimental research, which makes option a defensible; but randomisation is what gives an experiment its causal power, and it is the triad used in psychology and nursing research texts.", "traps": {"a": "Manipulation, control and observation is a list some education research texts give, but observation (measuring the dependent variable) happens in every kind of study, so it does not mark out an experiment; without randomisation the design is only quasi-experimental.", "c": "Control, randomisation and observation drops manipulation, the heart of an experiment: if the researcher does not change the independent variable, the study is observational or correlational, however well controlled.", "d": "Reference collection (gathering literature) is a planning step in any research, not a component of experimental design, and this option also leaves out control."}}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. Rogerian therapy makes greatest use of body languages. II. Behaviour therapy is based on the principles of classical and operant conditioning. Select the correct answer using the codes given below", "concept": "The answer is b: only statement II is correct. Statement I is false, because the therapy that makes the greatest use of body language is Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls (1951) working with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman. Gestalt therapists work in the here and now and treat posture, gestures, breathing and tone of voice as messages the client is not yet aware of. A therapist might ask Meera, who taps her foot while saying she is calm, to \"be your foot and speak for it\", so that a disowned feeling comes into awareness. Carl Rogers (1951), by contrast, built client-centred therapy on verbal reflection of feelings and three core conditions: unconditional positive regard, empathic understanding and congruence. The Rogerian therapist follows the client's words and is deliberately non-directive; interpreting body language would make the therapist an expert interpreter, which Rogers avoided. Statement II is true. Behaviour therapy rests on learning principles. Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) underlies systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958), flooding and aversion therapy, while operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938) underlies token economies, shaping, time-out and contingency management. Hans Eysenck (1959) defined behaviour therapy as the application of modern learning theory to abnormal behaviour. Option b is right because statement I gives Rogers the hallmark of Gestalt therapy, while statement II is the standard textbook definition of behaviour therapy. Exam trap: examiners swap the humanistic therapies. Client-centred therapy uses reflection and empathy; Gestalt therapy uses awareness of the body, the empty chair and confrontation of avoidance.", "traps": {"a": "Only 1 would make the Rogerian statement true, but Carl Rogers' client-centred therapy relies on verbal reflection of feeling and the core conditions; heavy use of body language is the mark of Fritz Perls' Gestalt therapy.", "c": "Both 1 and 2 needs statement I to be true as well, and it is not, because body language is Gestalt therapy's tool, not Rogers'.", "d": "None of the above would mean statement II is false too, but behaviour therapy is defined by its basis in classical and operant conditioning (Hans Eysenck, 1959)."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) meaning of speech is understood by morphemes. Reason (R) Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning in speech perception.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true and R explains A. Language is built in layers. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that distinguish one word from another (/b/ and /p/ in \"bat\" and \"pat\"), but on their own they carry no meaning. Morphemes are the smallest units that do carry meaning. A morpheme can be a free word (\"book\") or a bound piece that cannot stand alone, such as the plural \"-s\", the past tense \"-ed\" or the prefix \"un-\". So \"unhappiness\" has three morphemes: un + happy + ness. Above morphemes come syntax (rules for combining words), semantics (meaning of words and sentences) and pragmatics (use in a social context). Leonard Bloomfield (1933) defined the morpheme as the minimal meaningful form of a language, and every introductory psychology text repeats this. When Kabir hears \"the dogs barked\", he extracts meaning from the morphemes \"dog\", \"-s\", \"bark\" and \"-ed\": plural number and past time come from the bound morphemes, not from any single sound. A is true: grasping what speech means depends on identifying its morphemes. R is true: morphemes are the smallest meaningful units. R explains A: because morphemes are where meaning first enters the stream of sounds, the listener must decode them to understand speech. Option a is right because the reason gives the very property that makes the assertion work. Exam trap: phoneme versus morpheme. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound; morphemes are the smallest units of meaning, and a reversed definition is the usual distractor. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A strict reader could call A overstated, since full meaning also needs syntax, semantics and context, and pick d; but the setter pairs the two statements as claim and reason, so a is the better pick.", "traps": {"b": "This would say R is true but unrelated to A. Here R states exactly why morphemes carry meaning, so the link between the two is direct.", "c": "R is not false: since Leonard Bloomfield (1933) the morpheme has been defined as the smallest meaningful unit of language. The false version would be the smallest unit of sound, which is the phoneme.", "d": "This treats A as false, but understanding what an utterance means does depend on decoding its morphemes; syntax and context add to that meaning, they do not replace it."}}, {"q": "When we consider the height of adult males, height is the variable. But the height of any individual is a:", "concept": "The answer is a: the height of any one individual is a variate. In statistics a variable is a characteristic that can take different values across people, objects or occasions: height, weight, IQ, reaction time. A variate is one particular value that the variable takes for one case. The variable is the general dimension; the variate is a single observation on it. If a researcher records the heights of adult men, \"height\" is the variable. When Rohan is measured at 172 cm, that single figure, 172 cm, is a variate, and when Dev is measured at 165 cm, that is another variate of the same variable. A set of variates together forms the data, and organising them into a frequency distribution is the first step of descriptive statistics. The same root gives the words univariate (analysing one variable at a time, as with a mean or a histogram), bivariate (two variables, as in a correlation between height and weight) and multivariate (many variables at once, as in MANOVA or factor analysis). So the vocabulary runs from the general to the particular: variable (height), variate (Rohan's 172 cm), data (all the recorded heights) and scale (the system of units used, here a ratio scale in centimetres). Option a is right because the stem contrasts the general characteristic, height, with its value in one individual, which is the definition of a variate. Exam trap: \"score\" sounds right, but a score is a value earned on a test or rating scale, such as 45 on an anxiety inventory, while height is a physical measurement; and \"data\" is the whole collection of observations, not one value.", "traps": {"b": "Data is the whole collection of recorded observations (the heights of all the men measured), not the single value belonging to one person.", "c": "A score is the numerical result a person earns on a test or rating scale; height is a physical measurement on a variable, so its single value is called a variate.", "d": "A scale is the measuring system or level of measurement (nominal, ordinal, interval or ratio in Stanley Smith Stevens' 1946 scheme), not an individual's value on it."}}, {"q": "Which is NOT the characteristic of DSM-5?", "concept": "The answer is c: DSM-5 does not provide a multi-axial assessment system. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is published by the American Psychiatric Association. DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer (1980), introduced a five-axis system: Axis I clinical disorders, Axis II personality disorders and mental retardation, Axis III general medical conditions, Axis IV psychosocial and environmental problems, and Axis V the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF). DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) kept it. DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes in favour of non-axial documentation: mental disorders, personality disorders and relevant medical conditions are listed together, psychosocial problems are recorded with Z codes, and the GAF was replaced by tools such as the WHO Disability Assessment Schedule 2.0. DSM-5-TR (2022) kept this format. The other three statements are true. DSM-5 remains mainly categorical: a person either meets criteria for a disorder or does not, though it adds dimensional severity ratings. Most disorders use polythetic criteria sets, meaning a person needs only some symptoms from a list (for example 5 of 9 for major depressive disorder), so two people with the same diagnosis can share few symptoms. DSM-5 also gives an Outline for Cultural Formulation, a 16-question Cultural Formulation Interview and a section on cultural concepts of distress. If Ananya is diagnosed today, her record reads \"major depressive disorder, moderate; hypothyroidism\", not \"Axis I, Axis III\". Option c is right because the multi-axial system belonged to DSM-III to DSM-IV-TR and was abolished in 2013. Exam trap: students remember the five axes vividly from older books and assume they still exist; and \"categorical\" is still true of DSM-5 even though it added dimensional ratings.", "traps": {"a": "True of DSM-5: it is still a categorical system in which a disorder is present or absent, with dimensional severity specifiers added on top, so this cannot be the odd one out.", "b": "True of DSM-5: most disorders use polythetic criteria (a minimum number of symptoms from a list, such as 5 of 9 for major depressive disorder), so this is a genuine characteristic.", "d": "True of DSM-5: it gives an Outline for Cultural Formulation and a 16-question Cultural Formulation Interview, so this is a characteristic, not the exception."}}, {"q": "Match the following:- Sampling: 1. Quota, 2. Purposive, 3. Systematic, 4. Stratified, 5. Cluster. A. A process of sampling in which a number of groups are identified in a population, then some groups are randomly selected for participation in a research project. B. A process in which subgroups, usually convenience samples, are identified and a specified number of individuals from each group are included in the research. C. A non-random sampling technique in which participants are selected for a study because of some desirable characteristics, like expertise in some area. D. A process of sampling in which an apparently unbiased but non-random sample is created, such as by creating a list of every element in the population and selecting every nth member from the population. E. A process of sampling in which groups of interest are identified, then participants are selected at random from these groups.", "concept": "The answer is d: Quota B, Purposive C, Systematic D, Stratified E, Cluster A. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known chance of selection; in non-probability sampling it does not. Quota sampling (B) is non-probability: the researcher identifies subgroups, such as men and women or age bands, and recruits conveniently available people until each subgroup's set number (quota) is filled. Purposive or judgemental sampling (C) is also non-probability: people are chosen because they have a desired characteristic, such as being expert counsellors. Systematic sampling (D) takes every nth name from a list after a starting point; it looks unbiased, but strictly it is not simple random sampling, which is why the definition calls it apparently unbiased but non-random. Stratified random sampling (E) divides the population into strata of interest and then selects at random within each stratum, so every subgroup is properly represented. Cluster sampling (A) selects whole groups, such as schools or villages, at random and studies those clusters; it is efficient when no list of individuals exists. Leslie Kish (1965), in Survey Sampling, set out these designs. Suppose Ishaan studies exam stress in Pune. He could stratify by school board and pick students at random within each board (stratified), or randomly pick 10 schools and test every Class 12 student in them (cluster). Option d is right because it is the only sequence that pairs each method with its definition in the order 1 to 5. Exam trap: quota and stratified sampling both start by dividing the population into subgroups. The difference is the last step: quota fills each subgroup by convenience, stratified fills it at random. In cluster sampling, by contrast, the groups themselves are what get sampled.", "traps": {"a": "A C D E B gives quota the cluster definition and cluster the quota definition; cluster sampling picks whole groups at random, while quota sampling fills subgroup numbers by convenience.", "b": "B D E C A calls purposive sampling the every-nth procedure and systematic sampling random selection within strata; those are systematic and stratified sampling, and selection for a desirable trait (C) is purposive.", "c": "C B D A E swaps quota with purposive and stratified with cluster; choosing people for a trait such as expertise is purposive, and randomly selecting whole groups is cluster sampling."}}, {"q": "The bridge that connects the two lobes in the thalamus is called as", "concept": "The answer is c: the massa intermedia joins the two lobes of the thalamus. The thalamus is a pair of egg-shaped masses of grey matter, one on each side of the third ventricle, in the diencephalon at the top of the brainstem. It is the main relay station of the forebrain: almost every sensory pathway except smell synapses in a thalamic nucleus before reaching the cortex. In most human brains the two thalamic lobes touch across the third ventricle at a small bridge of grey matter called the massa intermedia, also known as the interthalamic adhesion. It is missing in a minority of people, more often in men, and its function is still uncertain. Neil Carlson (2013), in Physiology of Behavior, describes the thalamus as two lobes connected by the massa intermedia, which crosses the third ventricle. When Tara feels a pinprick on her hand, the signal reaches the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus and is relayed to the somatosensory cortex; sounds she hears are relayed through the medial geniculate nucleus to the auditory cortex, and what she sees passes through the lateral geniculate nucleus to the visual cortex. Each of these nuclei sits inside one thalamic lobe, while the massa intermedia is the only bridge between the two lobes. Option c is right because the massa intermedia is the named structure that links the left and right thalamus. Exam trap: diencephalon is tempting because the thalamus belongs to it, but it is the whole region (thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus and subthalamus), not a bridge. Commissures such as the corpus callosum join the cortical hemispheres, whereas projection fibres connect the cortex with lower centres.", "traps": {"a": "Projection fibres are white matter tracts that link the cortex with the thalamus, brainstem and spinal cord (such as the internal capsule); they run up and down, not across between the two thalami.", "b": "The diencephalon is the whole forebrain region containing the thalamus and hypothalamus; it is the home of the thalamus, not the bridge between its lobes.", "d": "The medial geniculate nucleus is a thalamic relay nucleus for hearing that sends auditory signals to the temporal cortex; it lies inside each thalamus and joins nothing across the midline."}}, {"q": "When treating a client with a specific phobia, a therapist has forced the client to imagined the feared stimulus which the client is unpleasant of, is a technique called?", "concept": "The answer is a: making a phobic client vividly imagine the feared stimulus at full strength is implosion, or implosive therapy. Exposure therapies reduce fear through extinction: if the feared stimulus keeps appearing and nothing bad happens, the conditioned fear weakens. They differ in pace and in whether the stimulus is real or imagined. Systematic desensitisation, by Joseph Wolpe (1958), is gradual: the client learns relaxation and climbs a hierarchy of scenes from least to most frightening. Flooding plunges the client into the most feared situation at once, usually in real life (in vivo), with escape blocked until anxiety subsides. Implosive therapy, developed by Thomas Stampfl (1967) with Donald Levis, is flooding in imagination: the therapist describes exaggerated, highly vivid scenes of the feared object, sometimes laced with psychodynamic themes, and keeps the client imagining them until the anxiety \"implodes\" and burns out. Neha, for example, fears dogs. Her therapist asks her to close her eyes and picture snarling dogs surrounding and biting her, keeping the scene going for an hour without relaxation, until her fear drops. Option a is right because the stem stresses that the client is forced to imagine the unpleasant stimulus, which is the defining feature of implosion. Exam trap: flooding and implosion both use maximum intensity and no relaxation; the key word is imagined (implosion) versus real-life exposure (flooding). Pairing an unwanted behaviour with something unpleasant is aversion therapy, a different technique altogether. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Some texts allow \"imaginal flooding\", so c is defensible, but where both terms are offered, imagined exposure at full intensity is keyed as implosion.", "traps": {"b": "Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) also uses imagination, but it is graded and paired with relaxation, so the client is never forced to face the most feared scene first.", "c": "Flooding uses the same full-intensity, no-escape exposure, but classically in real life (in vivo); when the exposure is imagined and exaggerated, the technique is called implosion.", "d": "Conditioned aversion (aversion therapy) pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking, with an unpleasant stimulus to make it unattractive; it targets habits, not the extinction of a phobia."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are the four stages of Adlerian therapy, through which the patients in therapy are encouraged to acquire a more positive and productive way of life by developing new insights, skills and behaviours.", "concept": "The answer is b: Adlerian therapy moves through engagement, assessment, insight and reorientation. Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology, which sees each person as a goal-directed, socially embedded whole whose problems come from a mistaken style of life and discouraged social interest. Rudolf Dreikurs (1967) turned Adler's ideas into a structured therapy, and Gerald Corey (2017) presents it in four phases. Engagement: therapist and client form a collaborative, respectful relationship and agree on goals. Assessment: the therapist explores the client's lifestyle through family constellation, birth order, early recollections and basic mistakes. Insight: the therapist offers tentative interpretations (\"Could it be that...?\") so the client understands the purposes behind the symptoms. Reorientation: the client turns insight into action, trying new behaviours and strengthening social interest, helped by encouragement, \"acting as if\" and \"catching oneself\". Consider Siddharth, a second-born who feels he must always outdo his brother. After building rapport, his therapist gathers his early memories, then helps him see that his goal of superiority keeps him anxious, and finally coaches him to act as if he were already good enough at work. Option b is right because these are the four phases named in standard counselling texts, ending in reorientation, where the change in behaviour happens. Exam trap: the other options mix in steps from other schools. Clarification, confrontation and interpretation come from psychodynamic technique, while \"reassessment\" and \"orientation\" are invented near misses for reorientation. Remember that insight alone is not the goal for Adlerians; it must be followed by action.", "traps": {"a": "Assessment, clarification, interpretation, reassessment sounds like a generic clinical cycle; it leaves out building the relationship and ends with reassessment instead of the action phase of reorientation.", "c": "Clarification, confrontation and interpretation are the classic psychodynamic intervention sequence (Ralph Greenson, 1967), and orientation is a near miss for Adler's reorientation.", "d": "This mixes Adler's assessment with psychodynamic clarification and interpretation and misnames the last phase; it also lacks the engagement phase, which Adlerians treat as essential."}}, {"q": "A general name for therapy is that attempt to change the clients behaviour by altering the consequences of the behaviour is a", "concept": "The answer is c: contingency management is the general name for therapies that change behaviour by altering its consequences. A contingency is an if-then link between a behaviour and what follows it. Operant conditioning, from Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) work, shows that behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent, and behaviour followed by no reward or by punishment becomes less frequent. Contingency management applies this deliberately: the therapist first records the target behaviour and what currently reinforces it, then rearranges the consequences. It is an umbrella covering positive reinforcement, shaping, token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968), response cost, extinction and time-out. It is also one of the best-supported treatments for stimulant and other substance use, where clients earn vouchers or prizes for drug-free urine samples. For example, Aarav's parents give him a star each evening he finishes homework without being reminded, and five stars buy a weekend cricket match, while tantrums no longer win attention. His behaviour changes not through talking about it but through changing what it earns. Option c is right because the stem asks for the general name for consequence-based therapies, and contingency management is exactly that category. Exam trap: time-out is one specific contingency (removing access to reinforcement after misbehaviour), so it is an example of contingency management, not the general name. In vivo desensitisation works on the stimulus that comes before the response, through classical conditioning, not on consequences.", "traps": {"a": "Time-out (from positive reinforcement) is a single consequence-based technique that briefly removes a child from rewards after misbehaviour; it is one tool within contingency management, not the general name.", "b": "In vivo desensitisation exposes a client step by step to the real feared situation, building on Joseph Wolpe's (1958) counterconditioning; it changes the response to an antecedent stimulus, not the consequences of behaviour.", "d": "In vitro means in glass (a test tube) and is not a therapy term; the real contrast is in vivo (real life) versus imaginal desensitisation, both classical conditioning methods aimed at fear."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Neurotic needs include such motives as a desire to dominate, to inflict pain or to subject oneself to the will of another person. Reason (R) Neurotic needs are non-productive and do not foster heath.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. The statements come from Abraham Maslow's (1970) holistic-dynamic theory, in the account given by Jess Feist and Gregory Feist (2009). Maslow separated several kinds of needs. Conative needs form the five-level hierarchy: physiological, safety, love and belongingness, esteem and self-actualisation; aesthetic and cognitive needs sit alongside them. Neurotic needs are different. They are usually reactive, compensations for basic needs that were never met, and they include motives such as a desire to dominate, to inflict pain, or to subject oneself to the will of another person. So A is true. Such needs are nonproductive: they keep an unhealthy style of life going, have no value in the striving for self-actualisation and lead to pathology whether or not they are satisfied. So R is true. But R is a further property of neurotic needs, not the reason why they include domination, cruelty and submission. A lists what neurotic needs contain; R describes their effect. The content of these needs is explained by their origin in thwarted basic needs. For example, Vikram was never made to feel safe or loved as a child and now needs to control every decision his wife makes; the control never makes him feel secure, however often he gets it, which is exactly why Maslow called such needs nonproductive. Option b is right because A and R are separate true facts. Exam trap: the list of domination, pain and submission sounds like Karen Horney's (1942) neurotic needs or Erich Fromm's (1941) sadism and masochism, but this wording is Maslow's. Key note: no key was published for this paper. One could read R as the criterion that makes these motives neurotic and so choose a; but R states what neurotic needs do, not why they take this form, so b is the safer pick.", "traps": {"a": "This would need R to be the cause of A. R says neurotic needs are nonproductive and unhealthy, which is their effect; it does not explain why they take the form of domination, cruelty or submission.", "c": "R is not false: Abraham Maslow (1970) held that neurotic needs are nonproductive, keep an unhealthy style of life going and lead to pathology whether satisfied or not.", "d": "A is not false: in Maslow's account neurotic needs include the desire to dominate, to inflict pain and to submit to another's will, usually as compensation for unmet basic needs."}}, {"q": "According to Lawrence Frank, Thematic Apperception Test is classified under which category of projective technique?", "concept": "The answer is c: in Lawrence Frank's classification the TAT is an interpretative technique. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term \"projective methods\" in a paper arguing that a person given ambiguous material will impose his or her own private world on it. He sorted the methods by what the person is asked to do with the material. Constitutive methods give formless material that the person must structure, such as Rorschach inkblots, finger painting or clay. Constructive methods give definite materials to arrange into a larger product, such as building a scene in the World Test or a design in the Mosaic Test. Interpretative methods ask the person to say what an experience or picture means to them, which is what the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) does: the person tells a story about what led up to a scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. Cathartic methods, such as play, release emotion, and refractive methods read personality through distortions of a conventional medium, such as handwriting. When Pooja tells a story about TAT card 1 (a boy looking at a violin), she interprets the picture in terms of her own needs and pressures. Option c is right because the stem asks for Frank's category, and Frank placed story-telling about pictures under interpretation. Exam trap: in Gardner Lindzey's (1959) later and more popular five-way scheme the TAT is a construction technique, so construction is the right answer only when the question names Lindzey. Frank's constructive group meant arranging materials into a product, not telling stories.", "traps": {"a": "Construction is where Gardner Lindzey (1959) placed the TAT; Frank's own constructive category was for arranging given materials into a product, such as the Mosaic Test, not telling stories.", "b": "Refractive methods in Frank's scheme study personality through distortions in a conventional medium of communication, such as handwriting or speech, not through stories about pictures.", "d": "Constitutive methods give unstructured material that the person must organise, such as Rorschach inkblots, clay or finger paints; TAT pictures already have content to be interpreted."}}, {"q": "The ______ contains the nucleus and much of the machinery that provides the life process of the cell.", "concept": "The answer is a: the soma, or cell body, contains the nucleus and much of the machinery for the life processes of the cell. A neuron has four main parts. Dendrites are branching extensions that receive messages from other neurons. The soma is the cell body. It holds the nucleus with the cell's DNA, and its cytoplasm contains ribosomes and rough endoplasmic reticulum that make proteins, Golgi apparatus that package them and mitochondria that supply energy. The soma integrates incoming signals and keeps the whole cell alive. The axon is a single long fibre that carries the action potential away from the soma, often wrapped in myelin. Terminal buttons (axon terminals) at the ends of the axon's branches release neurotransmitter into the synapse. The stem is almost word for word Neil Carlson's (2013) description in Physiology of Behavior. The importance of the soma was shown long ago. Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1888) argued from stained tissue that each neuron is a separate cell, and Augustus Waller (1850) had already found that a nerve fibre cut off from its cell body degenerates (Wallerian degeneration), because proteins are made in the soma and shipped down the axon. Think of Rhea's neuron as a factory: the dendrites are the mailroom, the soma is the head office and production floor, the axon is the delivery road and the terminal buttons are the loading docks. Option a is right because only the soma houses the nucleus and the protein-making machinery. Exam trap: students sometimes pick the axon because it is the most famous part, but the axon only conducts; dendrites receive and terminal buttons release.", "traps": {"b": "Dendrites are the tree-like branches that receive signals from other neurons; they contain some organelles but not the nucleus.", "c": "The axon carries the action potential away from the soma to other cells; it depends on proteins made in the soma and degenerates if cut off from it.", "d": "Terminal buttons are the swellings at the ends of axon branches that release neurotransmitter into the synapse; they handle output, not the cell's life processes."}}, {"q": "One of the dangers of individual testing is that the examinees may get bored if the questions are too easy or become frustrated if the items are too difficult. To get around this problem, some intelligence tests are arranged so that the more difficult questions are presented only to the examinees who have gotten the easier questions correct. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: giving harder items only to examinees who pass the easier ones is adaptive testing. In a fixed test everyone takes the same items, so a bright child wastes time on easy items and a struggling child faces a string of failures. Adaptive, or tailored, testing fits the items to the person. Individual intelligence tests have long done this by hand. Lewis Terman's (1916) Stanford-Binet started at a level suited to the child, established a basal age where all items were passed and stopped at a ceiling where all were failed; the modern Stanford-Binet and Wechsler scales keep such start points and discontinue rules. Computerised adaptive testing (CAT), built on item response theory by Frederic Lord (1980), chooses each next item from a calibrated item bank to match the current estimate of ability: a correct answer brings a harder item, a wrong answer an easier one. Adaptive tests reach the same reliability with far fewer items and keep examinees neither bored nor frustrated, which is why the GRE and many licensing exams use them. For example, when Diya, aged 9, passes all the vocabulary items at the age 8 level, the examiner moves her to age 9 and 10 items and stops when she fails several in a row, so she never sits through 40 trivial words. Option b is right because the stem describes items being chosen according to the examinee's previous answers, which is the definition of adaptive testing. Exam trap: because the stem mentions intelligence tests, students drift towards a validity term, but construct validity and cross-validation concern whether a test measures what it should, not how items are given.", "traps": {"a": "Construct validity is the extent to which a test measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955); it is a property of scores, not a way of choosing items.", "c": "Norm-referenced interpretation compares a person's score with a standardisation group, for example as a percentile rank; it describes how scores are read, not how items are tailored during testing.", "d": "Cross-validation re-tests a selection or regression equation on a fresh sample to check that its validity holds up (it usually shrinks); it has nothing to do with matching item difficulty to the examinee."}}, {"q": "A variable which can theoretically assume all values within a certain interval and as such are divisible into smaller and smaller fractional units is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a variable that can in theory take any value within an interval, and can be divided into ever smaller fractional units, is a continuous variable. Variables are classified by the kind of values they can take. A discrete, or discontinuous, variable takes only separate, countable values with gaps between them: the number of children in a family, the number of errors in a maze, the number of hospital admissions. A family can have 2 or 3 children but never 2.6. A continuous variable has no gaps: between any two values another value is always possible. Height, weight, time and temperature are continuous; a reaction time could be 0.43 seconds, 0.432 seconds or 0.4321 seconds, limited only by the precision of the instrument. Henry Garrett (1926), in Statistics in Psychology and Education, therefore treats a score on a continuous variable as an interval rather than a point: a score of 43 really stands for anything from 42.5 to 43.5, which is why grouped frequency tables use exact or real limits. Many psychological traits, such as intelligence or anxiety, are treated as continuous even though test scores come in whole numbers. For example, the time Aditi takes to solve a puzzle (63.7 seconds) is continuous, but the number of puzzles she solves (4) is discrete. Option b is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of continuity: all values within an interval and divisibility into finer fractions. Exam trap: discrete is the exact reverse and is the usual distractor. Qualitative (categorical) variables, such as gender or diagnosis, differ in kind rather than amount, so they cannot be divided at all.", "traps": {"a": "A discrete variable takes only separate, countable values with gaps between them, such as the number of siblings, so it cannot be divided into finer fractional units.", "c": "A qualitative (categorical) variable differs in kind rather than amount, such as gender, religion or diagnosis; it has no numerical interval to subdivide.", "d": "A score is a single value a person obtains on a test or measure, not a type of variable; scores may come from continuous or discrete variables."}}, {"q": "Assume that a researcher wants to do one time data collection and uses non-experimental research approach, suggest which methods are suitable", "concept": "The answer is d: predictive, survey and cross-sectional designs all collect data once without manipulating any variable, so they fit a one-time, non-experimental study. Research designs differ on two questions. First, does the researcher manipulate an independent variable? If yes, the design is a true experiment (with random assignment) or a quasi-experiment (manipulation without random assignment), the distinction drawn by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963). If no, it is non-experimental: variables are measured as they already exist. Second, how many times are data collected? A cross-sectional design measures people once, at one point in time; a longitudinal design follows the same people over repeated occasions. Non-experimental approaches include descriptive surveys, correlational designs, predictive correlational designs (a variable measured now is used to forecast an outcome) and ex post facto or causal-comparative designs. Example: Meera, a nursing researcher, gives a stress questionnaire and a sleep-quality scale to 300 hostel students in one week. She manipulates nothing, measures once, and uses stress scores to predict sleep quality. Her study is at once a survey, cross-sectional and predictive. Option d is right because every design it lists is non-experimental and can be completed in a single round of data collection. Exam trap: the word 'design' tempts students to accept any familiar design name. Cross-over designs (each participant receives two or more treatments in sequence) and quasi-experiments involve an intervention by the researcher, and longitudinal designs need repeated measurement, so none of them fits. Causal-comparative research is non-experimental, but option c pairs it with two intervention designs.", "traps": {"a": "Longitudinal designs measure the same participants repeatedly over time, and quasi-experimental designs involve a researcher-imposed intervention without random assignment; only the survey fits a one-time non-experimental study.", "b": "Survey and cross-sectional designs fit, but a cross-over design is an experimental design in which each participant receives two or more treatments in sequence, so it needs manipulation and repeated measurement.", "c": "Causal-comparative (ex post facto) research is non-experimental, but quasi-experimental and cross-over designs both involve a treatment introduced by the researcher."}}, {"q": "The premorbid intelligence is the intellectual functioning prior to known or suspected onset of brain disease or dysfunction. There are no validated methods to estimate premorbid ability using current ability alone. However obtained ability or achievement scores are sometimes used to provide a rough estimate of a child's suspected premorbid ability. Which of the following test is used to assess this function?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three listed instruments are ability tests whose obtained scores can give a rough estimate of a child's premorbid ability. Premorbid intelligence is the intellectual level a person had before a brain injury or illness began. Neuropsychologists need it because a score means 'impaired' only when compared with where the person started. In adults, estimates come from demographic formulas, 'hold' subtests that resist damage (the Deterioration Quotient of David Wechsler, 1958), or reading tests such as the National Adult Reading Test of Hazel Nelson (1982). In children these methods work poorly, because ability is still developing and reading is not yet a stable 'hold' skill. So clinicians fall back on obtained ability or achievement scores: results of any earlier ability test, school achievement records, or the child's best current performances. Any standardised ability test can supply such a score: the Coloured Progressive Matrices of John C. Raven (1947), the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (now WISC-V), or the Test of Nonverbal Intelligence of Linda Brown, Rita Sherbenou and Susan Johnsen (1982). Example: Kabir, aged 10, had a WISC at age 8 for a school referral. After a head injury, that earlier Full Scale IQ, or a pre-injury CPM or TONI result, is the best rough guide to his premorbid level. Option d is right because the stem asks which test supplies an obtained ability score, and all three are ability tests used with children. Exam trap: students pick the WISC alone because it is the best-known children's IQ test, but the stem's point is that any obtained ability score is used. Key note: no key was published for this paper. If the setter meant the single most standard measure, b (WISC) is defensible, since WISC-IV subtests underpin child premorbid estimation formulas; but nothing excludes the other two tests, so d is the safer reading.", "traps": {"a": "Raven's Coloured Progressive Matrices is a non-verbal reasoning test for children aged about 5 to 11; it does give an obtained ability score, so it is one of the valid tests rather than the single answer.", "b": "The WISC is the standard full-scale children's IQ battery and is certainly used, but the stem says obtained ability scores in general, so the CPM and TONI qualify too.", "c": "The Test of Nonverbal Intelligence is a language-free ability test that also yields an obtained ability score; choosing it alone ignores the other two equally usable tests."}}, {"q": "The probability that the desired response will be performed is increased by taking away or preventing something undesirable whenever the desired response is made. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: increasing a response by removing or preventing something unpleasant whenever the response occurs is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner (1938), consequences change how often a behaviour is repeated. Two words decide every case. 'Reinforcement' means the behaviour becomes more likely; 'punishment' means it becomes less likely. 'Positive' means something is added; 'negative' means something is taken away. That gives four cells. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise after homework). Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up). Positive punishment adds something unpleasant (a scolding). Negative punishment removes something pleasant (losing phone time). Negative reinforcement covers escape learning, ending an aversive stimulus already present, and avoidance learning, preventing it from starting, which is why the stem says 'taking away or preventing'. Example: Arjun has a headache, takes a painkiller, and the pain goes away. Next time he has a headache he reaches for the tablet sooner. The removal of pain has strengthened tablet-taking. Option b is right because the probability of the response goes up (reinforcement) and it does so by taking away or preventing something undesirable (negative). Exam trap: many students treat 'negative reinforcement' as a polite name for punishment. It is not: all reinforcement, positive or negative, strengthens behaviour, while punishment weakens it. Avoidance in anxiety disorders is maintained by exactly this process, the second stage of the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947).", "traps": {"a": "Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour (a scolding) and makes the behaviour less likely; the stem describes an increase produced by removal.", "c": "Positive reinforcement also increases behaviour, but by adding something pleasant (praise, money), not by removing or preventing something undesirable.", "d": "Negative punishment (time-out, response cost) removes something pleasant and makes the behaviour less likely, the opposite direction to the stem."}}, {"q": "Sam has the tendency to make everything seem so much worse than it usually is. Rick would say that Sam has a tendency to", "concept": "The answer is d: making things seem much worse than they really are is magnification, one of the cognitive distortions described by Aaron Beck (1967). Beck found that depressed and anxious patients did not simply feel sad or worried; they processed information in systematically biased ways. He named these errors cognitive distortions: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. David Burns (1980) later popularised a longer list in Feeling Good. Magnification means exaggerating the size, importance or badness of a negative event, a mistake or a flaw. Its twin, minimisation, shrinks the importance of positive events or of one's own strengths. Beck paired them because the same distorted lens is at work: the bad is blown up and the good is shrunk. Catastrophising, expecting the worst possible outcome, is a close relative. Example: Riya gets one critical comment on a 20-page project and tells her friend, 'This is a disaster, my teacher thinks I'm useless.' The size of the problem has been magnified. If she then dismisses three compliments as 'nothing', that is minimisation. In cognitive therapy the therapist helps her weigh the evidence and rescale the event. Option d is right because Sam's habit is to make everything seem much worse than it usually is, which is the definition of magnification. Exam trap: overgeneralisation is the near-miss. It draws a broad rule from one event ('I failed once, I always fail'), spreading the problem across time and situations rather than exaggerating its size. Minimise is the paired distortion working in the opposite direction.", "traps": {"a": "Overgeneralisation is Beck's distortion of drawing a sweeping conclusion from a single incident ('always', 'never'), not exaggerating how bad something is.", "b": "Minimisation is the opposite half of Beck's pair: shrinking the importance of good events or one's own strengths, not making things seem worse.", "c": "All-or-nothing (dichotomous) thinking sorts experiences into two extremes, total success or total failure; it is about rigid categories, not exaggerated size."}}, {"q": "Mentalization based treatment was developed as a treatment for ________ by ________.", "concept": "The answer is d: mentalisation-based treatment (MBT) was developed for personality disorders, specifically borderline personality disorder, by Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy (1999). Mentalisation is the capacity to understand one's own and other people's behaviour in terms of mental states: feelings, wishes, beliefs and intentions. Fonagy built the idea on the attachment theory of John Bowlby (1969): a child whose mind is reliably 'read' by a caregiver learns to read minds too. Bateman and Fonagy argued that people with borderline personality disorder lose this capacity under attachment stress, for example in a quarrel with a partner, and then fall into impulsive acts, self-harm and black-and-white views of others. MBT, delivered as individual plus group therapy, trains the patient to pause, wonder what is going on in their own and the other person's mind, and stay curious rather than certain. Their 1999 randomised trial of a day-hospital programme in London showed fewer suicide attempts and admissions than standard care, with gains still present at eight-year follow-up. Example: Meera, who has borderline personality disorder, sees that her boyfriend has not replied to a message, concludes 'he hates me' and cuts herself. In MBT the therapist slows the story down and asks what else he might have been thinking or feeling. Option d is right because it gives both the correct disorder group and the correct developers. Exam trap: interpersonal psychotherapy for depression belongs to Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), and transference-focused psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder to Otto Kernberg (1989); examiners swap these names. Key note: option a prints misspelt versions of the same two names ('Fenogy & Batman') but pairs them with major depression, so it is wrong on the disorder even if the names are read charitably.", "traps": {"a": "The names are garbled forms of Fonagy and Bateman, but MBT was not designed for major depression; the classic depression therapies are Beck's cognitive therapy and Klerman and Weissman's interpersonal psychotherapy.", "b": "Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) founded interpersonal psychiatry and died in 1949, long before MBT; reactive attachment disorder is a childhood condition, not MBT's target.", "c": "Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984) developed interpersonal psychotherapy for depression (adapted for bipolar disorder as interpersonal and social rhythm therapy by Ellen Frank); they did not create MBT."}}, {"q": "A friend asks you to bring sandwiches to a picnic and you agree. Later she asks you to get cold drink, burger and salad. The person has used which compliance strategy?", "concept": "The answer is a: getting someone to agree to a modest commitment and then raising its cost is the low-ball technique. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1978) named low-balling after a car-showroom trick. The salesperson offers an attractive deal, the buyer agrees, and then the deal changes: the 'manager' cancels the discount or the extras cost more. Many buyers still go ahead, because having said yes they feel committed to the decision and want to stay consistent. In Cialdini's classic study, students were asked to join an experiment at 7 a.m. When told the time first, only about 31 percent agreed; when first asked to agree to the study and only then told the time, about 56 percent agreed, and almost all of them turned up. The power lies in the earlier commitment, not in the size of the request. Example: Riya asks Kabir to bring sandwiches to a picnic and he agrees. Later she adds cold drinks, burgers and salad. Kabir grumbles but brings them, because backing out now would feel like breaking his word. Option a is right because the first agreement is made at a low cost and the cost of keeping it is then raised, which is the defining feature of the low-ball. Exam trap: foot-in-the-door also starts small, but there the second request is a separate, larger favour; in the low-ball, the original commitment itself becomes more costly. Door-in-the-face reverses the order: a large refused request first, then a smaller one. 'Over-commitment', 'whole-part' and 'passive obedience' are not standard compliance tactics.", "traps": {"b": "'Over-commitment strategy' is not a named compliance technique in social psychology; it borrows the word commitment, which is the mechanism behind the low-ball.", "c": "'Whole-part technique' is not a compliance tactic; the whole versus part distinction belongs to methods of learning and memorising, not to social influence.", "d": "'Passive obedience' is not a compliance strategy; obedience, as studied by Stanley Milgram (1963), is yielding to a direct order from an authority, whereas here a peer makes requests."}}, {"q": "The Variable Response Inconsistency Scale and The True Response Inconsistency Scales are the validity scales in", "concept": "The answer is d: the Variable Response Inconsistency (VRIN) and True Response Inconsistency (TRIN) scales are validity scales of the MMPI-2, printed here as 'MMPI - II'. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). Its original validity scales were ? (Cannot Say), L (Lie), F (Infrequency) and K (Correction, added in 1946 with Paul Meehl). When James Butcher and colleagues (1989) restandardised the test as the 567-item MMPI-2, they added new validity scales, including VRIN, TRIN and F(B) (Back F), with F(p) and S following later. VRIN uses pairs of items with similar or opposite content; answering them inconsistently suggests random or careless responding. TRIN uses pairs of items with opposite content; answering 'true' to both, or 'false' to both, shows acquiescence or nay-saying regardless of content. These scales are checked before any clinical scale is read, because a high F score is uninterpretable if VRIN shows the person answered at random. Example: Arjun completes the MMPI-2 in 20 minutes without reading the items. His VRIN T-score is very high, so the clinician concludes that the elevated F and clinical scales reflect random responding, not psychosis. Option d is right because VRIN and TRIN were introduced with the MMPI-2 and belong to it (versions also appear in the MMPI-A and, as VRIN-r and TRIN-r, in the MMPI-2-RF). Exam trap: students pick 'MMPI' because the scales belong to the MMPI family, but the 1943 original had only ?, L, F and K. The Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977) and its revisions use their own modifying indices (Disclosure, Desirability, Debasement) and a Validity index instead.", "traps": {"a": "The MCMI-II (Theodore Millon, 1987) checks response style with its modifying indices, Disclosure, Desirability and Debasement, plus a Validity index; it has no VRIN or TRIN.", "b": "The original MMPI of Hathaway and McKinley (1943) had only the ?, L, F and K validity scales; VRIN and TRIN arrived with the 1989 MMPI-2.", "c": "The first MCMI (Theodore Millon, 1977) relied on a validity index and response-style adjustments, not paired-item inconsistency scales like VRIN and TRIN."}}, {"q": "As per the Mac Lean's triune Brain, the ________ controls the survival activities like breathing, heart rate and balance.", "concept": "The answer is a: in the triune brain model of Paul MacLean (1990), the reptilian brain controls basic survival functions such as breathing, heart rate and balance. MacLean, who had named the limbic system in 1952, proposed from the 1960s that the human brain is really three brains laid down in evolutionary order. The reptilian brain or R-complex (the brainstem and basal ganglia, with the cerebellum usually added in popular accounts) is the oldest layer. It runs automatic, life-sustaining activity: breathing, heart rate, blood pressure, balance, and routine, territorial and ritual behaviours. The paleomammalian brain, the limbic system, adds emotion, memory and caregiving. The neomammalian brain, the neocortex, adds language, reasoning and planning. In this model the layers can pull against each other, for example when a strong emotion overrides reasoned judgement. Example: when Meera is in deep sleep or under anaesthesia, her neocortex is barely engaged, yet she keeps breathing and her heart keeps beating, because the medulla and pons, part of MacLean's 'reptilian' core, run these functions without thought. Option a is right because breathing, heart rate and balance are hindbrain and brainstem functions, which MacLean assigned to the reptilian layer. Exam trap: the mammalian brain is the tempting near-miss because it is 'older than the cortex', but it is MacLean's emotional (limbic) layer, not the life-support layer. 'Primate brain' and 'human brain' are not names of his layers. Modern neuroscience rejects the strict evolutionary story, since reptiles and birds have forebrain structures too, but the model remains a common exam item.", "traps": {"b": "The (paleo)mammalian brain is MacLean's limbic system, which governs emotion, motivation, memory and parental care rather than breathing or heart rate.", "c": "'Primate brain' is not one of MacLean's three layers; his newest layer is the neomammalian brain (neocortex), which handles reasoning and language, not vital reflexes.", "d": "'Human brain' is not a layer of the triune model; the whole model describes the human brain, and its highest layer, the neocortex, does thinking rather than survival functions."}}, {"q": "Consider the following statements:- I. In arbitrary interference, distortion of thinking, in which a person cannot draw a conclusion based on any evidence. II. In selective thinking, person focus on only one aspect of a situation while ignoring all other relevant position. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "concept": "The answer is c: both statements describe Aaron Beck's cognitive distortions correctly once the printed slips are read as intended (arbitrary 'inference' and selective 'abstraction'). Aaron Beck (1967) noticed that depressed patients made systematic errors in processing information, and two of his first-named errors are tested here. Arbitrary inference means reaching a conclusion without supporting evidence, or even against contrary evidence. Statement I, despite awkward wording ('cannot draw a conclusion based on any evidence'), is trying to say exactly that: the conclusion rests on no evidence. Mind reading and fortune telling, from David Burns (1980), are narrower later names for such jumps. Selective abstraction, also called the mental filter, means focusing on one detail taken out of context while ignoring other, more salient features, then judging the whole experience by that detail. Statement II calls it 'selective thinking' and says the person focuses on one aspect and ignores the other relevant parts, which is the standard definition. Example: Arjun's friend walks past without greeting him and Arjun decides, 'He is angry with me' (arbitrary inference; the friend may not have seen him). At his appraisal Arjun hears nine compliments and one suggestion, and leaves thinking only about the suggestion (selective abstraction). Option c is right because statement I defines arbitrary inference and statement II defines selective abstraction. Exam trap: selective abstraction is easily confused with overgeneralisation; the first narrows to one detail within a situation, the second stretches one event into a rule for all situations. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Read literally, statement I says a person 'cannot' draw a conclusion, which would make it false and the answer b; but the misprints ('interference', 'selective thinking', 'position') show a sloppy transcription of two standard definitions, so c is the likely intent.", "traps": {"a": "'Only 1' would require statement II to be false, but focusing on one aspect while ignoring the other relevant parts is the textbook definition of Beck's selective abstraction.", "b": "'Only 2' holds only if statement I is read literally as 'cannot draw a conclusion'; its intended meaning is Beck's arbitrary inference, drawing a conclusion without evidence, which is correct.", "d": "'None of these' needs both definitions to be wrong, yet both match Beck's (1967) distortions in substance despite the misprinted labels."}}, {"q": "What is the meaning of ethical standards in research?", "concept": "The answer is b: ethical standards are enforceable rules of conduct, as distinct from aspirational principles. The clearest model is the Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association (2002, amended 2010 and 2016). It has two layers. The five General Principles (beneficence and nonmaleficence, fidelity and responsibility, integrity, justice, and respect for people's rights and dignity) are aspirational: they describe the ideals psychologists aim for and are not themselves grounds for sanctions. The Ethical Standards, in ten sections, are enforceable rules for conduct; breaking one can lead to a complaint, sanctions, or loss of membership or licence. Standard 8 covers research and publication: institutional approval, informed consent, deception and debriefing, humane care of animals, honest reporting of results, plagiarism and authorship credit. In India, the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) play a similar role for biomedical and health research, enforced through institutional ethics committees. Example: Kabir, a doctoral student, collects data without the informed consent his ethics committee required. He has not merely fallen short of an ideal; he has broken an enforceable standard, and the committee can act against him. Option b is right because the code itself defines its standards as enforceable rules for conduct. Exam trap: 'all of the above' is tempting because standards do shape procedure and constrain researchers. But a 'choice of rules' is the opposite of enforceable, and a procedural guide describes how to run a study, not the obligations that bind the researcher. Remember: principles aspire, standards enforce.", "traps": {"a": "A 'choice of rules' implies the researcher may pick which rules to follow, which contradicts the binding nature of standards; optional ideals are closer to the aspirational General Principles.", "c": "A procedural guide (a manual or protocol) tells you how to carry out the steps of a study; ethical standards are obligations about conduct enforced by ethics committees and professional bodies.", "d": "'All of the above' fails because options a and c do not define ethical standards; only b matches the APA code's own definition."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Classical conditioning requires the learning of new behaviour through association where two stimuli are linked together to produce a new learned response. Reason (R) There are four basic components of a classical conditioning process (unconditioned stimuli, conditioned stimuli, unconditioned response and conditioned response).", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the Reason only lists the parts of classical conditioning; it does not explain why the Assertion is true. Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in his studies of digestion in dogs. A neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus (UCS, food) that naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR, salivation). After several pairings the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces a conditioned response (CR, salivation to the bell). Is A true? Yes: classical conditioning is learning by association, in which two stimuli become linked so that a new learned response occurs to the formerly neutral one. Is R true? Yes: the four basic components are the UCS, UCR, CS and CR. Does R explain A? No. A says what kind of learning classical conditioning is (association through pairing). R is a list of labels. The reason association is needed lies in the contiguity and, as Robert Rescorla (1968) showed, the contingency between CS and UCS: the CS must predict the UCS. Example: Riya's phone buzzes every time her boss sends an urgent message. After weeks the buzz alone makes her stomach tighten. Labelling 'buzz = CS, tension = CR' names the parts; it does not explain the learning. Option b is right because both statements are correct but R merely describes components. Exam trap: in Assertion-Reason items students pick a whenever both statements are true and on the same topic; the real test is whether R answers 'why is A true?' Key note: no key was published for this paper. A setter could treat the four components as explaining how the association forms, making a the key; b is preferred because R describes parts rather than giving a reason.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to explain A; listing the UCS, UCR, CS and CR names the parts of Pavlov's procedure but does not say why classical conditioning depends on association.", "c": "R is not false; Pavlov's paradigm does have exactly these four basic components.", "d": "A is not false; learning through the association of two stimuli is the standard textbook definition of classical conditioning."}}, {"q": "Personality is-", "concept": "The answer is c: personality evolves over time, and its basic shape is mostly in place by late adolescence, though it keeps maturing afterwards. Personality is the relatively enduring pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving that marks a person out. Gordon Allport (1937) called it a 'dynamic organisation': stable enough to be recognisable, yet able to change. Infant temperament, described by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1977), is the raw material; through childhood and adolescence, temperament, experience and identity work combine into adult traits. By late adolescence traits are fairly stable, which is why DSM-5-TR (2022) traces the onset of personality disorders to adolescence or early adulthood and urges caution before diagnosing them in children. Longitudinal studies reviewed by Brent Roberts and Wendy DelVecchio (2000) show that stability keeps rising with age, while average levels still shift: conscientiousness and agreeableness rise and neuroticism falls into middle age (the maturity principle). Example: Kabir was a shy, cautious child; at 18 he is a reserved, careful student; at 35 he is still reserved but calmer and more organised. His core pattern was set by late adolescence, yet it kept maturing. Option c is right because it captures both continuity and change, with most of the formation done by late adolescence. Exam trap: 'rigid and fixed' and 'everchanging' are the two extremes examiners use to catch students; neither fits the evidence. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Option d ('develops till adulthood') is close, and many texts say personality consolidates in early adulthood; c is preferred because it adds continued evolution while placing most development by late adolescence.", "traps": {"a": "An 'everchanging dimension' overstates change; trait research shows substantial rank-order stability, so personality is not in constant flux.", "b": "'Rigid and fixed' overstates stability; even the 'set like plaster by 30' view of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1994) allows development before it, and later work shows traits still shift in adulthood.", "d": "'Develops till adulthood' is close but implies development simply stops at adulthood; the better answer stresses gradual evolution, with most formation complete by late adolescence and smaller changes later."}}, {"q": "Which of the following 'symbol' is not a category of the shading determinant response in the comprehensive scoring system (CSS) of Rorschach Inkblot Method?", "concept": "The answer is b: FD (form dimension) is not a shading determinant; it codes depth seen from form and perspective alone, while FT, YF and FV all involve shading. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) for the Rorschach Inkblot Method, determinants code which feature of the blot made the person see what they saw: form (F), movement (M, FM, m), chromatic colour (C, CF, FC), achromatic colour (C', C'F, FC'), shading, form dimension (FD), and pairs and reflections. Shading has three subcategories. Texture (T, TF, FT) is shading felt as a tactile surface, like fur or silk. Vista (V, VF, FV) is shading seen as depth or distance. Diffuse shading (Y, YF, FY) is shading used in a vague, general way, like smoke or fog. The letter order shows whether form dominates: FT means form is primary and texture secondary, TF the reverse, and pure T has no form. Form dimension also codes depth, but the depth comes from size or perspective cues in the outline, with no use of shading. Example: on Card VI, Meera says, 'an animal skin, it looks furry here' (FT, texture). On Card IV, Kabir says, 'a giant seen from below; the feet are huge and the head is tiny because it is far away' (FD). Option b is right because FD is a separate determinant category coded without shading. Exam trap: vista and form dimension both code depth, so students mix them; vista needs shading, FD does not. Texture is linked to needs for closeness, vista to painful self-inspection, and diffuse shading to situational stress.", "traps": {"a": "FT is form-texture, a texture subtype of shading in which the shading is felt as a tactile surface and form is primary.", "c": "YF is shading-form, a diffuse shading response in which vague light and dark features dominate and form is secondary.", "d": "FV is form-vista, a vista shading response in which shading is seen as depth or distance, with form primary."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the level of cognition is not suggested by Albert Ellis?", "concept": "The answer is c: schemas are not one of the levels of cognition in Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy; schema is a term of Aaron Beck and Jeffrey Young. Albert Ellis (1962) built REBT on the ABC model: an Activating event, Beliefs about it, and emotional and behavioural Consequences. REBT separates kinds of cognition at point B. Inferences are 'cold' interpretations or hunches about what is happening, which may be true or false ('my friend ignored me on purpose'). Evaluations are 'hot' appraisals of what an event means for the person; these are the rational or irrational beliefs that produce emotion. At the root of irrational evaluations are demands: rigid musts, shoulds and oughts, which Ellis called musturbation. From demands flow awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global self-rating. REBT therapists spend little time disputing inferences and move quickly to the demands. Schemas, by contrast, come from the cognitive model of Aaron Beck (1967), in which early experience forms deep schemas or core beliefs that generate dysfunctional assumptions and automatic thoughts; Jeffrey Young (1990) extended this into schema therapy. Example: Arjun's manager criticises his report. He infers, 'She thinks I'm incompetent' (inference), then evaluates, 'I must never be criticised, and it is awful that I was' (demand and evaluation). An REBT therapist targets the must; a Beckian therapist might trace a defectiveness schema. Option c is right because evaluations, inferences and demands are Ellis's terms, while schemas belong to Beck and Young. Exam trap: Ellis and Beck are both cognitive pioneers, so examiners mix their vocabularies. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A student could argue d, since demands are a kind of irrational evaluation rather than a separate level; but demands are central to Ellis's theory, whereas schema is not his term, so c is the clearer odd one out.", "traps": {"a": "Evaluations are Ellis's 'hot' cognitions, the rational and irrational beliefs at point B of the ABC model, so they are an REBT level.", "b": "Inferences are Ellis's 'cold' cognitions, interpretations of what happened, which REBT treats as distinct from evaluations.", "d": "Demands (musts, shoulds, oughts) are, in Ellis's theory, the core irrational belief from which awfulising, low frustration tolerance and self-downing derive."}}, {"q": "Which of the following correctly explains the process of learned helplessness?", "concept": "The answer is c: learned helplessness runs from uncontrollable bad events, to a perceived lack of control, to generalised helpless behaviour. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs electric shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group the same shocks with no way to stop them, and a third group no shocks. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock, most yoked dogs lay down and whimpered instead of escaping, while the other dogs learned quickly. Both shocked groups had received the same amount of shock, so it was the lack of control, not the shock, that produced the passivity. David Myers (2010) summarises the chain in a flow diagram: uncontrollable bad events lead to perceived lack of control, which leads to generalised helpless behaviour. The middle step is cognitive: the animal or person learns that responses and outcomes are independent. Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) later added attributions: helplessness spreads most when the cause is seen as internal, stable and global, and this became a model of depression. Example: Meera fails three maths tests despite studying hard (uncontrollable bad events), decides that nothing she does affects her marks (perceived lack of control), and stops trying in every subject, even ones she likes (generalised helpless behaviour). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that puts the cause first, the perception in the middle and the behaviour last. Exam trap: options a and b reorder the same three phrases; the behaviour is the outcome and cannot come first. 'All of the above' is impossible when the options contradict each other.", "traps": {"a": "This puts the outcome, generalised helpless behaviour, first; in Seligman's model the behaviour is the end result of the uncontrollable events and the perceived lack of control.", "b": "This places helpless behaviour before the perception of no control, skipping the cognitive step that, in Seligman and Maier's account, links the events to the passivity.", "d": "'All of the above' cannot be correct because the three sequences contradict each other; only one causal order is right."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a parametric correlation?", "concept": "The answer is a: the product moment correlation (Pearson's r) is the parametric correlation. A parametric statistic estimates a population parameter and rests on assumptions about the population: both variables measured on an interval or ratio scale, a roughly normal distribution, a linear relationship and homoscedasticity (equal spread of scores along the line). Karl Pearson (1896) built the product moment coefficient on these assumptions; it uses the actual scores, multiplying each person's deviations from the two means (the 'products of moments'). A non-parametric (distribution-free) coefficient makes no such assumptions and usually works on ranks. Charles Spearman's (1904) rank difference method, rho, turns scores into ranks and uses the differences between ranks, so it suits ordinal data or badly skewed scores. Example: Riya correlates the exact marks of 50 students in statistics and in research methods; the marks are interval level and normally distributed, so she uses Pearson's r. If her teacher had only ranked the students from 1 to 50, she would use Spearman's rho. Option a is right because the product moment r is the classic parametric coefficient, the one every text names when it contrasts parametric with non-parametric correlation. Exam trap: examiners pair 'product moment' with 'rank difference' and hope students mix them up; rank difference is Spearman's non-parametric method. Point biserial and partial correlation are offshoots of Pearson's r built for special cases (a dichotomous variable, or a third variable held constant), not the basic parametric correlation. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Point biserial r is algebraically a Pearson r with one variable scored 0 or 1, and partial r is computed from Pearson coefficients, so both share parametric assumptions; the setter's intended contrast is product moment versus rank difference, and product moment is the parent coefficient, so a is the best pick.", "traps": {"b": "Rank difference is Charles Spearman's (1904) rho, computed from differences between ranks; it is the standard non-parametric correlation for ordinal data, the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "Point biserial r links a true dichotomy (pass or fail) with a continuous variable; it is a special case of Pearson's r for one data type, not the general parametric correlation the setter means.", "d": "Partial correlation measures the link between two variables with a third (say age) held constant; it is derived from Pearson coefficients and is a way of controlling variables, not the basic parametric coefficient."}}, {"q": "________ is the primary neurotransmitter transferred secreted by efferent axons of the central nervous system.", "concept": "The answer is b: acetylcholine is the main neurotransmitter released by the efferent (outgoing) axons of the central nervous system. Efferent neurons carry commands out of the brain and spinal cord. Every somatic motor neuron in the spinal cord and brainstem releases acetylcholine (ACh) onto skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction, where it binds nicotinic receptors and makes the muscle contract. The preganglionic neurons of both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic systems also release ACh, so nearly all output leaving the central nervous system begins at an ACh synapse. Otto Loewi (1921) showed that nerves signal chemically: stimulating a frog heart's vagus nerve released a substance ('Vagusstoff') that slowed a second heart bathed in the same fluid. Henry Dale (1914) had already described the actions of acetylcholine, and the two shared the 1936 Nobel Prize. In the synapse ACh is broken down by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase. Example: when Arjun decides to kick a football, motor neurons in his spinal cord fire and release ACh onto his leg muscles, which contract. Option b is right because acetylcholine is the transmitter itself, released at motor and preganglionic autonomic synapses. Exam trap: the other options are substances that act on the acetylcholine synapse (botulinum toxin blocks its release; curare blocks its receptor) or an adjective describing dopamine-using neurons, so they sound related but none is a transmitter secreted by efferent axons. Key note: 'transferred secreted' in the stem is a printing slip for 'secreted'; the meaning is unchanged.", "traps": {"a": "Botulinum toxin, from the bacterium Clostridium botulinum, stops the release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction and causes paralysis (Botox uses tiny doses); it is a toxin acting on the synapse, not a transmitter.", "c": "Dopaminergic is an adjective for neurons that use dopamine, as in the nigrostriatal and mesolimbic pathways; dopamine is a brain modulator, not the transmitter of efferent motor output, and the word is not even a transmitter's name.", "d": "Curare, a South American arrow poison, blocks nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on muscle and paralyses it; it is an acetylcholine antagonist, not a transmitter."}}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. Specific phobia is fear of objects II. Claustrophobia is fear of being in small place. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "concept": "The answer is c: both statements are correct. A specific phobia is a marked, persistent and excessive fear of a particular object or situation, out of proportion to the real danger, that leads to avoidance or intense distress and, in DSM-5-TR (2022), lasts 6 months or more. Statement I is true: the fear is tied to specific objects (dogs, spiders, needles), though the full definition also covers situations. DSM-5-TR lists five types: animal, natural environment (heights, water, storms), blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, planes, enclosed places) and other. Statement II is also true: claustrophobia (from Latin claustrum, a closed place) is fear of small or enclosed spaces such as lifts, tunnels or MRI scanners, and it belongs to the situational type. The person usually fears being trapped or unable to breathe. Example: Meera climbs ten floors to her office every day because the thought of being shut inside the lift makes her heart race; this is claustrophobia, a situational specific phobia. Phobias are often explained by conditioning. John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear of a white rat in Little Albert, and Orval Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory adds that avoidance is negatively reinforced because it lowers anxiety, which keeps the phobia alive. Martin Seligman (1971) added preparedness: we learn to fear snakes and heights more readily than cars. Option c is right because statement I gives the core of specific phobia and statement II gives the correct meaning of claustrophobia. Exam trap: students confuse claustrophobia with agoraphobia, the fear of open or public places where escape may be hard (a separate DSM-5-TR disorder), or reject statement I because it leaves out situations; an incomplete definition is not a false one.", "traps": {"a": "Only I would mean claustrophobia is not fear of small spaces, but claustrophobia is exactly the fear of enclosed places, so statement II cannot be rejected.", "b": "Only II would mean specific phobia is not fear of objects, yet DSM-5-TR defines specific phobia as fear of a specific object or situation, so statement I is true.", "d": "None of these would need both statements to be false, but both match the standard DSM-5-TR descriptions."}}, {"q": "Some psychologist stated that the relationship between intelligence and creativity is not the same throughout the range of intelligence (IQ). Which of the following is appropriate explanation of relationship between these at different levels of IQ?", "concept": "The answer is d: below a critical IQ the relationship between intelligence and creativity is positive, and above it the relationship is about zero. This is the threshold theory (threshold hypothesis). Creativity, in the terms of Joy Paul Guilford (1950), rests on divergent thinking: producing many, varied and original ideas (fluency, flexibility, originality, elaboration). Intelligence tests mostly reward convergent thinking towards one correct answer. Ellis Paul Torrance (1962) and Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found that creativity and IQ correlate positively up to an IQ of about 120. Above that level more IQ adds little or nothing, and creativity depends on motivation, openness to experience and other personality factors. So a minimum level of intelligence is necessary but not sufficient for creativity. Emanuel Jauk and colleagues (2013) later supported a threshold but showed that its height depends on how creativity is scored. Example: Kabir (IQ 95) and Ishaan (IQ 115) differ in creativity much as they differ in IQ, but between Ananya (IQ 130) and Rohan (IQ 150) IQ no longer predicts who writes the more original story. Option d is right because it states both halves of the threshold theory: a positive correlation below the critical level and a zero correlation above it. Exam trap: option c also begins with 'positive', so students who remember only the first half pick it; the threshold theory says the link fades to zero above about 120, it does not turn negative. Option a simply reverses the two halves.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the threshold theory: it puts the zero correlation at low IQ and the positive one at high IQ, whereas the data show the opposite.", "b": "A negative link below the threshold would mean less intelligent people are more creative; no study shows this, and the threshold idea is about a positive link that levels off.", "c": "This gets the lower range right but claims creativity falls as IQ rises past the threshold; the classic finding (Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson, 1962) is that the correlation becomes near zero, not negative."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Many eccentric characters are charming and emotionally stable. Reason (R) Strictly following social norms are guarantee of mental health.", "concept": "The answer is c: the assertion is true, but the reason is false. This item tests how abnormality is defined. One definition calls behaviour abnormal when it breaks social norms; another uses statistical rarity. Both fail as complete definitions, because being unusual is not the same as being disordered, and conformity is no proof of health. The assertion (A) is true. David Weeks (1995) studied about 1,000 eccentric people in Britain and the United States and found them, on average, happier and healthier than most people, with fewer visits to the doctor; few had any mental disorder. Clinicians therefore look for distress, dysfunction and danger, not mere difference. The reason (R) is false. Strict conformity does not guarantee mental health. A person can follow every rule and still have obsessive-compulsive disorder or depression, and norms themselves change across cultures and eras (homosexuality was listed in the DSM until 1973). Marie Jahoda (1958) defined positive mental health by self-acceptance, growth, autonomy, accurate perception of reality and environmental mastery, not by conformity. Example: Devika, a retired teacher, wears bright mismatched clothes, keeps twenty parrots and chats with them for hours, yet she has close friends, sleeps well and enjoys life; she is eccentric, not disordered. Option c is right because A matches the evidence on eccentrics, while R makes an absolute claim ('guarantee') that every modern definition of abnormality rejects. Because R is false, it cannot explain A; in fact R would contradict A. Exam trap: students accept R because the social norms definition sounds familiar, missing that the word 'guarantee' turns one partial criterion into a false claim.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to be true and to explain A, but strict conformity guarantees nothing, and the healthy eccentrics in A break norms, so R would contradict A rather than explain it.", "b": "This also needs R to be true; the claim that following social norms guarantees mental health is false, because conformists can be disordered and norms differ across cultures.", "d": "A is not false: research on eccentrics (David Weeks, 1995) shows many are well adjusted, and R is the false half, so this reverses the truth values."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are true about adherence? 1. The patient must understand the treatment regimen 2. The practitioner must be intelligent 3. The patient must be satisfied with the relationship and treatment regimen 4. The patient must decide to adhere", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 1, 3 and 4 are true; adherence needs a patient who understands the regimen, is satisfied and decides to adhere, while the practitioner's intelligence is not a condition. Adherence (the older term is compliance) is the extent to which a patient's behaviour, such as taking medicine, following a diet or attending check-ups, matches the agreed treatment advice. Roughly half of patients with chronic illness do not adhere fully, which makes it a core topic in health psychology. Philip Ley's (1988) cognitive hypothesis model holds that adherence is predicted by the patient's understanding of the information, memory for it and satisfaction with the consultation. M. Robin DiMatteo (2004) and colleagues put the same ideas as information, motivation and strategy: the patient must know what to do, want to do it and be able to do it. Satisfaction grows from a warm relationship, clear communication and a regimen that fits daily life. Finally the patient must intend, or decide, to follow it; much nonadherence is intentional, such as stopping a drug because of side effects. Example: Sameer, newly diagnosed with diabetes, follows his insulin plan because his doctor explained it in simple Hindi (understanding), listened to his worries (satisfaction) and agreed the plan with him, so he chose to stick to it (decision). Option c is right because statements 1, 3 and 4 are the patient-side requirements named in adherence models. Exam trap: statement 2 sounds sensible, but what predicts adherence is the practitioner's communication, warmth and clarity, not intelligence; a brilliant doctor who speaks in jargon still gets poor adherence.", "traps": {"a": "This includes statement 2, but practitioner intelligence appears in no adherence model; Philip Ley's model stresses the practitioner's communication, not intellect, and this option also drops satisfaction and decision.", "b": "Satisfaction and the decision to adhere are true, but this leaves out understanding, which Philip Ley's model treats as the first requirement; a patient cannot follow a regimen he does not understand.", "d": "Deciding to adhere is necessary but not sufficient; without understanding and satisfaction the decision is not formed or not kept, so 'only 4' is incomplete."}}, {"q": "The scale, where absolute zero point is known, is popularly known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the ratio scale is the scale with a known, true (absolute) zero point. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names or classifies (blood group, roll numbers). An ordinal scale adds order (class ranks, socioeconomic class), but the gaps between ranks need not be equal. An interval scale adds equal units, so differences are meaningful, but its zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius does not mean no heat, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds an absolute zero, meaning a complete absence of the property, so ratios become meaningful: 0 kg means no weight, and 80 kg is twice 40 kg. Reaction time, height, weight, age and number of errors are ratio scales. Every statistic is allowed on ratio data, including the geometric mean and the coefficient of variation. Example: Neha measures reaction time in milliseconds; 600 ms really is twice 300 ms, because 0 ms is a real zero, so she has ratio data. Option b is right because a known absolute zero is the defining feature that separates the ratio scale from all the other levels. Exam trap: the interval scale is the near miss, since it also has equal units; students forget that its zero is arbitrary. Psychological test scores such as IQ are treated as interval, because an IQ of 0 does not mean zero intelligence, so an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70.", "traps": {"a": "The ordinal scale only ranks (first, second, third); its intervals are unequal and it has no meaningful zero, so ratios mean nothing.", "c": "The interval scale has equal units but an arbitrary zero, as with Celsius temperature or IQ scores; because its zero is not absolute, it is the classic near miss for this stem.", "d": "The nominal scale only labels categories such as gender or diagnosis; it has no order, no equal units and no zero point."}}, {"q": "Which of these following occur in trace and delayed conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is a: in trace conditioning the CS comes on and goes off before the US appears, and in delayed conditioning the CS comes on first and is still present when the US arrives. These are timing arrangements in classical conditioning, first studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927). A conditioned stimulus (CS, such as a tone) is paired with an unconditioned stimulus (US, such as food) that naturally produces a response (salivation). Four arrangements are taught. In delayed (delay) conditioning the CS starts first and overlaps the US; it is the most effective arrangement, because the CS acts as a clear warning signal. In trace conditioning the CS ends before the US begins, leaving a gap (the trace interval), so the learner must hold a memory 'trace' of the CS; learning is weaker and gets worse as the gap grows. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and US start together, giving no warning, so learning is weak. In backward conditioning the US comes first and the CS follows; it is the least effective and may even teach that the CS signals safety. Example: in an eyeblink study, a tone keeps sounding while a puff of air hits Kabir's eye (delay); for Riya the tone stops half a second before the puff (trace). Kabir learns to blink to the tone faster. Option a is right because its first half describes trace conditioning and its second half describes delayed conditioning, in the order the stem asks. Exam trap: 'delay' does not mean the CS comes late or the response is slow; it means the US is delayed while the CS stays on. Examiners also slip in the backward (US first) and simultaneous (together) descriptions.", "traps": {"b": "Its first half is backward conditioning (US first, then CS), the weakest arrangement, and its second half is trace conditioning; delayed conditioning is missing and the order is wrong.", "c": "Its first half is simultaneous conditioning (CS and US together) and its second half is delayed conditioning; trace conditioning, with its gap between CS and US, is missing.", "d": "Its first half correctly describes trace conditioning, but its second half describes backward conditioning (US before CS), not delayed conditioning."}}, {"q": "The pragmatic paradigm proposes that", "concept": "The answer is c: the pragmatic paradigm says the researcher should choose an approach based on what the research question demands. A paradigm is a set of beliefs about reality (ontology), knowledge (epistemology) and method that guides research, a term made famous by Thomas Kuhn (1962). Positivism holds that one objective reality exists and that the researcher studies it from a detached, value-free position, usually with experiments and statistics. Constructivism (interpretivism) holds that reality is built by people, so the researcher works subjectively and closely with participants, usually through interviews and qualitative analysis. Pragmatism comes from the philosophy of Charles Sanders Peirce (1878), William James (1907) and John Dewey (1938), which judges ideas by their practical consequences: what works. In research methods, Abbas Tashakkori and Charles Teddlie (1998) and John Creswell (2003) made it the philosophical basis of mixed methods. The pragmatist sets aside the objective versus subjective debate and lets the problem decide: a survey where numbers are needed, interviews where meanings are needed, or both. Example: Priya wants to know how many college students in Pune use counselling services and why others avoid them; she runs a questionnaire for the first part and focus groups for the second, because each question needs a different tool. Option c is right because 'an approach based on research demands' is the defining feature of pragmatism: the question comes first and the method follows. Exam trap: options a and b are the stances of positivism (objective) and constructivism (subjective); pragmatism is defined by refusing to be locked into either, so it can use both.", "traps": {"a": "An objective, detached stance defines positivism and postpositivism, the tradition of Auguste Comte (1844) and the basis of quantitative research; pragmatism does not commit to objectivity in advance.", "b": "A subjective stance, with the researcher co-constructing meaning with participants, defines constructivism or interpretivism, the basis of qualitative research, not pragmatism.", "d": "Option c is a correct description of pragmatism, so 'none of above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "The hypothesis that a match between one's needs and what one receives from others in one's social network is known as the?", "concept": "The answer is b: the matching hypothesis states that social support helps most when what a person receives from the social network matches what that person needs. Social support is information from others that one is loved, cared for, valued and part of a network of mutual obligation. It comes in several forms: emotional (warmth, listening), tangible or instrumental (money, transport, meals), informational (advice) and esteem or appraisal support. Sheldon Cohen and Garth McKay (1984) proposed that support buffers stress only when it fits the demands of the stressor, and Carolyn Cutrona and Daniel Russell (1990) developed this into optimal matching theory: uncontrollable stressors such as a death call mainly for emotional support, while controllable ones such as losing a job call for practical help and advice. Shelley Taylor (2018), in her health psychology text, calls this the matching hypothesis. Mismatched support can fail or even backfire: advice offered to someone who needs comfort can feel like criticism. Example: when Ananya's father is admitted to hospital, she needs someone to drive her there and look after her younger brother. Friends who only send motivational messages leave her stressed, while a neighbour who gives her a lift and cooks dinner matches her need and truly lowers her distress. Option b is right because the 'match' between needs and the support received is the exact definition of the matching hypothesis. Exam trap: the same name is used in social psychology for the matching hypothesis of attraction of Elaine Walster (1966), which says we choose partners of similar attractiveness; the stem's mention of needs and the social network shows the health psychology meaning is intended.", "traps": {"a": "'Matching social context hypothesis' is not a recognised term; it borrows the word 'matching' to look like the right answer.", "c": "Person-environment fit theory (John French, Robert Caplan and Van Harrison, 1982) explains work stress as a mismatch between a person's abilities or needs and the demands or supplies of the job; it concerns the whole environment, not support received from one's social network.", "d": "'Need hypothesis' is not a named theory of social support; needs feature in Henry Murray's (1938) need-press theory and Abraham Maslow's (1943) hierarchy, neither of which is about matching support to needs."}}, {"q": "John feels as though he is not living up to expectations of his parents. How would he be described by Adler:", "concept": "The answer is c: Alfred Adler would describe John as having an inferiority complex. Adler (1907) began with 'organ inferiority', the idea that people compensate for weak body parts, and widened it into a general theory. Every child starts small, weak and dependent, so feelings of inferiority are universal and normal. They are the engine of growth, because they drive a striving for superiority (later he said perfection or completion) through compensation. When these feelings become exaggerated and overwhelming, so that the person feels helpless and unable to meet life's tasks, Adler called it an inferiority complex. He traced it to three childhood conditions: organ inferiority, pampering and neglect. Some people mask it with a superiority complex, an inflated show of boasting and arrogance. In Understanding Human Nature (1927) he also stressed that each person's fictional final goal and style of life shape how these feelings are handled. Example: Riya's parents expect top marks and a medical seat; she feels she can never measure up, compares herself unfavourably with her cousins and stops trying. In Adlerian terms, her normal feelings of inferiority have grown into an inferiority complex, exactly like John in the stem. Option c is right because a persistent sense of failing to meet others' standards is the textbook picture of the inferiority complex, Adler's best-known concept. Exam trap: the distractors swap in humanistic growth terms. Self-actualisation belongs to Abraham Maslow (1943) and Carl Rogers (1961), and self-realisation to Carl Jung's (1921) individuation and Karen Horney's (1950) real self, not to Adler.", "traps": {"a": "Self-realisation belongs to Carl Jung's (1921) goal of individuation and to Karen Horney's (1950) realisation of the real self; Adler did not describe people as having 'low self-realisation'.", "b": "'Adlerian complex' is not a term Adler or anyone else used; it is a made-up option that echoes his name. The complex he named is the inferiority complex.", "d": "Actualisation is the humanistic idea of fulfilling one's potential, from Kurt Goldstein (1939), Abraham Maslow (1943) and Carl Rogers; it is not Adler's account of someone failing to meet parental expectations."}}, {"q": "Lesions to the language dominant thalamus can result in ________.", "concept": "The answer is d: a lesion of the language-dominant (usually left) thalamus can produce a transient aphasia syndrome, known as thalamic aphasia. Aphasia is a loss or disturbance of language caused by brain damage. Most aphasias follow damage to the cortex of the dominant hemisphere, such as Broca's area, described by Paul Broca (1861), or Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874). But subcortical structures also take part in language. The thalamus is a relay and integration centre in the diencephalon, and its pulvinar, ventral anterior and mediodorsal nuclei are wired to the language cortex. Bruce Crosson (1984) summarised the classic picture after left thalamic stroke or haemorrhage: fluent but reduced speech, word-finding difficulty, frequent semantic paraphasias (wrong words) and relatively spared repetition and comprehension. It is usually mild and often clears within days to weeks, which is why it is called transient; the lesion is thought to disrupt the connected cortex only temporarily. Example: after a small left thalamic stroke, Ramesh, aged 62, keeps calling a pen a 'pencil' and struggles to name objects, yet repeats sentences well; within three weeks his speech is almost normal. Option d is right because 'language-dominant' points to a language disorder, and aphasia from dominant thalamic lesions is typically transient. Exam trap: anosognosia, unawareness of one's own deficit, follows damage to the non-dominant (right) hemisphere, especially the right parietal lobe, the reverse side. Key note: no key was published for this paper, and 'Asphasia' is a misprint for aphasia. Thalamic lesions can also cause anterograde amnesia, as in Korsakoff syndrome, but that needs mediodorsal or anterior damage, often on both sides; the stem's stress on language dominance favours aphasia.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer (1906), is a progressive dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles that begins in the medial temporal lobe; it is not caused by a focal thalamic lesion.", "b": "Anterograde amnesia does follow some thalamic damage (mediodorsal and anterior nuclei, as in the Korsakoff syndrome of Sergei Korsakoff, 1887), but it is a memory disorder not tied to the language-dominant side, so it fits the stem less well than aphasia.", "c": "Anosognosia, named by Joseph Babinski (1914), is unawareness of one's own illness or paralysis; it is linked to non-dominant (right) hemisphere damage, the opposite side from the stem."}}, {"q": "This is amongst the 16 source traits concluded by Cattell in his personality theory:", "concept": "The answer is a: privateness is one of Raymond Cattell's 16 source traits (Factor N). Cattell (1946) built a trait theory by factor analysis. Surface traits are clusters of behaviour that appear together on the surface; source traits are the deeper underlying factors that produce them. Starting from the list of about 4,500 trait words drawn up by Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), Cattell factor-analysed L-data (life records), Q-data (questionnaires) and T-data (objective tests) and arrived at 16 primary source traits, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). In the fifth edition (1993) the factors are warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, liveliness, rule-consciousness, social boldness, sensitivity, vigilance, abstractedness, privateness, apprehension, openness to change, self-reliance, perfectionism and tension. Privateness runs from forthright, open and genuine at the low end to discreet, guarded and non-disclosing at the high end. Example: Arjun scores high on privateness; he is polite with colleagues but rarely shares personal details and chooses his words carefully, while his friend Vikram, a low scorer, tells everyone everything. Option a is right because privateness is the exact name of Factor N in the current 16PF list. Exam trap: love and hope sound like personality qualities, but they are character strengths in the classification of Christopher Peterson and Martin Seligman (2004), not 16PF factors. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Factor B was called 'intelligence' in older 16PF versions and in several textbooks, and Cattell counted intelligence as an ability source trait, so d can be defended; but the fifth edition, the one that uses the label 'privateness', names Factor B 'reasoning', so a setter who knew 'privateness' most likely keyed a.", "traps": {"b": "Love is an emotion and, in positive psychology, a character strength (capacity to love and be loved) in the list of Christopher Peterson and Martin Seligman (2004); it is not one of Cattell's factors (the nearest is warmth, Factor A).", "c": "Hope is a positive psychology construct (the hope theory of Charles Richard Snyder, 1991: agency plus pathways) and a character strength, not a 16PF factor.", "d": "Intelligence was the old label of Factor B, but the current 16PF calls it reasoning, and an option list that includes 'privateness' follows the current names; d is the second-best answer (see Key note)."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) The thoughts and action of the individual reflect his/her wants and goals. Reason (R) Behaviour is solely determined by motivational dynamics.", "concept": "The answer is c: the assertion is true, but the reason is false. Motivation is the set of processes that start, direct and sustain goal-directed behaviour. The assertion (A) states a core idea of motivational psychology: what a person thinks about and does largely reflects their needs, wants and goals. Henry Murray (1938) listed psychological needs such as achievement and affiliation that show up in fantasy and action (hence the Thematic Apperception Test), Abraham Maslow (1943) ranked needs from physiological to self-actualisation, and Edwin Locke (1968) showed in goal-setting theory that specific goals direct attention and effort. So A is true. The reason (R) is false because of the word 'solely'. Behaviour is multiply determined: motivation interacts with ability, habit and learning, emotion, biology, personality and the situation. Kurt Lewin (1936) summed this up as B = f(P, E): behaviour is a function of the person and the environment. A highly motivated student without ability or opportunity will not succeed, and much behaviour runs on habit or reflex with little motive at all. Example: Tanvi badly wants to become a clinical psychologist, so she reads about therapy and joins a counselling club (A in action); yet her entrance score also depends on her preparation, test anxiety and the paper's difficulty, not on motivation alone. Option c is right because A is a sound statement about motivation while R overstates it; since R is false, it cannot explain A. Exam trap: absolute words such as 'solely', 'only', 'always' and 'guarantee' in a reason are a strong sign that it is false, even when the idea behind it is familiar.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to be true; behaviour is shaped by ability, learning, situation and biology as well as motives, so 'solely determined by motivational dynamics' is false and cannot explain A.", "b": "This also treats R as true; the word 'solely' makes R false, since Kurt Lewin's (1936) B = f(P, E) and expectancy-value models show behaviour has many causes.", "d": "A is not false: needs, wants and goals do shape thought and action, as Henry Murray's (1938) needs and Edwin Locke's (1968) goal-setting theory show; it is R that is false."}}, {"q": "Raj wakes up in an unknown hotel room. He does not know where he is or how he got there and not sure which day it is. This is most likely due to dissociative", "concept": "The answer is c: waking in a strange place with no idea how one got there points to a dissociative fugue. Dissociation is a break in the normal integration of memory, identity, consciousness and perception. In dissociative amnesia the person cannot recall important personal information, usually about a traumatic or stressful event, far beyond ordinary forgetting. A fugue (Latin fuga, flight) adds sudden, unexpected travel or bewildered wandering away from home or work, with amnesia for the past and sometimes confusion about identity or a new identity. Fugues last from hours to months and often follow severe stress such as war, disaster or a personal crisis. During the fugue the person may seem normal to others; when it ends, they 'come to' in an unfamiliar place, confused and frightened, with no memory of the journey. DSM-IV-TR (2000) listed dissociative fugue as a separate disorder; DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022) made it a specifier: dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue. Example: after a heavy business loss, Sunil walks out of his office in Mumbai, and two days later he finds himself in a hotel room in Goa, unsure of the date and with no memory of the trip; that is the end of a fugue. Option c is right because the key clue is the unexplained journey: Raj is in an unknown hotel and does not know how he got there, so there was travel he cannot recall. Exam trap: the vignette never says 'travelled', so students pick plain amnesia; the strange location is the travel clue that separates fugue from simple amnesia. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Under DSM-5-TR this would be coded as dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, so b is defensible; but among these options, fugue names the specific picture the stem describes.", "traps": {"a": "'Dissociative personality' points to dissociative identity disorder (formerly multiple personality disorder), with two or more distinct identities and memory gaps; the stem shows no alternate identities.", "b": "Dissociative amnesia is memory loss for personal information without the travel; it is the parent diagnosis in DSM-5-TR, but Raj's unexplained arrival in a strange hotel points to the fugue form.", "d": "Dissociative trance (trance disorder in ICD-11, an other specified dissociative disorder in DSM-5-TR) is a narrowing of awareness and unresponsiveness to surroundings, often with stereotyped movements; it does not involve waking in an unknown place with no memory of the journey."}}, {"q": "Health promoting behaviours are also known as?", "concept": "The answer is c: health-promoting behaviours are also called behavioural immunogens. The term comes from Joseph D. Matarazzo (1984), the psychologist who had earlier defined health psychology. He borrowed his words from immunology. A biological pathogen (a virus or bacterium) causes disease, while an immunogen is something that triggers protective immunity. Matarazzo argued that behaviour works the same way. Behavioural pathogens are habits that raise the risk of illness: smoking, heavy drinking, a high-fat diet, unprotected sex and inactivity. Behavioural immunogens are habits that protect health: regular exercise, enough sleep, wearing a seatbelt, eating fruit and vegetables, brushing teeth and going for screening tests. The Alameda County study by Nedra Belloc and Lester Breslow (1972) showed why this matters. Adults who kept more of seven simple habits (seven to eight hours of sleep, breakfast, no snacking between meals, a healthy weight, regular exercise, little or no alcohol, no smoking) were healthier, and later follow-ups found they also lived longer. For example, Arjun stops smoking and starts a daily brisk walk. He has dropped a behavioural pathogen and added a behavioural immunogen. Health psychologists such as Shelley Taylor (1986) also call these health behaviours or health habits, and they try to build them early, because habits formed in adolescence tend to last. Option c is right because \"behavioural immunogen\" is the exact technical label Matarazzo gave to health-promoting behaviours, in contrast to health-damaging behavioural pathogens. Exam trap: examiners place \"behavioural pathogens\" first because it sounds similar and comes from the same paper, but it means the opposite (health-damaging habits). A student who remembers only that Matarazzo joined behaviour with an immunology word can easily pick the wrong half of the pair.", "traps": {"a": "Behavioural pathogens are Matarazzo's (1984) term for the opposite group: health-damaging habits such as smoking and heavy drinking. They raise disease risk, so they cannot be another name for health-promoting behaviours.", "b": "Antigens are foreign molecules (on viruses, bacteria or pollen) that trigger the body's immune response and the making of antibodies. They are a biological concept, not a class of behaviour.", "d": "Behaviour modification is the use of conditioning techniques (reinforcement, shaping, token economies) to change behaviour. It can be used to build health habits, but it is a method of change, not a name for the healthy behaviours themselves."}}, {"q": "The three important components of any paradigm are", "concept": "The answer is b: the three core components of any research paradigm are epistemology, ontology and methodology. A paradigm is a set of basic beliefs, a worldview, that guides how research is done. Thomas Kuhn (1962) made the word popular in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, but the three-part definition used in research methods comes from Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994). They said every paradigm answers three linked questions. Ontology asks what the nature of reality is: is there one objective reality (realism) or many realities constructed by people (relativism)? Epistemology asks what the relationship is between the knower and what can be known: can the researcher stand apart and be objective, or is knowledge created between researcher and participant? Methodology asks how the inquirer should go about finding things out: by controlled experiments and measurement, or by dialogue and interpretation. The answers flow in order. Your ontology limits your epistemology, and both shape your methodology. Positivism, for example, assumes one real world, a detached and objective researcher, and experimental, hypothesis-testing methods. Constructivism assumes multiple realities, knowledge built jointly with participants, and interpretive, qualitative methods. Suppose Meera studies how mothers in Pune experience a child's autism diagnosis. Her choice of in-depth interviews follows from a constructivist ontology and epistemology. Option b is right because only it lists the three philosophical components that Guba and Lincoln named. Exam trap: data collection methods and theory are distractors. Methods (interviews, questionnaires) are tools chosen within a methodology, not a component of the paradigm, and a theory is a specific explanation tested within a paradigm. Later writers add axiology (the role of values) as a fourth element, but the classic triad remains ontology, epistemology and methodology.", "traps": {"a": "Methodology and ontology are genuine components, but data collection is a set of methods (interviews, tests, observation) chosen once the methodology is fixed. It is a practical step, not one of the three basic beliefs, and epistemology is missing.", "c": "Ontology is correct, but data collection methods are research tools and a theory is a specific explanation tested inside a paradigm. Neither is a philosophical component, and epistemology and methodology are both missing.", "d": "Epistemology and methodology are components, but theory has replaced ontology. A theory is a testable explanation that sits inside a paradigm; ontology (the nature of reality) is the missing first question."}}, {"q": "Which of the following definitions of abnormality is currently through best?", "concept": "The answer is c: harmful dysfunction is the definition of abnormality most widely thought best today. Each older definition captures something but fails on its own. Statistical infrequency calls behaviour abnormal if it is rare, far from the average. But rarity is not disorder: a very high IQ is rare yet healthy, while depression is common yet clearly a disorder. Norm violation calls behaviour abnormal if it breaks social rules. But norms change across cultures and eras (homosexuality was listed as a disorder until 1973), and many rule breakers, such as protesters, are not ill. Jerome Wakefield (1992) proposed a hybrid. A condition is a disorder only when two things are true. First, there is a dysfunction: some internal mechanism (perception, memory, emotion regulation) fails to do the job natural selection designed it for. That part is meant to be a scientific fact. Second, the dysfunction is harmful, judged by the person's cultural values: it causes distress or disability. Because it joins a factual element to a value element, it sorts both rare-but-harmless and common-but-harmful conditions correctly. DSM-5 builds both ideas into its definition of mental disorder: a dysfunction in psychological, biological or developmental processes, usually with significant distress or disability. For example, Kabir's panic attacks come from a fear system firing without real danger (dysfunction) and stop him travelling to college (harm), so he meets both tests. Option c is right because harmful dysfunction repairs the main weaknesses of the purely statistical and purely social definitions. Exam trap: \"none of these\" tempts students who remember that no definition is perfect. Critics such as Scott Lilienfeld and Lori Marino (1995) argue that evolutionary function is hard to establish, yet the stem asks which is currently thought best, and that is harmful dysfunction. Key note: the printed \"currently through best\" is a typo for \"currently thought best\".", "traps": {"a": "Statistical infrequency defines abnormality as rarity, scores far from the mean. It fails because rare traits can be desirable (very high intelligence) and common conditions such as anxiety and depression can be disorders.", "b": "Norm violation defines abnormality as breaking social or cultural rules. It is culture-bound and shifts over time, so it confuses unconventional with disordered, as when homosexuality was labelled a disorder before 1973.", "d": "No definition is flawless, but the stem asks which is currently thought best, and Jerome Wakefield's harmful dysfunction is widely treated as the strongest account, so 'none of these' ignores a defensible answer."}}, {"q": "You are asked by a close friend to give you notes for exam. You refuse to help. Later your friend asks if you would give notes for two subjects. Feeling guilty, you now agree to help. Your behaviour is predicted by the:", "concept": "The answer is d: agreeing to a smaller request after refusing a bigger one is the door-in-the-face effect. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1975) first showed it. They asked university students to counsel juvenile delinquents for two hours a week for at least two years. Almost everyone refused. The same students were then asked to take a group of delinquents to the zoo for one afternoon, and about 50 percent agreed, against roughly 17 percent of students who heard only the zoo request. The technique has two steps: a large request that is almost sure to be refused, then the request the asker really wanted. Two explanations are offered. Reciprocal concessions: the asker seems to have backed down, and the norm of reciprocity makes us feel we should back down too. Guilt and self-presentation: refusing once makes us feel bad or look unhelpful, and saying yes repairs that feeling. In the stem, the friend first asks for exam notes, you refuse, then the friend asks for notes in just two subjects. Feeling guilty, you agree. For instance, Riya refuses to lend Kabir her notes for all five papers, but says yes when he asks for only two. Option d is right because the sequence (large request refused, smaller request accepted, driven by guilt or reciprocity) is exactly door-in-the-face. Exam trap: students mix up door-in-the-face with foot-in-the-door, which runs in the opposite order (a small request accepted first, then a larger one). \"Foot-in-the-face\" is a made-up blend of the two names. Key note: the stem is loosely worded (\"give you notes\" should read \"give them notes\"), and it leaves the size of the first request implied; read sensibly, notes for only two subjects is the smaller second request, which is what makes this door-in-the-face.", "traps": {"a": "There is no 'foot-in-the-face' technique. It is a fake blend of foot-in-the-door (small request, then a larger one) and door-in-the-face (large request, then a smaller one), set as a distractor.", "b": "'High-ball' is not a standard compliance technique in social psychology texts; in sales slang, highballing means quoting an inflated trade-in price to draw a buyer in. It does not describe agreeing to a smaller favour after refusing a larger one.", "c": "The low-ball technique (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1978) gets a person to agree to a deal and then makes the terms worse; they still comply out of commitment. Here no agreement came first; the first answer was a refusal."}}, {"q": "A fraction of a population drawn by using a suitable method so that it can be regarded representative of the entire population is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: a representative part of a population, drawn by a proper method, is called a sample. A population (or universe) is the whole group the researcher wants to draw conclusions about: all Class 12 students in Maharashtra, or all adults with schizophrenia in India. Studying everyone (a census) is usually too costly or impossible, so the researcher studies a sample: a subset chosen so that it mirrors the population. Henry E. Garrett (1926), in Statistics in Psychology and Education, and Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, both stress that a sample is useful only when it is representative, because the whole point is to generalise from the part to the whole. How representative it is depends on the sampling method. Probability methods give every member a known, non-zero chance of selection: simple random, systematic, stratified and cluster sampling. Non-probability methods (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball) are cheaper but risk bias. The famous failure is the 1936 Literary Digest poll, which predicted that Alf Landon would beat Franklin Roosevelt because its huge sample came from telephone directories and car registrations, lists that over-represented wealthier voters. Size alone does not rescue a biased sample. For example, Meera wants to know how stressed undergraduates in Mumbai feel. She cannot test all of them, so she randomly selects 400 from college rolls, stratified by stream. Those 400 students are her sample. Option d is right because \"a fraction of a population drawn by a suitable method so that it represents the whole\" is the textbook definition of a sample. Exam trap: \"estimate\" is close in spirit, because sample values are used to estimate population parameters, but an estimate is a number calculated from the sample (such as a sample mean), not the group of people itself.", "traps": {"a": "A variable is any characteristic that can take different values across people or conditions (age, anxiety score, gender). It is what gets measured in a sample, not the subset of people.", "b": "An estimate is a value calculated from sample data (for example the sample mean) and used to infer a population parameter. It is a statistic, not the selected portion of the population.", "c": "A group is any collection of people, such as an experimental or a control group. The word carries no requirement of being drawn by a sampling method to represent a population, so it is too general."}}, {"q": "A researcher was interested in stress levels of lecturers during lectures. She took the same group of 8 lecturers and measured their anxiety (out of 15) during a normal lecture and again in a lecture in which she had paid students to be disruptive and misbehave. The data were not normally distributed. Which test should she use to compare her experimental conditions?", "concept": "The answer is d: two conditions, the same participants and non-normal data call for the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Choosing a test needs three questions. How many conditions? Two here (a normal lecture and a disrupted lecture). Same or different people? The same 8 lecturers gave both scores, so this is a repeated-measures (related, within-subjects) design. Are parametric assumptions met? No: the data are not normally distributed, and with only 8 people and scores out of 15 normality cannot be trusted anyway. The parametric test for two related conditions is the paired (dependent) samples t-test. Its non-parametric equivalent is the signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). It works on the difference between each person's two scores. It drops zero differences, ranks the remaining differences by size while ignoring their sign, then gives each rank back its plus or minus sign. If the disruptive lecture truly raises anxiety, most of the large differences will be positive, so the sum of the negative ranks (T) will be small, and a small T means a significant result. Because it uses ranks instead of raw scores, it does not assume normality and is less affected by outliers. For example, Arjun scores 6 in the normal lecture and 13 in the disrupted one, so his difference of 7 gets a high positive rank. Option d is right because the signed-rank test is the non-parametric test for comparing two related (paired) conditions. Exam trap: Wilcoxon gave his name to two tests. The rank-sum test, equivalent to the U test of Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), compares two independent groups; the signed-rank test compares two related conditions. Students who see \"Wilcoxon\" and \"non-normal\" often pick the wrong one.", "traps": {"a": "The paired samples t-test does suit two related conditions, but it is parametric and assumes the difference scores are normally distributed. The stem says the data are not normal, so its non-parametric equivalent is needed.", "b": "The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) is the non-parametric test for two independent groups of different people. Here the same 8 lecturers were measured twice.", "c": "The Wilcoxon rank-sum test is Frank Wilcoxon's (1945) independent-groups test, equivalent to the Mann-Whitney test. It ignores the pairing of scores, so it does not fit a repeated-measures design."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A) Psychopathology is characterized by subjective discomfort. Reason (R) Psychopathology refers to mental disorder themselves.", "concept": "The answer is d: the Assertion is false as a general claim, while the Reason is true. Start with R. The word psychopathology has two meanings. It names the scientific study of mental, emotional and behavioural disorders, and it also refers to the mental disorders themselves, the abnormal or maladaptive patterns of thinking and behaving. Dennis Coon and John Mitterer (2010) define it in exactly these two ways, so R is true. Now A. Subjective discomfort means private feelings of pain, unhappiness or emotional distress. It is one of several criteria for judging abnormality, alongside statistical abnormality, social nonconformity and maladaptive behaviour. Many disorders do bring distress: depression, panic disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder. But psychopathology does not always cause personal discomfort. A person in a manic episode may feel wonderful. A person with antisocial personality disorder may feel no guilt after hurting others. Someone with a fixed delusion may be calm and certain. Sometimes the absence of discomfort is itself the sign: if Kabir shows no grief at all after his closest friend dies, a clinician might suspect a problem. So subjective discomfort is common but neither necessary nor sufficient, which is why David H. Barlow and V. Mark Durand (1995) say no single criterion defines disorder. Saying psychopathology is characterised by subjective discomfort turns one partial criterion into the defining feature, so A is false. Because A is false, R cannot explain it. Option d is right because R is a correct definition of the term while A overstates one criterion as a universal rule. Exam trap: distress appears in every abnormality chapter, so students treat it as defining and pick a or b. Key note: no key was published for this paper. If the setter meant A loosely (distress is a common feature of disorders), both statements would be true and b would be the pick; read as a general claim, A is false and d fits best.", "traps": {"a": "This needs A to be true and explained by R. A overstates subjective discomfort, which many disorders lack (mania, antisocial personality), and a definition of a term cannot explain why disorders feel distressing.", "b": "This fits only if A is read loosely as 'distress is common in disorders'. Read as a general claim, A is false, because psychopathology can occur without any personal discomfort.", "c": "This reverses the truth values. R is a standard textbook definition (the term refers to the disorders themselves as well as to their study), so R cannot be the false statement."}}, {"q": "The ________ plays important role in formation, consolidation and retention of memories, spatial navigation and regulation of stress.", "concept": "The answer is d: the hippocampus forms and consolidates memories, supports spatial navigation and helps regulate the stress response. It is a curved, seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. The evidence for each role is classic. Memory first. In 1953 the surgeon William Scoville removed most of both hippocampi from Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) to stop his seizures. Brenda Milner (Scoville and Milner, 1957) showed that he could no longer form new long-term declarative memories (anterograde amnesia), although his short-term memory, older memories and motor learning were largely intact. So the hippocampus consolidates new facts and events into long-term storage, which is later held in the cortex. Spatial navigation next. John O'Keefe (1971) found place cells in the rat hippocampus that fire when the animal is in one particular spot, together building a cognitive map; he shared the 2014 Nobel Prize for this. Eleanor Maguire (2000) found that London taxi drivers, who memorise thousands of streets, had larger posterior hippocampi. Stress last. The hippocampus is rich in glucocorticoid receptors. When cortisol rises, it signals the hypothalamus to switch off the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, a negative feedback brake. Chronic stress and high cortisol can shrink the hippocampus, which links stress to memory problems in depression and PTSD. For example, Meera, revising under heavy stress for weeks, may find new facts harder to retain partly because of this cortisol effect. Option d is right because only the hippocampus combines all three roles named in the stem. Exam trap: the amygdala sits right next to the hippocampus and is also limbic and stress-related, but it handles fear and the emotional colouring of memories, not general consolidation or spatial maps. The hypothalamus starts the stress response but does not form memories.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala, an almond-shaped limbic nucleus, processes fear and threat and adds emotional strength to memories, as in fear conditioning. It does not consolidate everyday declarative memories or map space.", "b": "The thalamus is the relay station that passes sensory signals (except most of smell) to the cortex. Some thalamic damage disturbs memory, as in Korsakoff syndrome, but it is not the structure tied to consolidation, navigation and stress feedback together.", "c": "The hypothalamus controls hunger, thirst, temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary, and it starts the HPA stress response by releasing CRH. It does not form memories or support spatial navigation."}}, {"q": "Cause and effect relationship can be established using ________.", "concept": "The answer is c: cause and effect can be established with experimental designs. In an experiment the researcher actively manipulates an independent variable (IV), measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV), and controls other variables, ideally by randomly assigning participants to conditions. These features meet the conditions for causation set out by the philosopher John Stuart Mill (1843): the cause must come before the effect, cause and effect must vary together, and other explanations must be ruled out. Random assignment is the key step. It spreads all other differences between people evenly across the groups, so the only systematic difference left is the IV. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) called this internal validity and showed that true experimental designs, such as the pretest-posttest control group design, rule out threats like history, maturation and selection. For example, Kabir wants to know whether a mindfulness programme reduces exam anxiety. He randomly assigns 60 students to the programme or to a waiting list, then compares their anxiety scores. If the programme group ends up calmer, he can say the programme caused the drop. Non-experimental designs cannot do this. Descriptive designs (case studies, observation) say what is happening. Survey designs measure attitudes and behaviours in large groups, usually at one time. Predictive or correlational designs show that variables move together and allow forecasts, but a third variable may explain the link, and the direction of influence may be unclear. Option c is right because only experiments combine manipulation, control and random assignment, which together allow a causal conclusion. Exam trap: \"predictive\" sounds causal, because if X predicts Y it seems X must cause Y. But correlation is not causation: ice cream sales predict drowning deaths only because both rise in hot weather.", "traps": {"a": "Predictive (correlational) designs test how well one variable forecasts another, as when entrance scores predict college grades. Without manipulation and random assignment, a third variable or reverse causation cannot be ruled out.", "b": "Descriptive designs (case studies, naturalistic observation, archival research) record what occurs and how often. They manipulate nothing, so they cannot show what causes what.", "d": "Survey designs collect self-reports from many people about attitudes or behaviour, usually at one point in time. They describe and correlate but do not control variables, so causal claims are not justified."}}, {"q": "Philip hates watching movies but watches because his wife wants to. After three years Philip comes to genuinely enjoy watching movies. This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Philip's story shows how compliance can lead to acceptance. David Myers (1983), in his Social Psychology textbook, separates kinds of going along with others. Compliance is outwardly acting in line with a request or social pressure while privately disagreeing: Philip sits through movies he dislikes because his wife wants him to. Obedience is acting in response to a direct order. Acceptance means both acting and believing in line with the pressure: after three years Philip genuinely enjoys movies. The interesting point is the direction of change. We usually assume attitudes drive behaviour, but repeated behaviour can also change attitudes. Leon Festinger (1957) explained this with cognitive dissonance: acting against an attitude creates discomfort, and over time we reduce it by coming to like what we are doing. Daryl Bem (1972) offered self-perception theory: Philip watches himself going to movies again and again and infers that he must enjoy them. Familiarity helps too, because Robert Zajonc (1968) showed that mere exposure makes things more liked. In this way public compliance turns into private acceptance. A similar example: Arjun joins his father's early morning yoga only to please him, but after a year he looks forward to it himself. Option b is right because Philip first complied (acted without liking) and the repeated behaviour later produced genuine liking, which is acceptance. Exam trap: option a reverses the order; acceptance is already inner agreement, so it cannot be the starting point that leads to mere compliance. Reactance is resistance to pressure, and it would make Philip dislike movies more, not less.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the sequence. Acceptance is already inner agreement, so it cannot be the starting point; Philip began by complying (watching without liking) and only later accepted.", "c": "The boomerang effect is persuasion that backfires, producing attitude change opposite to the one intended. Philip moved towards liking movies, the direction his wife wanted, so nothing backfired.", "d": "Psychological reactance (Jack Brehm, 1966) is the urge to restore freedom when we feel pushed, which makes people resist or do the opposite. It would make Philip dislike movies more, not lead him to acceptance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of the technique is used as a logical disputation by a cognitive behaviour therapist?", "concept": "The answer is b: operationalising a negative construct is an example of logical disputation in cognitive behaviour therapy. Albert Ellis (1962) made disputing of beliefs the active step of rational emotive behaviour therapy, and Raymond DiGiuseppe (1991) sorted disputes into types that CBT writers such as Windy Dryden and Michael Neenan (2004) still use. An empirical dispute asks \"Where is the evidence?\" and tests a belief against facts. A pragmatic (functional) dispute asks \"Does this belief help you?\". A logical dispute asks \"Does this conclusion follow?\" and examines the meaning and internal reasoning of the belief. Operationalising a negative construct is a logical method. The client uses a global label such as \"I am a failure\" or, in a voice hearer, \"I am evil\". The therapist asks for a precise definition: what exactly would a failure be, what would an evil person do with their time? Once the label is defined, the client can see that the conclusion does not follow: failing one exam does not make a whole person a failure. The error exposed is a reasoning error, such as overgeneralisation or labelling. For example, Arjun says \"I'm useless\" after a poor interview. His therapist asks him to list what a truly useless person would do every day; Arjun sees that he does almost none of it, and the label collapses on logical grounds. Option b is right because defining the negative construct tests whether the conclusion follows from its own meaning, which is the essence of logical disputation. Exam trap: weighing up the evidence for and against a thought is the classic empirical technique of Aaron Beck (1979), not a logical one. Key note: no key was published for this paper. A student could argue for c if logic and evidence are not kept apart, but standard CBT texts file evidence-weighing under empirical disputing, leaving b as the logical technique.", "traps": {"a": "'Calculating the evidence' is not a standard named CBT technique. It looks like a blend of weighing up the evidence and calculating the real probability of a feared event, both of which test a thought against facts, which is empirical, not logical, disputation.", "c": "Weighing up the evidence for and against an automatic thought is the core empirical technique of Aaron Beck's (1979) cognitive therapy. It tests the thought against facts, so it is an empirical rather than a logical dispute.", "d": "Guided discovery is the overall Socratic style of CBT, in which the therapist's questions lead the client to reach conclusions himself. It is the way all kinds of disputes are delivered, not a specific logical technique."}}, {"q": "Consider the following statements- I. In partial correlation relationship between two variables are calculated. II. In multiple correlation relationship between two or more than two independent variables are calculated. Which of the statements given below is/are correct?", "concept": "The answer is c: as the setter clearly intended, both statements describe the two techniques correctly in outline. Begin with simple correlation, which measures the relationship between two variables, such as hours studied and marks, using the coefficient r of Karl Pearson (1896). Partial correlation also looks at the relationship between just two variables, but it removes (partials out) the influence of one or more other variables on both. Written r12.3, a notation from George Udny Yule (1907), it is the correlation between variables 1 and 2 with variable 3 held constant. For example, shoe size and reading ability correlate in children only because both rise with age; partial out age and the correlation falls to near zero. So statement I is true: partial correlation calculates the relationship between two variables, after controlling for others. Multiple correlation (R) measures the relationship between one dependent (criterion) variable and two or more independent (predictor) variables taken together. Written R1.23, it is the correlation between actual criterion scores and the best weighted combination of the predictors, and R squared gives the share of criterion variance the predictors explain together, as in multiple regression. For example, Riya predicts first-year college marks (criterion) from Class 12 marks and an aptitude score (two predictors); the R between marks and that combination is a multiple correlation. Statement II is a compressed version of this: multiple correlation is built on two or more independent variables. Option c is right because statements I and II each capture the defining feature of their technique. Exam trap: students often swap the two terms. Partial correlation always ends with one pair of variables; multiple correlation always ends with one criterion against a combined set. Key note: no key was published for this paper. Read strictly, statement II omits the dependent variable, which would make a the answer; c is preferred because statement I is just as compressed and the wording of this paper is loose throughout.", "traps": {"a": "This is the answer only on a strict reading of statement II, which leaves out the dependent variable. Statement I is equally compressed (it omits the controlled variable), so the setter's intent is that both describe their techniques correctly.", "b": "Statement I is not false. Partial correlation does calculate the relationship between two variables, with the effects of other variables removed, so 'only 2' wrongly rejects it.", "d": "Both statements describe real features: partial correlation focuses on one pair of variables, and multiple correlation combines two or more independent variables. Rejecting both ignores the standard definitions."}}, {"q": "Shadow in Carl Jung personality theory is-", "concept": "The answer is a: in analytical psychology the shadow is the dark side of personality. Carl Gustav Jung (1917) divided the psyche into the ego (the conscious mind), the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious (inherited images shared by all humanity). The contents of the collective unconscious are archetypes, universal patterns that appear in myths, dreams and art across cultures. The shadow is one of the main archetypes. It holds the qualities we do not want to admit we have: aggression, selfishness, envy, sexual and animal impulses, and anything our upbringing taught us is unacceptable. Jung called it the archetype of darkness and repression. In stories it appears as the devil, the villain or the evil twin. We often project our shadow onto others, so a person who loudly condemns greed in others may be denying his own. The shadow is not purely bad; it also holds energy, spontaneity and creativity. Healthy growth, which Jung called individuation, requires recognising and integrating the shadow rather than denying it. In Aion (1951) he wrote that becoming aware of the dark aspects of the personality as present and real is the essential condition for any self-knowledge. For example, Kabir, who prides himself on being gentle, keeps dreaming of violent quarrels; a Jungian therapist would see his shadow pressing for recognition. Option a is right because Jung's own description of the shadow is the dark, rejected side of the personality. Exam trap: the persona is the protective mask we show the world, so \"protective personality mechanism\" describes the persona, not the shadow. Repressed childhood memories belong to the personal unconscious and to the ideas of Sigmund Freud (1915), not to this inherited archetype.", "traps": {"b": "The shadow is itself an archetype, but 'unconscious archetypes' names the whole class of inherited images in the collective unconscious (persona, anima, animus, wise old man, self). It does not say what the shadow specifically is.", "c": "A protective mask fits Jung's persona, the public face we adopt to meet social expectations, or Sigmund Freud's defence mechanisms. The shadow is the hidden, rejected side, not a shield.", "d": "Repressed childhood memories belong to Jung's personal unconscious (and its complexes) or to Freud's repressed unconscious. The shadow is an inherited archetype of the collective unconscious, not a store of one person's memories."}}, {"q": "Match the following terms- 1. Debriefing, 2. Dehoaxing, 3. Desensitization, 4. Active Deception. A. Mislead the participants by providing them within formation that is not true B. Disclosure of any deception and providing an opportunity for participants to ask questions about the research C. Telling research participants of any deception or uses used in a study D. Process of eliminating any negative after effects that a participant might experience", "concept": "The answer is b: the correct matching is 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A. Deception in research means misleading participants about the purpose or procedures of a study so that they behave naturally. The obedience study of Stanley Milgram (1963), where participants believed they were shocking a learner, is the famous case. Because deception overrides fully informed consent, codes such as the American Psychological Association's (2002) Ethics Code allow it only when the study is valuable, no non-deceptive method exists, and participants learn the truth afterwards. The four terms fit together. Active deception (4-A) is deliberately giving participants false information, such as a fake cover story or a confederate posing as another participant; passive deception simply withholds information. Debriefing (1-B) is the full disclosure after participation: explaining the true purpose, revealing any deception and giving participants the chance to ask questions. David S. Holmes (1976) divided debriefing into two parts. Dehoaxing (2-C) is telling participants about any deception or ruses used and convincing them the false information was false. Desensitising (3-D) is detecting and removing any negative after-effects, such as anxiety or a damaged self-image; a Milgram participant who now believes he is cruel needs to hear that most people obeyed. For example, Riya tells students they failed an intelligence test when they did not (active deception). Afterwards she explains the real aim and answers questions (debriefing), shows that the scores were fake (dehoaxing) and makes sure no one leaves feeling stupid (desensitising). Option b is right because B, C, D, A is the only sequence that pairs each term with its own definition. Exam trap: dehoaxing and debriefing are easily swapped. Debriefing is the whole post-study conversation, while dehoaxing is the specific step of revealing the deception. Desensitising here has nothing to do with the systematic desensitisation therapy of Joseph Wolpe (1958).", "traps": {"a": "This gives debriefing definition A (misleading with false information), which is active deception, and gives active deception definition B (full disclosure and questions), which is debriefing, so the first and last terms are swapped.", "c": "This gives dehoaxing definition A (false information), the opposite of revealing a deception, and gives active deception definition C, which is the meaning of dehoaxing. The second and fourth terms are swapped.", "d": "This uses D twice and never uses B. It gives debriefing the after-effects definition, which belongs to desensitising, so it cannot be a valid one-to-one matching."}}, {"q": "________ is divided into four sections rostrum, genu, body and splenium.", "concept": "The answer is b: the corpus callosum is divided into the rostrum, genu, body and splenium. The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain, a thick arched band of about 200 million myelinated axons that links the left and right cerebral hemispheres so they can share information. In a midline (sagittal) slice of the brain it looks like a curved band lying above the lateral ventricles. Anatomists name four parts from front to back. The rostrum is the thin beak that curves down and back under the front end. The genu (Latin for knee) is the sharp bend at the front, carrying fibres between the two prefrontal cortices. The body, or trunk, is the long middle arch, linking motor, sensory and parietal areas. The splenium is the thick, rounded rear end, carrying fibres between the occipital and posterior temporal lobes, so it is vital for sharing visual information. Some texts add a narrow isthmus between the body and the splenium. Clinical cases show why the parts matter. Roger Sperry (1968) and Michael Gazzaniga (1967) studied split-brain patients whose corpus callosum had been cut to control epilepsy; words flashed only to the right hemisphere could not be named aloud. Joseph Jules Dejerine (1892) described pure alexia, loss of reading with writing preserved, after damage to the left visual cortex plus the splenium, because words seen by the right hemisphere could no longer cross to the language areas. Option b is right because rostrum, genu, body and splenium are the standard named subdivisions of the corpus callosum and of no other structure in the list. Exam trap: the cingulum is a white-matter bundle running just above the corpus callosum inside the cingulate gyrus, so it is a tempting near-miss, but it is not divided into these four parts.", "traps": {"a": "Efferent is a direction, not a structure: efferent (motor) fibres carry signals away from the central nervous system to muscles and glands, the opposite of afferent fibres. It has no rostrum or splenium.", "c": "The basal ganglia are grey-matter nuclei deep in the forebrain (caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus, with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra) that help control movement and habits. They are divided into nuclei, not into rostrum, genu, body and splenium.", "d": "The cingulum is an association bundle of white matter inside the cingulate gyrus that links frontal, parietal and temporal regions. It lies just above the corpus callosum but is described as one long tract, not in these four parts."}}]});
